{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:55:27.257972+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de4fdd829860a83309fe373fa82abe65c5fb509cc35300f9ab2f8b7f1dbca853","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:3 of arXiv:2410.23250, 'Strict inequalities for arm exponents in planar percolation'] Fix $t\in [0,1/2]$.   Let $A,B,S,T\subset[n]$ pairwise disjoint. Let $F,G:\{0,1\}^n\times\{0,1\}^n\to \mathbb R$ such that
  \begin{itemize}
  \item $F(\omega,\omega_t)$ is  measurable w.r.t.\@ $\{(\omega(x),\omega_t(x))\:: \: x\in A\cup S\cup T\}$, 
  \item  $G(\omega,\omega_t)$ is measurable w.r.t.\@ $\{(\omega(x),\omega_t(x))\:: \: x\in B\cup S\cup T\}$,
  \item $F,G$ are both $S$-increasing and $T$-decreasing.
  \end{itemize}
  Then for every $t\in [0,1/2]$
  \begin{equation}
        \mathbb E[F(\omega,\omega_t)G(\omega,\omega_t)]\ge\mathbb E[F(\omega,\omega_t)]\mathbb E[G(\omega,\omega_t)]
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

