{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.478202+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df25b55ffafa1c2ca05017fb558de76a2d160178ff490772c5a16ea706b5e0f8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:spec-Borel of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] Let $\mu$ be a Borel measure on a topological space $X$. Then $\mu$ is a 
\emph{Radon measure}

\footnote{Radon measures are also known as \emph{tight} measures [topsoe70].}

[schwartz73] if $\mu$ is inner regular on compact sets, that is to say, the 
measure $\mu(A)$ of any $A\in\mathcal{B}(X)$ is the supremum of $\mu(B)$ over all compact 
subsets $B\subset A$. The measure $\mu$ is \emph{$\tau$-smooth}

\footnote{It is well known that every Radon measure is $\tau$-smooth
[bogachev07-vol2]. Also, for every $\tau$-smooth 
measure $\mu$ there exists a smallest closed set $A$ of full measure 
$\mu(A)=\mu(X)$, called the \emph{support} of $\mu$.}

if $\mu(\bigcup_{i\in I}A_i)=\sup_{i\in I}\mu(A_i)$ for every increasing net 
$(A_i)_{i\in I}$ of open sets [topsoe70,winkler85]. Let $R$ and $P\!_\tau$
be the sets of Radon and $\tau$-smooth probability measures on $X$, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

