{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.871702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2fbceee39ccdc4edcc30d353f0a85875e3e7b2cab43acd3a27aa262b228d6b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:fully_linear_val_sec2 of arXiv:2608.24963, 'Why and When Neural Networks Improve Local Approximation in Optimization'] A model $m_k$ is said to be \emph{fully linear} on $\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$ if there exist constants $\kappa_f,\kappa_g>0$, independent of $k$, such that for all $x\in\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$,
