{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.331716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dfc2dafc083a047bb48b6e9be88e6ed7d5ed80b0bdb8e474fc80bb39487c66db","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma cf.~\cite{shah2021parametrized}*{Theorem~6.7} of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] A functor $f\colon{\mathcal{I}}\to{\mathcal J}$ is final if and only if $f(A)\colon{\mathcal{I}}(A)\to{\mathcal J}(A)$ is final in the non-parametrized sense for every $A\in T$. Dually, $f$ is initial if and only if each $f(A)$ is initial.
    \begin{proof}
        It suffices to prove the second claim. By the parametrized version of Quillen's Theorem~A from [martini2021yoneda]*{Corollary~4.4.8}, $f$ is initial if and only if for every $T$-$\infty$-groupoid $X\in\PSh(T)$ and each functor $X\to{\mathcal J}$, the induced map
        $\Ar({\mathcal{I}})\times_{{\mathcal J}}{\mathpalette\ulhelper{X}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{X}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}$ (where the pullback is via the target map) becomes an equivalence after applying the groupoidification functor $\Cat_T\to\Spc_T$ left adjoint to the inclusion. As the latter is just given by applying groupoidification pointwise, we see that $f$ is initial if and only if the projection \[\Ar({\mathcal{I}}(A))\times_{{\mathcal J}(A)}{\textup{Hom}}(A,X)\to{\textup{Hom}}(A,X)\] becomes an equivalence after groupoidification for every $A\in T$ and ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{X}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}\to{\mathcal J}$. Applying the statement of the lemma for $T=1$ (and the $\infty$-groupoid ${\textup{Hom}}(A,X)$) then shows that this holds provided that $f(A)$ is initial for every $A\in T$.

        Conversely, assume that $f$ is initial. If $T$ has a terminal object $1$, then running the above argument backwards for $A=1$ shows that the $\infty$-category $\Ar({\mathcal{I}}(1))\times_{{\mathcal J}(1)} X$ is weakly contractible for every $\infty$-groupoid $X$ and every $X\to{\mathcal J}(1)$, i.e.\ $f(1)$ is initial. The claim now follows since for any $A\in T$ the $T_{/A}$-functor obtained from $f$ by restriction along $T_{/A}\to T$ is initial by [martini2021yoneda]*{Remark~4.4.9}.
    \end{proof}","status":"unverified"}

