{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.332563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0967e66764ceebce0b2fb8dc3bf74f3ea06b851b520b1cca3a11843c4b0cc63","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2607.14718, 'Anchored Nash inequalities and heat kernel bounds for a class of random conductance models'] Suppose that Assumption~\ref{a2-2} holds. Then, for every $d' > d$ there exists a constant $C_{12}>0$ such that for every $R \geq R_1$ (here $R_{1}$ is the same constant in Assumption~\ref{a2-2} associated with $d'$) and $f, g\colon B(R) \to \R$ with $(g)_{B(R)} = 1$,

  \begin{align}
    &\norm{f - (fg)_{B(R)}}{\rho_{*}}{B(R)}
    \nonumber\\%[.5ex]
    &\mspace{36mu}\leq\;
    C_{12}\, R^{1-d/d'}\,
    \Bigl(
      1 + \Norm{g}{\rho_{*}/(\rho_{*}-1), B(R)}
    \Bigr)\,
    \Biggl(
      \sum_{\substack{y, z \in B(\delta_{\mathrm{S}} R) \\ y \sim z}}
      \mspace{-12mu}\bigl|f(y) - f(z)\bigr|^{\rho}
    \Biggr)^{\!1/\rho}.
  \end{align}







  Here $\rho, \rho_{*} \in (1, \infty)$ and $\delta_{\mathrm{S}} \in [1, \infty)$ are the same constants as in Assumption~\ref{a2-2}-(ii) satisfying \eqref{a2-2-3} and \eqref{a2-2-2}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.817337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0968d5cd7900b378ca7a0cf8fe07ae44a80b9409d1e08fa6bda350c26bcefe6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem LSL^2 of arXiv:2605.10309, 'Exponential Decay of $L^2$-Solutions to Stochastic Nonlinear Schr\"odinger Equations Drive'] Assume (H1) and (H2), and let $1<\alpha <1+\frac{4}{d}$.
Then for any $x\in L^2$ and $0<T<\infty$, there exists a sequence of $L^2$-solutions $(y_n,\tau_n), n\in\mathbb{N}$ of (equation RSNLS2 of the paper) where $(\tau_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of increasing stopping times.
For every $n\ge 1$, it holds ${
{\mathbf{P}}\text{-a.s.}}$ that
\begin{align}
y_n|_{[0,\tau_n]}\in C([0,\tau_n];L^2)\cap L^{q}(0,\tau_n;L^{\alpha+1}),
\end{align}
and uniqueness holds in $C([0,\tau_n];L^2)\cap L^{q}(0,\tau_n;L^{\alpha+1})$. Here, $(q,\alpha+1)$ is a Strichartz pair, where $q=\frac{4(\alpha+1)}{d(\alpha-1)}\in (2+\frac{4}{d},\infty).$
Moreover, defining $\displaystyle \tau^*(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\tau_n$ and $\displaystyle y=\lim_{n\to\infty}y_n\mathbf{1}_{[0,\tau^*(x))}$, we have the blowup alternative, that is, for ${
{\mathbf{P}}\text{-a.s.}} \ \omega$, if $\tau^*(x)(\omega)<T$, then
\[ \lim_{t\to \tau^*(x)(\omega)}\|y(t)(\omega)\|_{L^2}=\infty. \]","status":"unverified"}

