{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:46.422546+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0e3f6b9b7051e0cf74db4847778a049ff7e67d3db610b03820b8e27122e1bdd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 82 of arXiv:2608.20252, '2608.20252'] Suppose $Q$ is a probability measure governing $W_t$, concentrating on $C([0,T], \mf D')$, and for each $H\in \mf D$,
$$M_t^{U,H} \ = \ W_t(H) - W_0(H) - \int_0^t W_s(UH)ds$$
and
$$N^{U,H}_t \ = \ (M_t^{U,H})^2 - \|BH\|^2_{L^2} t$$
are $L^1(Q)$, $\F_t$-martingales.  Then, for all $0\leq s<t$, and subsets $A\subset \R^d$, $Q$ a.s.,
\begin{eqnarray}

&&Q\Big[W_t(H)\in A| \F_s\big] \\ 
&& \ \ = \ \int_A \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \int_0^{t-s}\|BT_rH\|^2_{L^2} dr}}\exp\Big\{\frac{-|y- W_s(T_{t-s}H)|^2}{2\int_0^{t-s}\|BT_rH\|^2_{L^2} dr}\Big\}dy.\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
Therefore, the finite-dimensional distributions of $Q$, and hence the measure $Q$ on $C([0,T], \mf D)$, are determined by its restriction to $\F_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.543051+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0e4ac209db56debee51e70aa155eb7fb11ad9a06596a3e1e5e92ac1d99ba781","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 of arXiv:2608.25971, 'Positive \texorpdfstring{$\tau$}{tau}-bi-Ricci curvature and Mean curvature flow with surg'] Under the same assumptions on $M_0$, there exists
