{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:44.251049+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0f060463b26508a904b93e00b53b2606174547ac0d2382f7c3d1632a2c5b918","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2604.15226, '\bfseries Nonlinear Schrödinger equations with spatial white noise potential on full space'] Let $T>0$ and $\mu\in(0,1)$. Let $(p_1,q_2)$ and $(p_2,q_2)$ two Strichartz pairs, that is
\begin{equation}
\frac{2}{p_i}+\frac{d}{q_i}=\frac{d}{2}
\end{equation}
for $i\in\{1,2\}$ with $(p_i,q_i)\neq(2,\infty)$ in two dimensions. Let $w$ be a solution to
\begin{equation}
i\partial_tw=-\Delta w+F
\end{equation}
with initial data $w_0$. Then the solution satisfies the bound
\begin{equation}
\|w\|_{L^p([0,T],L^q(\IR^d))}\lesssim\|w_0\|_{L^2(\IR^d)}+\|F\|_{L^{p_2'}([0,T],L^{q_2'}(\IR^d))}
\end{equation}
where $p_2'$ and $q_2'$ denotes the conjugated exponent of $p_2$ and $q_2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.238665+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0f1ce4c4c9b9a327fe561d1d755c0eb8efaaf6adabea518981d3fd8489baa81","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 44 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Let $\delta>0$. For all $M \in \N_0$, $i \in \lbr \delta^{-1}\rbr$, and $z>0$, we define the events 
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{B}_{\delta}^{(1)} &:= \bigcap_{i\in \lbr \delta^{-1} \rbr}\bigg\{ \sup_{ |x-y|> \frac{1}{8}, \, (i-1)\delta\leq s < t \leq i\delta} \Lp(x,s;y,t)  \leq - \frac{1}{300}\delta^{-1} \bigg\} , \\
    \mathcal{B}_{\delta}^{(2)} &:= \bigcap_{i\in \lbr \delta^{-1} \rbr}\bigg\{  \inf_{ (x,s;y,t) \in  \mathcal{D}_{\delta} \colon \, (i-1)\delta\leq s < t \leq i\delta} \ \Lp(x,s;y,t)  \geq - \delta^{-1/2} \bigg\} . 
\end{align*}
There exist constants $c,C>0$ 
such that for all $\delta>0$
\begin{equation}
    \Pper\Big( \mathcal{B}_{\delta}^{(1)}  \cap  \mathcal{B}_{\delta}^{(2)} \Big) \geq 1- C\delta^{-1}\exp\big(-c \delta^{-1/2}\big) . 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.747153+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0f524a6572153195e573f3ddce2dc09119451b8c93206ab1b483656f6b901e4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 (Microscopic fluctuations) of arXiv:2607.28865, 'First-Passage Percolation on Spread-out line graphs: Microscopic Regime'] Assume that $\ell\ge2$ is fixed and $\go$ is not deterministic.
\begin{enumeratei}
	\item If $\E\go<\infty$ or $\go$ satisfies condition~A.\ref{ass:A2} with $\gc>1/{\lc}$, then the weak law of large numbers holds, \ie\
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{n}T^{(\ell)}(0,n)
    \stackrel{\pr}{\to}
    \mu_{\mathrm{FPP}} \text{ as } n\to\infty
\end{align*}
for some constant $\mu_{\mathrm{FPP}}\in (0,\infty).$

\item If $\E\go^2<\infty$ or $\go$ satisfies condition~A.\ref{ass:A2} with $\gc>2/{\lc}$, then the following Gaussian central limit theorem holds
\begin{align*}
    n^{-1/2}\cdot (T^{(\ell)}(0,n)-n\cdot \mu_{\mathrm{FPP}})
    \Longrightarrow
    \N(0,\gs_{\mathrm{FPP}}^2),
\end{align*}
where $\mu_{\mathrm{FPP}}$ is as in~\eqref{thm:mean}, and
$\gs_{\mathrm{FPP}}^2$ is a positive constant given in~\eqref{eq:var-CLT-def}.

\item Finally, if condition~A.\ref{ass:A2} holds with $0<\gc<2/{\lc}$, then
\begin{align*}
    \frac1{a_n}\cdot (T^{(\ell)}(0,n)-b_n)
    \Longrightarrow
    Z_\alpha,
\end{align*}
where $\alpha=\gc\lc$ and $Z_\alpha$ is the totally right-skewed $\alpha$-stable law associated with the normalization $a_n=n^{1/(\gc \lc) +o(1)},b_n$ explicitly given in~\eqref{eq:stable-law-const}. 
\end{enumeratei} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.548613+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0f73658db94fd21ea431697ccbad2d2ea0d255e78a011b2a1139aefeea170fd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 (Prediction Range \citep{hamman2024quantifying,watson2023predictive}) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] The prediction range of $\emprashomonset$ is the range of predicted probabilities of models for a given test instance $x$, and is defined as,
\begin{align}
    \predrange(x) =  \max_{f_k \in  \emprashomonset}f_{k}(x) -  \min_{f_k \in  \emprashomonset}f_{k}(x).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.365714+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0f884b31376e7db75256d6897169b02a0f34608d1755c9534ca427af997712e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:sep3 of arXiv:2606.02472, 'Correlated uniform attachment trees'] For all $\varepsilon>0$ there exist $m,k,K \in \N$ such that for all $n \geq m$, whenever $\left( T_n^1, T_n^2 \right)$ is distributed according to $\alpha{\text -}\mathrm{CUA}$, then
