 We denote the variance associated with this measure by $\mathrm{Var}_{L , n ,\beta_1, \beta_2 , \lambda, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}} - \mathrm{XY}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:35:04.164786+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e12ed71b11faee49a5780250db861e1d1262d39dbe93bb3c2cf85ddf40560f3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 40 of arXiv:2608.30059, 'Multihomogeneous Measures and Stochastic Polar Representations'] The function \(K_\nu\) is a finite positive semidefinite kernel on
\(G\times\mathsf T^q\) and
\begin{align}
K_\nu\bigl((ar,\boldsymbol h),(as,\boldsymbol k)\bigr)
&=
\chi_\alpha(a)^{-1}
K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol k)\bigr),
\\
K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol h)\bigr)
&=
p_{\boldsymbol h}\chi_\alpha(r\vee s)^{-1},

\end{align}
where \((r\vee s)_i=\max(r_i,s_i)\).  If \(\nu\) is
\(B\)-invariant, then
\begin{equation}

K_\nu\bigl((r,u\boldsymbol h),(s,u\boldsymbol k)\bigr)
=
K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol k)\bigr),
\qquad u\in\mathsf T.
\end{equation}

If \(Z\) is any stochastic representer of \(\nu\), then
\begin{equation}

K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol k)\bigr)
=
\E*{
\prod_{i=1}^q
\left[
\left(\frac{\gamma_{h_i,i}(Z)}{r_i}\right)^{\alpha_i}
\wedge
\left(\frac{\gamma_{k_i,i}(Z)}{s_i}\right)^{\alpha_i}
\right] }.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.739350+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e13555d9ababa2d86f8b1ace1a39059742924add6903da1769d3588a21112686","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 44 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] We have that
