{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.013785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1aa0cba3d04ad607ac20ab6d42aeebaabd3f2ce413d4f5b192fdff6f308578f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2605.04684, 'Ergodicity of stochastic functional differential equation with jumps and finite delay'] A stochastically continuous Markovian semigroup $P_t$ is called eventually Feller (see \cite{REISS2006SPA}) if there exists a $t_0 \geq 0$ such that for any $h \in C_b(\mathscr{D})$ the following two conditions are satisfied:
\begin{equation}
    P_t h \in C_b(\mathscr{D} ), \quad \text{for every } t \geq t_0, 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{s \downarrow t} P_s h(\xi) = P_t h(\xi), \quad \text{for every } \xi \in \mathscr{D} \text{ and } t \geq t_0.
\end{equation}
In particular, when $t_0 = 0$, $P_t$ is Feller.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.788620+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1abe1275a54193017ce8025cb6fc2d89fa570f92aaf2bce5bc65b10a6c10037","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:similar of arXiv:2411.08295, 'Improving the convergence of Markov chains via permutations and projections'] Let $P \in \mathcal{L}(\pi)$ and $Q\in \mathcal{I}(\pi) \cap \mathcal{L}$ be an isometric involution transition matrix. We have 
