 as $r\to \infty$ for each polynomial $P:\mathbb R^d\to \mathbb R$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.142016+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1e35ae5f555353606145f711092f7f7b05c347298633ba4455afd0105c58cbf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:7 of arXiv:2510.13572, 'Coalescence in Markov chains'] {ncftp}}]
Let $S$ be a non-empty, finite set, let $P\in\mathcal{P}_S$, and let $\mathcal S = \{S_r: r\in I\}$  be  a partition of $S$
with index set
$I=\{1,2,\dots,\ell\}$. 
For $i\in S_r$, let
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{r,s}^{(i)}= \sum_{j \in S_s} p_{i,j}.
\end{equation}
If there exists an $\mathcal S$-block measure $\mu\in \mathcal L_P$, then
\begin{letlist}
\item
for $r,s\in I$, 
\begin{equation}
\text{$\lambda_{r,s} := \lambda_{r,s}^{(i)}$ is constant for $i\in S_r$},
\end{equation}

\item
the \lq block-transition matrix'  $\Lambda=(\lambda_{r,s}: 1\le r,s\le \ell)$ is irreducible. Moreover, 
\begin{equation}
\text{$\Lambda$ is doubly stochastic,}
\end{equation}
which may be expressed as
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i\in S}\sum_{j \in S_s} \frac1{|S_{r(i)}|}p_{i,j}=1, \qquad r,s\in I,
\end{equation} 
where $r(i)$ is the index $r\in I$ such that $i \in S_r$.
\end{letlist} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.273992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1e814f6949b97cc41b2062d6b28b7540425270aed0b9fa5f047389b8c34a182","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (Bures--Wasserstein in terms of Cholesky factors) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] Let $A, B \in \mathscr{S}_+(n)$. Write $A = LL^{\intercal}$ and $B = MM^{\intercal}$, where $L, M \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$.\footnote{Given $A$ and $B$, the factors $L, M \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ always exist but are generally not unique. The stated results hold for each choice of $L$ and $M$.}
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] We have
\begin{equation} 
\begin{split}
\dist_{\BW}^2(A, B) &=  \|L\|_{\mathrm{F}}^2 + \|M\|_{\mathrm{F}}^2 - 2 \| L^{\intercal} M \|_*.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\item[(ii)] Let $L^{\intercal}M$ have singular value decomposition $U \Sigma V^{\intercal}$. Consider a coupling $\tilde{\pi}$ of $(\cN_n(0, A), \cN_n(0, B))$ of the form $\tilde{\pi} = \cL(X, Y)$, where $(X, Y) = (L\epsilon^X, M \epsilon^Y)$ and 
\begin{equation*} 
\pi := \cL(\epsilon^X, \epsilon^Y) \in \Cpl(\cN_n(0, I), \cN_n(0, I)).
\end{equation*}
Then $\tilde{\pi}$ is a Brenier coupling between $\cN_n(0, A)$ and $\cN_n(0, B)$ if and only if
\begin{equation} 
P := \bE_{\pi}[\epsilon^Y (\epsilon^X)^{\intercal}] \in \mathscr{P}(L^{\intercal}M).
\end{equation}
In particular, we may let $\pi$ be the Gaussian coupling
\[
\begin{bmatrix} \epsilon^X \\ \epsilon^Y \end{bmatrix} \sim \cN_{2n} \left( \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} I & P \\ P^{\intercal} & I \end{bmatrix} \right), \quad P \in \mathscr{P}( L^{\intercal}M).
\]
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.413635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1e95fdc0960a50e61e3576b2ff856c870584df75601f813f99fd88b32547f4f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:waterfall_region of arXiv:2507.22011, 'Random Lozenge Waterfall:\\Dimensional Collapse of Gibbs Measures'] Fix the scaled dimensions
	$\mathsf{T},\mathsf{S},\mathsf{N}$
	of the hexagon, and let $(\mathsf{t},\mathsf{x})$ be the scaled
	coordinates. Denote by $\mathcal{H}$ the scaled hexagon, that is,
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{H}\coloneqq \left\{
		(\mathsf{t},\mathsf{x})\colon 0< \mathsf{t}<\mathsf{T},\
		\max(0,\mathsf{t}-\mathsf{T}+\mathsf{S})< \mathsf{x}<
		\min(\mathsf{S}+\mathsf{N},\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{N})\right\}.
	\end{equation}
	Let
	\begin{equation}

		\mathsf{t}_l\coloneqq
		|\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{S}|
		,
		\qquad
		\mathsf{t}_r\coloneqq
		\min(\mathsf{N}+\mathsf{S},2\mathsf{T}-\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{S}).
	\end{equation}
	Denote the \emph{waterfall region} by
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{W}\coloneqq \left\{ (\mathsf{t},\mathsf{x})\in \mathcal{H}\colon
			\mathsf{t}_l< \mathsf{t}< \mathsf{t}_r,
			\
			|2\mathsf{x}-\mathsf{S}-\mathsf{t}|< \mathsf{N}
		\right\}.
	\end{equation}
	Also, let the \emph{center line} be the following line segment of slope $\frac{1}{2}$:
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{C}\coloneqq
		\left\{ (\mathsf{t},\mathsf{x})\in \mathcal{H}\colon
		\mathsf{t}_l< \mathsf{t}< \mathsf{t}_r,\
		\mathsf{x}=\tfrac12(\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{S})
		\right\}
		\subset \mathcal{W}.
	\end{equation}
	See (fig:waterfall_region of the paper) for illustrations of the waterfall
	region and the corresponding exact samples
	from the $q$-Racah measure generated by the
	shuffling algorithm of [borodin-gr2009q].","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:29:14.964361+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1eca934d64eacc7d3934b4ad446fd6b55d54bdcf91d895cbb52d7471d9a1baf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Random Walk of arXiv:2606.14895, 'Effective Resistances and Commute Times in Sparse Random Geometric Graphs'] A random sequence $X_1, ..., X_k$ of vertices in $G$ is a random walk if independently for all $i \in [k-1]$:
\begin{equation*}
    \Pr\left(X_{i+1} = v \mid X_i = u\right) = 
    \begin{cases} 
        \frac{w_{(u,v)}}{\deg(u)} & \text{if } (u,v) \in E\,, \\
        0 & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, let $\mathbf{W} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{D}^{-1}$ so that 
$\mathbf{W}_{vu} = \Pr\left(X_{i+1} = v \mid X_i = u\right)$. Then
$\mathbf{W}\vec{\mathbf{1}}_u$ is the distribution of vertex locations after one step of a random walk starting from $u$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.009458+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1f02c51012333fd65e962f611a978c13a8387a5f70e1b8c73764dd19eaecd77","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma M and M of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] [cai2025mesoscopicratesconvergencehermitian]
	Consider two DPPs $\mathfrak{X}$ and $\widetilde{\mathfrak{X}}$ with Hermitian kernels $K(x,y)$ and $\widetilde{K}(x,y)$ and associated integral operators $\mathbf{K}$ and $\widetilde{\mathbf{K}}$ respectively. Assume the integral operators are trace class. Then,
	\begin{equation*}
