{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.128518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1f12f953eaf5d048e33b0527115e90d69f087d6748f2b33e2195681b7b5ece0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2q:moment of arXiv:2510.09818, 'On the Fourier dimension of fractional Brownian graphs'] [LL25].
Let $B$ be a fractional Brownian motion with Hurst parameter $H \in (0,1)$, and recall that the Fourier transform of $\mu_{G}$ is given by~(equation b1 of the paper). Then for any $q\in \mathbb N_+$ and $\xi_1, \xi_2 \in \mathbb R \setminus\{0\}$, we have
\begin{align}
	\mathbb E\left[ |\hat{\mu}_G(\xi_1,\xi_2)|^{2q} \right]
	= \frac{(q!)^2}{|\xi_2|^{2q/H}} \sum_{\varepsilon\in \mathcal{A}_{2q}} \mathcal{I}_{T}^{\lambda}[\varepsilon, G_\varepsilon],
\end{align}
where the parameters $\lambda, T$ and the function $G_{\varepsilon}$ are chosen as
\begin{align}
	\lambda = \frac{\xi_1}{|\xi_2|^{1/H}}, \qquad
	T = |\xi_2|^{1/H} ,\quad \text{ and }\quad
	G_\varepsilon(u) = e^{-\pi\mathrm{Var}(\sum_{i=1}^{2q} \varepsilon_i B_{u_i})}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.062107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1fa99193dd1ca2a0d2b9ed3382750348de14702d78c732ba856284de59c07e4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem equalfnbootstrap of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $\Omega$ be an open subset of ${\mathbb R}^2$. 
Let $\{Z_n(x,y)\}_{(x,y)\in \Omega}$, $n\in {\mathbb N}$, and $\{Z(x,y)\}_{(x,y)\in \Omega}$ be random fields.  
Let $g: \Omega^2\to {\mathbb R}$ be continuously differentiable functions such that 
$(x,y)\mapsto (x,g(x,y))$ is invertible with continuously differentiable inverse function for $(x,y)\in \Omega$, and the following two conditions hold:
Suppose that the following two conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item For every $(x_1,y_1), (x_2,y_2) \in \Omega$ satisfying $g(x_1, y_1) \ne g(x_{2},y_{2})$,  as $n\to \infty$, 
\begin{equation} 
		(Z_n(x_1, y_1), Z_n(x_2, y_2))  \xrightarrow{d}  (Z(x_1, y_2), Z(x_2, y_2)) 
\end{equation}
\item For every $x_1, x_2 \in \pi_x(\Omega)$ and $r_1, r_2 \in {\mathbb R}$, 
\begin{equation*}
    		(y_1, y_2) \mapsto \mathbb{P} (Z(x_1,y_1)\le r_1, \, Z(x_2, y_2)\le r_2)
\end{equation*}
is a jointly continuous function of $(y_1, y_2)\in \prod_{i=1}^2 \pi_y(\Omega \cap (\{x_i\} \times {\mathbb R}))$. 
\end{enumerate}
Then, (equation eq:convergenced2 of the paper) holds for all $(x_1,y_1), (x_2, y_2)\in \Omega$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.499385+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2091d5e772d9b4c0ce8ea828169c83896819e2828419d6192c84c12ee46c7d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] We have $\bbr^m \otimes \bbr^n \cong \bbr^{m \times n}$, and the tensor product mapping is given by the dyadic product
\begin{align}
\otimes : \bbr^m \times \bbr^n \to \bbr^{m \times n}, \quad v \otimes w := v \cdot w^{\top}.
