\]
where \(G^{(\ell)}\) is a \(n_{\ell}\times n_{\ell-1}\) random matrix with independent, mean-zero Gaussian coordinates, with second moment equal to \(C_W/n_{\ell-1}\). These are also independent from \((W^{(\ell)})_{\ell\in\{1,\ldots L+1\}}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.884944+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29e41bd42737a8316ee84c8651e84a99ac80bfe1d9e4dd21c86826acb5791cf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2506.11918, 'Central limit theorems for the Euler characteristic in the Random Connection Model for hig'] Let $(W_n)_{n\in\N}$ be a sequence in $\K^d$ with $r(W_n)\rightarrow\infty$ for $n\rightarrow\infty$ and $f:\left(\R^d\right)^m\rightarrow [0,\infty)$, $m\in\N$, be a measurable and translation-invariant function such that
\begin{align*}
\nu_f \,:=\, \int_{\left(\R^d\right)^{m-1}} f(\mathbf{0},x_2,\dots,x_{m}) \; \d {(x_2,\dots,x_m)} \,<\, \infty.
\end{align*}
For $m=1$, we read this term as $f(\0)$.
Then it is true that
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{|W_n|} \int_{W_n^m} f(x_1,\dots,x_{m}) \; \d {(x_1,\dots,x_m)} \;\xrightarrow{\;\;}\; \nu_f \qquad \text{for } n\rightarrow\infty.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.591060+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29ed6cf06b1520c259856ab6bf9e77da9bc3a7c1076374b7f435acdda22b023","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm_besrec of arXiv:2411.09976, 'Occupation times on the legs of a diffusion spider'] For any $r\in\{1,\dotsc,R\}$ and $n_1,\dotsc,n_r \geq1$, 
\begin{equation} 
\mathbf{E}_\mathbf{0}\left(\prod_{i=1}^r (A_1^{(i)})^{n_i}\right) = \sum_{1\leq k_1 \leq l_1 \leq n_1} \!\!\dotsm\!\! \sum_{1\leq k_r \leq l_r \leq n_r} (-1)^{K -1} \frac{\Gamma(K)}{\Gamma(N)} \nu^{L-1} \prod_{j=1}^r \stirlingone{n_j}{l_j} \stirlingtwo{l_j}{k_j} \beta_j^{k_j}, 
\end{equation}
where $N=n_1+\dots+n_r$, $K=k_1+\dots+k_r$ and $L=l_1+\dots+l_r$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.951986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29f04077f3d9403c5a672a1acd5fa0ee5d571998ede5cdea359f7cbc83445a5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Qeq of arXiv:2608.16915, 'Exact mean--covariance dynamics of the Weber field in the stochastic Lagrangian representa'] The tensor $Q$ solves
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal D_uQ+G^\top Q+QG=2\nu\sum_{k=1}^3(\partial_kM)^\top(\partial_kM),\qquad Q(0)=0,
\end{equation}
and, with $S:=\tfrac12(G+G^\top)$ the symmetric strain,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal D_u\tr Q+2\tr(SQ)=2\nu\left\lvert \nabla M\right\rvert_{\mathrm F}^2.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.136858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29fa5fa8389c0d03ee3dbfa61c24829039946c671f68b39ca50e6cef58e6375","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:introexamples of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] For any $\alpha\in(0,\infty)$:
    \begin{enumerate}    
        \item[{\rm (i)}] The interval $[0,1]$ with its Euclidean metric and absolutely continuous measure with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate 
        \begin{equation} 
        \mbox{$\left(O\left(n^{-\alpha} \right),O\!\left(\sqrt{\frac{\ln n}{n}}\right)\right)$}. 
        \end{equation}
        \item[{\rm (ii)}] The circle in $\mathbb{R}^2$ with normalised geodesic metric and absolutely continuous measure with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate~(equation big-oh of the paper).
        \item[{\rm (iii)}] Any finite connected simple graph $(V,E)$ embedded in $\mathbb{R}^2$ with appropriate graph metric and absolutely continuous measure with with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate~(equation big-oh of the paper).
        \item[{\rm (iv)}] For any natural number $D\geq 2$, the unit cube $[0,1]^D$ with its $\ell^{\infty}$ metric and any absolutely continuous measure with with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is absolutely disintegrable with rate $\left(O(n^{-\alpha}),O\!\left((\frac{\ln n}{n})^{1 / (2D)}\right)\right)$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:06.324496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2a15f926cf1eb3b9e629ad211c03fc39e666d2fbb35a93f959808fbc0ec1d2d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 13 (Local contraction) of arXiv:2607.05219, 'Random free semigroups of affine groups'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\ASim(V)$ satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(i)] \textit{(Moment condition)}
$\displaystyle\int\!\bigl(\log^+c(g)+\log^+\|b(g)\|\bigr)\,d\mu(g)<\infty$.
\item[\rm(ii)] \textit{(Affine non-degeneracy)}
There is no $v\in V$   fixed by all $g\in\supp\mu$.
\item[\rm(iii)] \textit{(Centered ratio)}
$\displaystyle\int\!\log c(g)\,d\mu(g)=0$.
\item[\rm(iv)] \textit{(Non-trivial ratio)}
$\mu\{g; c(g)=1\}<1$. 
\end{enumerate}
Let $L_n:=X_n\cdots X_1$ be the associated left random walk. Then for
every compact $U\subset V$ and every $x,y\in V$,
\[
\|L_nx-L_ny\|\,\mathbf{1}_{\{L_nx\in U\}}\longrightarrow 0
\qquad\text{almost surely}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.606318+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2a86ad082c4532fdb4d5650b09dfd0e39acb3e0a9b4706e04cc6b85ede1652f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:semi of arXiv:2412.05142, 'Strong convergence of the Euler scheme for singular kinetic SDEs driven by $\alpha$-stable'] For $\epsilon>0, \beta\in(0,\alpha-1),$ some constant $\gamma$ as below, $\epsilon<\mu\leqslant 1+\epsilon$ and $\mu\geqslant \frac{\beta-\gamma}{\alpha}$, there exists a constant $N=N(d,\alpha,\beta,\mu,\gamma,\epsilon)$ so that the following holds: for any $f\in {\mathbb C}_{\boldsymbol{a}}^{(1+\alpha)\beta}$ and $(s,t)\in(0,1]_\leqslant^2$,
\begin{align}

    \|\nabla^\gamma P_tf\|_{\infty}\leqslant& N \|f\|_{{\mathbb C}_{\boldsymbol{a}}^{\beta}}t^{\frac{(\beta-\gamma)\wedge0}{\alpha}}, \quad t\in(0,1], \gamma\geqslant0;\\

    \|\nabla^\gamma (P_tf-P_s\Gamma_{t-s}f)\|_{\infty}\leqslant& N (\|f\|_{{\mathbb C}_{\boldsymbol{a}}^{\beta}}+t^{\beta}\|f\|_{{\mathbb C}_{\boldsymbol{a}}^{(1+\alpha)\beta}} )s^{\frac{(\beta-\gamma)}{\alpha}-\mu}(t-s)^{\mu-\epsilon}, \gamma=0,1.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.776133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2a9f190e6b0077e2681c5181694daf0854a532cfc69a7166c81bbfe9f09435e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:APZ of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] A line ensemble $\mathbf{X}$ is asymptotically pinned to zero if for any $ \varepsilon, T > 0$ there is some
