{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.864955+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2f9d990a1725abc9389bbffb7bfe6020a7ffa2b35ca121abe22090139bf3cad","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 of arXiv:2609.02426, 'Deformations of the $\osp(n|2)$-action on the superspace\\ of symbols of differential oper'] Let $n\geq3$ and $2d=m\in\N$, and consider the general infinitesimal deformation \eqref{eq:28},
$$L_1=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{m}\tau_kf_{\frac k2}+\sum_{k=1}^m\bigl(t_kh_k+\widetilde{t}_k\widetilde{h}_k\bigr).$$
Then
\begin{equation}

\tfrac12(L_1\vee L_1)=\sum_{k=1}^m\Bigl(\tau_{2-n-k}\bigl(t_k-\widetilde{t}_k\bigr)+\tau_k\widetilde{t}_k\Bigr)\,\Omega_k ,
\end{equation}
and consequently:
\begin{itemize}
\item[i)] the quadratic integrability conditions are exactly the $m$ relations
\begin{equation}

\tau_{2-n-k}\bigl(t_k-\widetilde{t}_k\bigr)+\tau_k\widetilde{t}_k=0,\qquad 1\leq k\leq m ;
\end{equation}
\item[ii)] these conditions are also sufficient: if \eqref{eq:52} holds, then $L+L_1$ itself, with no term of
order $\geq2$, satisfies \eqref{eq:18}. In particular no integrability condition of order $\geq3$ occurs.
\end{itemize}
Therefore the versal deformation of the $\osp(n|2)$-module $\Sc^n_d$ is
\begin{equation}

\widetilde{L}=L+\sum_{k=-\infty}^{m}\tau_kf_{\frac k2}+\sum_{k=1}^m\bigl(t_kh_k+\widetilde{t}_k\widetilde{h}_k\bigr)
\end{equation}
over the base
$\mathcal{A}=\Cc\bigl[[\{\tau_k\},\{t_k\},\{\widetilde{t}_k\}]\bigr]/R$, where $R$ is the ideal generated
by the $m$ quadratic relations \eqref{eq:52}; and every integrable formal deformation of $L$ is equivalent to its
infinitesimal part.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.320743+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2fe6bae51b772107050cadf78d21be71c27ee9545b861ae68d16c30d584559f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop subpolynomialconc of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] There exist $\varepsilon_0>0$, $\beta>0$, $R_0 > 0$, and a function $p_0(R)$ such that the following holds for each $R \ge R_0$ and $p < p_0(R)$. Assume that 
