{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.696553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e30fa56ec5a12cad9e2f05b40f5531511b686fbede69bcd5a6837b28b10cf9a3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 20 (Random-walk absorption probabilities) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $U_2,U_3,\dots$ be independent random variables with
$
U_m\sim\operatorname{Bern}(\frac 1 {2m})$,
$m\geq 2$.
Then, for $n\geq 1$ and $d\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \pwalk{n}{d}
    =\pr\left(\sum_{m=2}^{n}U_m\geq d\right).

\end{equation}
Consequently, for $n\geq 2$ and $d\geq 1$,
\begin{equation}
    \pwalk{n}{d}
    =\frac {2n-1}{2n} \pwalk{n-1}{d}
    +\frac {1}{2n} \pwalk{n-1}{d-1}.

\end{equation}
Together with the boundary conditions $\pwalk{n}{0}=1$ for all  $n\geq 1$ and $\pwalk{1}{d}=0$  for all $d\geq 1$, this
recurrence uniquely determines the array.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:40:22.274601+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e312c9875df9b9809cd9663825f4fabb875aea72a7a447f6ac7cfe7b024c60ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm some.precise.degrees of arXiv:2609.02588, 'Ramsey-type results for Maker-Breaker games'] The following holds:
