{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.250140+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e47c3c19e6e2f76ddf39554ab41c2194eed065850e9cefdebf3169d2301fbfd9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition ATTRACTOR of arXiv:2603.12774, 'Synchronization by noise for stochastic differential equations driven by fractional Browni'] Let $\Phi$ be an RDS. A random compact set $\mathcal{A}$ is { called pullback attractor} if 
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item [1)] $\mathcal{A}$ is invariant under \( \Phi \).
		\item [2)] For every compact set $K\subseteq  \mathcal{X}$ 
		we have
		\[ \lim\limits_{t\to\infty} \sup\limits_{x\in K} d(\Phi^t_{\theta_{-t}\omega}(x), \mathcal{A}(\omega)  )=0~~~\mathbb{P}-a.s. \]
	\end{enumerate}
	We call $\mathcal{A}$ a {weak attractor} if the convergence in 2) holds in probability instead of almost surely. The set $\mathcal{A}$ is called a {(weak) point attractor} if it satisfies the properties stated above, assuming that the compact set $K$ in condition 2) consists of a single point.~A (weak) point attractor is said to be {minimal} if it is contained in every other (weak) point attractor.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.735721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e47d1b8f9fcf12e7db24c27b0fc9d35b4b7cddc8f730f097ef4ed9d350746c3b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 15 of arXiv:2506.10213, 'Coupling of forward-backward stochastic differential equations on the Wiener space, and ap'] If the FBSDE \eqref{fbsde:equ:re}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(1)] satisfies Assumption \ref{coefficient:assume:general}, Assumption \ref{fbsde:basic:assume:p:general} with some $p\in [2,\fz)$ and Assumption \ref{ass:linear:diffusion};
        \item[(2)] admits a regular decoupling field $w$ with the Lipschitz constants $L_w$;
        \item[(3)] \eqref{p-condition} holds with $L_g$ replaced by $L_w$.
    \end{itemize}
    Let $\Tz := (X,Y,Z)$ be the solution of \eqref{fbsde:equ:re}.
    Then the estimation for $p$-coupling variance holds
    \begin{align}
