{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.966500+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e378d69625196cbbda63931bd857e5fb8cdde626f81d75ad2a09cc3a1fa255","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 29 of arXiv:2606.12720, 'On McDiarmid's Inequality under Dependence \\ via Approximate Tensorization of Entropy'] {Cumulative Distribution Functions}


    Let $\nu$ be a probability measure on $\R^n$ and $X \sim \nu$. Respectively, define the empirical and average marginal cumulative distribution functions at $x \in \R$ as 
    \begin{align*}
        \hat F_n(x)
        := \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \indicator{X_i \leq x}, 
        \quad \text{and} \quad 
