{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.133813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4edb82ed275f789ce586ea650082ada12ffdc730825de88f8dc400ad0bfeee9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:free_product of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Suppose that we are given two non-trivial connected transitive graphs $G_i = (V(G_i), E(G_i))$, $i = 1,2$, with disjoint vertex sets and some distinguished vertices $o_i$, $i = 1,2$. The {\bf free product}
	\begin{equation}

		G := (V(G), E(G)) :=
		G_1 \star G_2
	\end{equation}
	is constructed in the following way.
	The vertices of $G$ are all the finite {words}:
	\begin{equation}

		V(G) :=  \left\{
		x_1 \ldots x_n \, \left| \, 
		\parbox{17em}{
			$n \in \mathbb{N}; \, x_j \in V(G_1) \cup V(G_2) \setminus \{ o_1, o_2 \}; \\
			x_k \in V(G_i) \implies x_{k+1} \notin V(G_i), \, i=1,2$
		}		
		\right. \right\} 
		\cup
		\left\{ o \right\}
	\end{equation}
	where $o$ denotes the {empty word}, the distinguished vertex of $G$, obtained by identifying each $o_i$, $i = 1,2$ with $o$.
	If we are given words $x = x_1 \ldots x_k \in V(G)$ and $y = y_1 \ldots y_m \in V(G)$ with $x_k \in V(G_i)$ and $y_1 \notin V(G_i)$, then $xy$ stands for the concatenation as words. In addition, for all $x \in V(G)$ we define $x o = o x = x$.
	Furthermore, adjacency in $G$ is given as follows: for $i = 1,2$, if $\{ x,y \} \in E(G_i)$, then $\{ zx, zy \} \in E(G)$ for all $z = z_1 \ldots z_{\ell} \in V(G)$, with $z_{\ell} \notin V(G_i)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.420519+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4ee71e87cec361dfa1e3e59653661e2efae87c6ac87c09d8af36eccf84779fc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2607.28215, 'Almost stochastic dominance via optimal transport'] For $X,Y\in \mathcal{L}_{c,g}(E)$ with $\E[g(X)]\le \E[g(Y)]$ we define 
