{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.963449+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5d676de20469fb92a1f6af3077e348f6e5d9a59687e597b5edb3ab20046bd40","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 25 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Let $T<\infty$. The function $(t,l)\mapsto \tilde W_T(t,l)$ is $\llip{\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_T}$. Specifically, at points of differentiability it holds that
    \begin{equation}
        \partial_l \tilde{W}_T(t,l)=\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{0}^{\tilde\tau^*}\partial_l L^l_{u}\partial_l(\tilde{\mathcal{L}}\tilde{g})(L^l_{u})\,du\right]
    \end{equation}
    where $\tilde\tau^*:=\tilde\tau^*_T(t,l)$ is the optimal stopping time, and 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}[c(t+\tilde\tau^*)]-c(t)\leq \partial_t\tilde{W}_T(t,l)\leq  \mathbb{E}[c(t+\tilde\tau^*)]-c(t) + K''\mathbb{P}(\tilde\tau^*=T-t),
    \end{equation}
     where $K''$ is defined in Proposition \ref{prop:time_lip}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.723877+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5d7b39750d122d145d53e9f430e7975a08343250e50a8346c605ab6bbd37333","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defi:pioxWP of arXiv:2511.06321, 'Torus scaling limits and the plateau of the critical weakly coupled $|\varphi|^4$ model in'] When $j < N$,  the ${\sf ox}$-projection of $W_{j,V}$ is defined as
\begin{align}
	\pi_{{\sf ox}} W_{j, V} =  \pi_{{\sf ox}}  \mathbb{W}_{w_j ,  V_j }

\end{align}
The $\pi_{\sf ox}$-projection of $P_{j,V}$ is defined as
\begin{align}
	\pi_{{\sf ox}} P_{j,V} = \begin{cases} 
			\frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Loc}^{{\sf ox}} \Big( \operatorname{Cov}_{\pi, j+1} [\theta V ; \theta V] + \E_{j+1} \theta W_{j,V}  \Big) & (j +1 < N) \\
			0 & (j+1=N) .
		\end{cases}

\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.332345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5d94cf5f96b028190ee317bb18a7e0a6dbeed3e30866a6bfd399c1c367d45d9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Pro transformation of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] We denote by $T \subset \mathbb{R}$ an interval, we fix $d \geq 1$ and we consider $P, P' \in \mathcal{P}((\mathbb{R}^d)^T)$.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Let $(f_t)_{t \in T}$ be a family of continuous injective functions from $\mathbb{R}^d$ to $\mathbb{R}^d$ and set $  f := \left( \otimes_{t \in T} f_t \right): (x_t)_{t \in T} \in {(\mathbb{R}^d)}^T \mapsto (f_t(x_t))_{t \in T} \in {(\mathbb{R}^d)}^T$.  If $P'$ is a weak (resp. the strong) local Markov transform of $P$, then ${f }_\# P'$ is a weak (resp. the strong) local Markov transform of ${ f}_\# P$.
			\item Let $S,T \subset \mathbb{R}$ be two intervals and $\phi :S \to T$ be a strictly increasing continuous function. If $P'$ is a weak (resp. the strong) local Markov transform of $P$, then $P'^{\phi} \in \mathcal{P}((\mathbb{R}^d)^S)$ is a weak (resp. the strong) local Markov transform of $P^\phi \in \mathcal{P}((\mathbb{R}^d)^S)$.
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.473436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5db930fde45d8467d1b84d26463da2f6577f38d9f6b62246987c8227caf3a6f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:l2-chain of arXiv:2606.19763, 'Optimal Sparsification of Gaussian Processes'] Let $T$ be finite.
	Suppose $v_t:[0,1]\to\R^n$ and $a_t:[0,1]\to\R$ are absolutely continuous and that, for constants $L,M<\infty$,
	\[
		\|v_t'(\theta)\|_2\leq  L\,,
		\qquad
		|a_t'(\theta)|\leq  M
	\]
	for every $t\in T$ and for a.e.~$\theta\in[0,1]$.
	Fix $\beta>0$, and set
	\[
		H_\theta(g)
		=
		\frac{1}{\beta}
		\log\pbra{
		\sum_{t\in T}
		\exp\pbra{\beta\pbra{g\cdot v_t(\theta)+a_t(\theta)}}
		}\,.
	\]
	Then $\theta\mapsto H_\theta$ is absolutely continuous as a map from $[0,1]$ into $L^2$.
	Moreover, for a.e.~$\theta$, the $L^2$-derivative is represented by
	\[
		\frac{d}{d\theta}H_\theta(g)
		=
		\sum_{t\in T}
		\omega_t^\theta(g)
		\pbra{
		g\cdot v_t'(\theta)
		+
		a_t'(\theta)
		}\,,
	\]
	where
	\[
		\omega_t^\theta(g)
		:=
		\frac{
		\exp\left(\beta(g\cdot v_t(\theta)+a_t(\theta))\right)
		}{
		\sum_{t'\in T}
		\exp\left(\beta(g\cdot v_{t'}(\theta)+a_{t'}(\theta))\right)
		}\,.
	\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:48:00.287512+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5dc49f940051336f267ede609ee8e97be64c4ae3d48a4f84d77788c186264a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm fdmn of arXiv:2609.03033, 'An Overview of Einstein $4$-Manifolds'] Two smooth  simply-connected compact oriented $4$-manifolds are orientedly {\sf homeomorphic} 
if and only if 
\begin{itemize}
\item 
they have the same Euler characteristic $\chi$;
\item 
they have the same signature $\tau$; and 
\item both are spin, 
or both are 
non-spin.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.708506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5de368b91a9e99de8c545e919ddfd46d8ea7e1097266ce72fec28f2f8c93f29","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:GNpos of arXiv:2609.00481, 'The frameset of the second-order B-spline along $ab=1/q$'] For $N\in \mathbb{N}$, and $\gamma\in \mathbb{R}$, we have
\begin{align}
