        and the coefficients defined above are real and satisfy
        \begin{align}
                c\leq\mu_1\leq C, \qquad |\mu_2|+|\mu_3|\leq C. 
        \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.143913+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5eec5f074f5d32480269281de5fc3dfc635f104c5f0554d12555b9cd87e3444","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2608.11071, 'Scaling Laws for Majority-based Opinion Dynamics in the Presence of Stubborn Agents'] Fix $(\gamma_0, \gamma_1)\in \Gamma_{\text{crit}}^k$. The two possibilities are $\gamma_0=\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_0\neq\gamma_1$. For $\gamma_0=\gamma_1$, let $r$ be the unique root of $\Delta_k$ in $\mc S$ with multiplicity three and for $\gamma_0\neq\gamma_1$, let $r$ denote the root of $\Delta_k$ in $\mc S$ with multiplicity one. The following statements hold.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If $\gamma_0 \neq \gamma_1$, then, for each sufficiently small $\delta>0$ and $x\in \mc S^n$, we have $\EE_{x}[T_{\text{enter}}^n(B_{\delta}(r))]= O(n^{1/3})$, and the mixing time of the process $X^n$ satisfies $t_{\text{mix}}^n =\Theta(n^{1/3})$.
        \item If $\gamma_0=\gamma_1$, then for any $x \in \mc S^n$, we have $\EE_x[T^n_{\text{enter}}(B_{1/n}(r))]= O(n^{1/2})$ and the mixing time of the process $X^n$ satisfies $t_{\text{mix}}^n =\Theta(n^{1/2})$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.668658+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5ef7b9c6833e69c7fc76c27801dfc387a573ed8f7b61e07cf861a747de2ae38","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_map_psi_sigma of arXiv:2605.26804, '\Large Large Deviation Principle for the Empirical Measures of Simple Random Walks on $\ov'] For $\sigma \in \{-,+\}$ we define the map $\psi^{\sigma}$ by setting
\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{cccc}
\psi^{\sigma} & : \, \Omega_{\textup{c}} \times \Omega_{\textup{exc}}^{-\sigma} \times \Omega_{\textup{mea}}^{\sigma} & \longrightarrow & \mathscr{C}_{n}^{\sigma} \\
& \big(v^{\textup{c}},v^{\textup{exc}},v^{\textup{mea}}\big) & \longmapsto & v^{\textup{c}} \cdot \xi^{-\sigma}(v^{\textup{c}}_{-1}) \cdot v^{\textup{exc}} \cdot \chi^{\sigma} \cdot v^{\textup{mea}} \cdot b
\end{array},
\end{equation*}
where $b \in \Omega_{\textup{fin}}$ is an arbitrary word which satisfies the properties \textit{1.}-\textit{3.} of Lemma~(lemma_existence_b_lower_bound of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.788465+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5f0e99e8aef6a26137ec15b14afbdb103a1fc0ab6a34dd9294e0584526f6b43","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Definition~3.2 of~\cite{LMST} of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] For $n\in \mathbb N$, $\delta>0$, $t>0$, consider the Ising model in $R_n$ with random boundary condition $\tau \sim \mathbf{P}$. We say $\mathcal A(L_n,t_n,\delta_n)$ holds if 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbf{E}||\mu_{t_n}^\pm - \pi^\tau\|\le \delta_n \qquad \text{for all $\mathbf P\in \mathcal D(R_{L_n})$}\,.
    \end{align*}
    The statement $\mathcal B(L_n,t_n,\delta_n)$ is defined with $Q_{L_n}$ replacing $R_{L_n}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:12:13.499677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5f14ae489e79d353c2dcd3abb78ed50741a9b2c795529bf574826bcf4be3fd5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2608.28528, 'Strong solutions of SDEs with critical discontinuities in diffusion coefficients'] Under the conditions of Theorem \ref{thm1}, for every $p>1$, provided that the form-bound $\nu$ of the derivatives of $\sigma$ is sufficiently small, and $\theta$ is fixed sufficiently small, the solution to the terminal-value problem
\begin{equation}

\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
(\partial_t + \frac{1}{2} a\cdot \nabla^2)u=0 \quad \text{ in } ]0,T[ \times \mathbb R^d, \\
u|_{t=T}=f \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^d), 
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $a=\sigma\sigma^{\top}$, satisfies
$$
\|u(t)\|_{L^p_\rho} \leq e^{\omega (T-t)}\|f\|_{L^p_\rho}, \quad t \in [0,T],
$$
where $\omega=\omega(c_\delta,\theta)$.  The same estimate holds in the non-weighted case $\rho = 1$, i.e.\,$\theta=0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.162031+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5f1aa6f0fe09e4b71bbb6b45fa847f08ec1f9111d343d6ade80f3434273ceae","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def:randomindex of arXiv:2606.24793, '\LARGE Random sequential nearest-neighbor coloring on trees'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a connected, locally finite graph, and let $v \in V$.
We say that a random sequence $(z_j)_{j \ge 1}$ is a uniform increasing indexing of the vertices of $G$ centered at $v$ if it satisfies the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] (indexing of $V$) $\{z_j : j \ge 1\} = V$ and $z_j \neq z_i$ for $j \neq i$;
    \item[(ii)] (increasing) if $j < i$, then $d(v,z_j) \le d(v,z_i)$;
    \item[(iii)] (uniformity) for every $r \ge 0$, the order of the vertices at distance $r$ from $v$ induced by $(z_j)_{j \ge 1}$ is uniform among all possible orderings and independent of the orderings induced at other distances.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.853133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5f6453c08ea2ed065cc90409b94d95563711bbfb8ddc0d9d5d62cfd61b7bdaa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:WishartNorm of arXiv:2410.23915, 'Asymptotic Bounds and Online Algorithms for Average-Case Matrix Discrepancy'] Let $W$ be an $m \times m$ Wishart matrix of rank $r \leq m$. Then
        \begin{equation*}
            \norm{\overline{W}}_{\psi_1} \leq O(\sqrt{r}).
        \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.548149+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5f742cd899598f2e286ff553f4c9c782811f1107e8765676d62754bba1c4ff4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 19 (Pairwise Disagreement~\citep{black2022model}) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] The pairwise disagreement of the empirical Rashomon set $\emprashomonset$, at a test instance $x$, is the average disagreement between pairs of models $f_{k},f_{l} \in \emprashomonset$ and is defined as,
\begin{align}
      \pairwisedisag(x) = \frac{1}{\abs{\emprashomonset}(\abs{\emprashomonset}-1)}\sum_{h_k,h_l \in \emprashomonset, h_k \ne _l}\Indic{h_{k}(x) \ne h_{l}(x)},
\end{align}  
where $h(x)= \Indic{f(x) \geq \frac{1}{2}}$ is the hard decision of $f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.742100+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5fa840ee5b06e7cde8878d976a6d3d37d254291b28c705fb24f138faed965de","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 43 of arXiv:2506.10494, 'Geometric Jensen-Shannon Divergence Between Gaussian Measures On Hilbert Space'] Let	$A \in \Sym(\Hcal)\cap\HS(\Hcal)$, $I+A > 0$. Then
		$(A-\log(I+A))\in \Sym^{+}(\Hcal) \cap\Tr(\Hcal)$. Furthermore,
		\begin{align}
			||A-\log(I+A)||_{\tr} = -\log\dettwo(I+A) \leq ||(I+A)^{-1}||\;||A||^2_{\HS}.
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

