{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.116101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e697e6349af0e5dd913cd7da1a06be9d0ac3f34d02adc8b515a5feb84d322170","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 135 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Let $\md,\me\lesssim1$ be positive numbers. There exists a constant
    $C>1$ such that the following holds. 
	Fix $(x', y') = (x', \gamma_{x'})$ on the arctic boundary $\mathfrak A_{q;a,b,c}$ with $x' \in [1/4, 5/4]$, and denote $f = f_{x'} (\gamma_{x'})$; $\gamma = \gamma_{x'}$; $\gamma' = \gamma_{x'}'$; and $\gamma'' = \gamma_{x'}''$. 
	 Let $z=(x,y)$ with 
	\begin{flalign}

		x = x' + (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{1/2} \hat{x} - \gamma' \mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e} \hat{y}; \qquad y = \gamma_{x'} + \gamma' (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{1/2} \hat{x} + \mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e} \hat{y}
	\end{flalign}
        and assume that
        \begin{equation}

       \frac{ \md}{|\gamma'|},\frac{\me_{z;q}}{\me},\frac{\md_{z}}\md\in   [C^{-1},C].
        \end{equation}

	\noindent Denoting  
	\begin{flalign}

		\hat{f}(\hat{x}, \hat{y}) = (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{-1/2} ( f(x,y) - f); \qquad \Gamma =  \gamma''- \gamma'(1 - \gamma') \log \mathfrak{q}, 
	\end{flalign}
	\noindent we have 
	\begin{flalign}

		\hat{f} (\hat{x}, \hat{y}) =  \displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{i} \Gamma^{1/2}}{(\gamma'-1)^2}   \big( 2 (\gamma'^2 + 1) \hat{y} - \gamma'' \hat{x}^2 \big)^{1/2}  - \displaystyle\frac{\gamma'' \hat{x}}{(\gamma'-1)^2} + \mathcal{O} ( (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{1/2}).
	\end{flalign} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:40:59.361580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e69d75508d1a45fb7e5e1993fafd299a7904c97073f87a3240c9d4ee89edf70c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 (Global reciprocal bounds) of arXiv:2609.03958, '2609.03958'] Let $n\geq 2$ and $K\geq 0$. Suppose that $\lambda\in \Gamma_2$ satisfies $\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_n\geq -K$ and $\sigma_2(\lambda)=f$ with $f_0\leq f\leq f_1$ for two constants $f_1\geq f_0>0$.  Then there exists  $C >0$ depending only on $n$, $K$, $f_0$ and $f_1$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sigma_{1;i}(\lambda)}\leq C(\sigma_1(\lambda)+1),
 \qquad
 \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sigma_1^2(\lambda)\sigma_{1;i}(\lambda)}\leq C.
