{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:53:51.329813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6b9aeab11487bfb4df3414fb2951a6a6a4fe3d7113885e4883ad5cc3cd4065f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:rep of arXiv:2605.03703, 'Scaling Limits of Bivariate Nearly-Unstable Hawkes Processes and Applications to Rough Vol'] Let
$M_t^{T,i}:=N_t^{T,i}-\int_0^t\lambda_s^{T,i}\,ds$.
Then $d\langle M^{T,i}\rangle_t=\lambda_t^{T,i}\,dt$,
$\langle M^{T,1},M^{T,2}\rangle\equiv0$, and
\begin{align}
\lambda_t^{T,1}
  &=\mu_T^1\Bigl(1+\int_0^t\psi_T^1(u)\,du\Bigr)
   +\int_0^t\psi_T^1(t-s)\,dM_s^{T,1},
   \\[2pt]
\lambda_t^{T,2}
  &=\mathbb{E}[\lambda_t^{T,2}]
   +\int_0^t\psi_T^2(t-s)\,dM_s^{T,2}
   +\int_0^t\Psi_T^{12}(t-s)\,dM_s^{T,1},

\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\Psi_T^{12}}(z)
=\widehat{\phi_T^{12}}(z)
\frac{1}{1-a_T^1\widehat{\phi^1}(z)}
\frac{1}{1-a_T^2\widehat{\phi^2}(z)}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\lambda_t^{T,2}]
=\mu_T^2\Bigl(1+\int_0^t\psi_T^2\Bigr)
+\mu_T^1(\Psi_T^{12}*\mathbf 1)(t).
\end{equation}
Consequently the renormalized martingale parts are
\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_t^{T,i}=
\frac{1-a_T^i}{m_iT^{\alpha_i-1}}
\int_0^{Tt}\psi_T^i(Tt-s)\,dM_s^{T,i},
\qquad
\mathcal C_t^{T}=\frac{1-a_T^2}{m_2T^{\alpha_2-1}}
\int_0^{Tt}\Psi_T^{12}(Tt-s)\,dM_s^{T,1}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
\psi_T^i(r)=\frac{\varepsilon_T^i}{1-a_T^i}
 g_T^i(\varepsilon_T^i r),
\qquad
\Psi_T^{12}(r)=
\frac{\varepsilon_T^1}{T^{2\alpha_1-\alpha_2}(1-a_T^1)(1-a_T^2)}
\tilde h_T(\varepsilon_T^1r).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.173419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6bab3d680eefadd134cff3f80c6dd3c1cf7d640ce98c94f504cd17a5c4bfae3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D_Kr_tree of arXiv:2510.26724, 'Sharp Fuss-Catalan thresholds in graph bootstrap percolation'] We call a graph $T$ a {\it $K_r$-tree}
(or a {\it clique tree})
of {\it order} $\vartheta$ if it is 
the union of $\vartheta$
many copies $H_1,\ldots,H_\vartheta$ of $K_r$, 
and that, 
for every $1<i\leqslant \vartheta$, $H_i$ shares exactly 
one edge with $H_1 \cup\cdots \cup H_{i-1}$.
We call an edge $e\in E(G)$ an {\it internal edge} 
of $T$ if it is contained in at least two of the copies of $K_r$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.577764+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c28f1ef61145861068066cd48f8d3e80ac31ec538534f9a936dd8886862515","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 40 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] A \textit{current} $\n$ on $G_\fg$ is a function defined on the edge set $E_\fg$ and taking its values in $\mathbb N=\lbrace 0,1,\ldots\rbrace$. We let $\Omega_{G_\fg}$ be the set of currents on $G_\fg$. The set of \emph{sources} of $\n$, denoted by $\sn$, is defined as
\begin{equation}
\sn:=\Big\{ x \in V_\fg \: : \: \sum_{y\in V_\fg:\: xy\in E_\fg}\n_{xy}\textup{ is odd}\Big\}.
\end{equation}
We also define, for every $\beta\geq 0$ and $\mathsf{h}\in (\mathbb R^+)^V$, 
\begin{equation}
w_{\beta,\mathsf{h}}^G(\n):=\prod_{\substack{xy\in E}}\dfrac{\beta^{\n_{xy}}}{\n_{xy}!}\prod_{x\in V}\frac{\mathsf{h}_x^{\n_{x\fg}}}{\n_{x\fg}!}.
\end{equation}
If $\mathsf{h}=0$, we simply write $w_{\beta}^G(\n)=w_{\beta,0}^G(\n)$.  Similarly, we define $\Omega_G$, the set of currents on $G$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.673000+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c3e588a9cbff1f5706ba501791aa713ddf5d892fc745becfa78c645e45afe7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:gamma:summing of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] The space \( \gamma_\infty(H,X) \) is a Banach space with respect to the norm
    \begin{equation} 
        \| A \|_{\gamma_\infty(H,X)} =  \sup_{(h_k)_{k=1}^n \in \mathcal{F}(H)} \left( \int_\Omega \left\| \sum_{k=1}^n \xi_k(\omega) A h_k \right\|^2 \mu(d\omega) \right)^\frac{1}{2}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.728180+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c430ecc4a74ae64bb80ac1081b0b6a32b0fe50e0bcd2d64237e291ee27f7c8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mixing-time-langevin of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] Fix $\alpha \in (0,\infty)$, $\beta = \frac{\alpha}{\theta}$,  and $\theta>\theta_0(\alpha)$ large. Then with $\mathbb{P}_M$-probability $1-o(1)$, for large $N$, the following hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item  If $\alpha<1$, then  $t_{\mathsf{mix}} = \Theta(\log N)$.
          \item If $\alpha>1$, then $t_{\mathsf{mix}}^{\mathsf{sym}} = \Theta(\log N)$ even though $t_{\mathsf{mix}} = \exp( \Theta(N))$. 
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.703217+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c463a5fd8097eb2674dafd2046fb66b31b36313e2fcaf64947d07fcb466f75","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:crossover_analysis of arXiv:2604.20215, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices II: Markov Chain Comparison and Cr'] For any diagram  $\Gamma=(V,E)\in \mathcal{T}_s$,  the critical  diagram functions   admit a transition
\begin{equation}
    \lim\limits_{\gamma\rightarrow\infty}\gamma^{|E|-|V|}\mathcal{F}_{\Gamma}^{\mathrm{(Crit)}}(\gamma^{-\frac{1}{3}}t_1,\ldots,\gamma^{-\frac{1}{3}}t_s;\gamma^{\alpha})=\mathcal{F}_{\Gamma}^{\mathrm{(Super)}}(t_1,\ldots,t_s),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \lim\limits_{\gamma\rightarrow0}\gamma^{|E|-|V|+1}\mathcal{F}_{\Gamma}^{\mathrm{(Crit)}}(t_1,\ldots,t_s;\gamma^{\alpha})=\mathcal{F}_{\Gamma}^{\mathrm{(Sub)}}(t_1,\ldots,t_s).
