{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.313315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6cacd5b53b2f418c651617470422421f3f42aaa056bb0341f37cf796972eab9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem WP_H^2 of arXiv:2606.00325, 'Ergodicity for a class of stochastic Hasegawa-Mima equations with dissipation'] Assume that $A$ satisfies (ass2 of the paper), $v\in C^\infty_c(\mathcal{D})$ and the noise coefficients $(f_k)_{k=1}^M \subset L^2(\mathcal{D})$. Then for every $\phi_0 \in H^2 \cap V$ let $\phi_t$ be the unique solution of (eq:main of the paper) in the sense of (def-sol-H1 of the paper). Then the process $\xi = \phi -\Delta \phi - v$ belongs to $C(0, T; L^2) \cap L^2(0, T; Y) $ almost surely and it verifies, for every $\psi \in Y$
    \begin{align}
        \left\langle\xi_t, \psi\right\rangle - \left\langle\xi_0, \psi\right\rangle = -\int_0^t \brak{B^{1/2 }\xi_s, B^{1/2} \psi}ds + \int_0^t\left\langle \xi_s, \nabla^\perp \phi \cdot \nabla \psi\right\rangleds + \int_0^t \left\langle\xi_s , dW_t\right\rangle.
    \end{align}
    Moreover it satisfies the following energy balance
    \begin{align}
    \mathbb{E}[|\xi(t)|^2] - \mathbb{E}[|\xi_0|^2]
     +2\int_0^t\mathbb{E}\left[\|\xi\|_{Y}^2\right]dt = 2 \int_0^t\mathbb{E}\left[\brak{B^{1/2} \xi, B^{1/2} v} \right]dt+ \sigma t,
\end{align}
    where $\sigma = \sum_{k=1}^M \sigma_k^2|f_j|^2$. Moreover, $\xi$ is a Markov process in $L^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.951777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6cb51ecaa029bad5be3fa89159c9c52ec7c18df4e709cd33fd1c308a2dfb6d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition binomial of arXiv:2512.19897, 'On the convoy of the ASEP speed process'] Let $s_\lambda$ be the Schur polynomial indexed by a partition $\lambda$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        s_\lambda\left(1+x_1,\dots 1+ x_n\right) = \sum_{\mu \prec \lambda} det\left[\binom{\lambda_i + n -i}{\mu_j + n -i}\right]_{i,j = 1}^{n} s_{\mu}\left(x_1, \dots x_n\right).
    \end{equation}
    In particular, take $\lambda$ as a $n \times 1$ one row Young diagram, we have the binomial formula for homogeneous symmetric function.
    \begin{equation}
        h_k\left(1+x_1,\dots 1+ x_n\right) = \sum_{j = 0}^{k} \binom{k + N -i}{j + N -1} h_{j}\left(x_1, \dots x_n\right).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.625532+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6d4bee6fea1e7f9dc5a947ef8552d8488faddb4f03519deacc11fc9b7dfe46a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lem: property of convolution of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] For a given $u:\mathbb{R}_+\times\mathbb{R}^d\to \mathbb{R}$, assume that there exist $\beta\in (0,1]$, and $a_1,a_2,a_3\geq 0$ such that
		\begin{equation*}
			\begin{split}
				&|u(t,x)-u(t,y)|\leq a_1|x-y|^{\beta},\\
				&|u(t,x)-u(s,x)|\leq a_2|t-s|^{\beta}+a_3.
			\end{split}
		\end{equation*}
		Then for any $\epsilon\in (0,1)$, $t,s\in(\epsilon,\infty)$ and $x,y\in \mathbb{R}^d$, we have
		\begin{equation*}
			\begin{split}
				&|u_{\epsilon}(t,x)-u(t,x)|\leq (a_1+a_2)\epsilon^{\beta}+a_3,\\
				&|u_{\epsilon}(t,x)-u_{\epsilon}(t,y)|\leq a_1|x-y|^{\beta},\\
				&|u_{\epsilon}(t,x)-u_{\epsilon}(s,x)|\leq a_2|t-s|^{\beta}+a_3,\\
				&|\partial_tu_{\epsilon}(t,x)-\partial_tu_{\epsilon}(t,y)|\leq a_1|\partial_t\zeta|_{L^1}\epsilon^{-1}|x-y|^{\beta},\\
				&|\nabla_xu_{\epsilon}(t,x)-\nabla_xu_{\epsilon}(t,y)|\leq a_1|\nabla_x\zeta|_{L^1}\epsilon^{-1}|x-y|^{\beta},\\
				&|\partial_tu_{\epsilon}(t,x)-\partial_tu_{\epsilon}(s,x)|\leq |\partial_t\zeta|_{L^1}\epsilon^{-1}(a_2|t-s|^{\beta}+a_3),\\
				&|\nabla_xu_{\epsilon}(t,x)-\nabla_xu_{\epsilon}(s,x)|\leq |\nabla_x\zeta|_{L^1}\epsilon^{-1}(a_2|t-s|^{\beta}+a_3),
			\end{split}
		\end{equation*}
		where
		\begin{equation*}
			|\eta|_{L^1}:=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{1+d}}|\eta(t,x)|dtdx, \ \ \eta=\partial_t\zeta, \nabla_x\zeta.
		\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

