{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.007114+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6dfe3659f6c71ab50f6110ee3b300bb8a1096c70a4cfb49d9fd6853320e8b8e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 (Classification of all fixed points) of arXiv:2608.14802, 'Dynamical Gibbs-non-Gibbs transitions for finite-alphabet models on trees'] Assume that $\theta>1+2\sqrt{2}$ and define 
\begin{equation*}
    x_\pm:=
\left(
\frac{\theta-1\pm\sqrt{(\theta-1)^2-8}}{2}
\right)^2.
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, for each $i\in \{1,2,3\}$, define the vectors $l^{(i)}_-,l^{(i)}_+\in \Delta^2$ componentwise by
\begin{equation}
    l^{(i)}_\pm(j):=\frac{1+(x_\pm-1)\delta_{ij}}{x_\pm+2}
\end{equation}
for $j\in \{1,2,3\}$.
Then, for $\theta\neq 4$, the points $l^{(i)}_-$ where $i\in \{1,2,3\}$ are precisely the fixed points of $f$ with exactly one unstable direction, that is, the fixed points $l$ for which $Df(l)$ has exactly one eigenvalue of modulus strictly greater than $1$. We refer to these fixed points as $f$-saddles. The remaining symmetry-breaking fixed points are $l^{(i)}_+$ where $i\in \{1,2,3\}$, and they are all $f$-stable.
Moreover, both eigenvalues of $Df$ at the free fixed point $l_{\text{free}}:=\left(\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3}\right)$ have modulus strictly greater than $1$ if $\theta>4$ and strictly smaller than $1$ if $\theta<4$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.044680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e0d2c6252cb6bb0362cf1ad7fdd86e2aab70bc191c9b9500d108f081251846","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2510.24244, 'Statistical properties of Markov shifts: part II-LLT'] For every $0<\delta<T$ and $L\in\bbN$ there exists $0<\delta(L)<1/2$ such that if
\begin{equation}

\varepsilon\leq \delta(L) 
\end{equation}
then  for every $j$ and $\ell\leq L+1$ we have the following.
If  for some nonzero real $t$ such that $\del \leq |t|\leq T$ there exists $h$ with 
$\|h\|_{\om,*}\leq 1$ and
\begin{equation}

\inf_{x}|\cL_{j,t}^{\ell}h(x)|\geq 1-\varepsilon 
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
f_{j+\ell-1}=g_{j,t,\ell}+H_{j+\ell-1}-H_{j+\ell}\circ T_{j+\ell-1}+(2\pi/t) Z_{t,j,\ell}
\end{equation}
where $Z_{t,j,\ell}$ is integer valued, and for every $0<r<1$ we have 
$\sup_{t,j,\ell}\|g_{t,j,\ell}\|_{j+\ell,\om^r}\leq c_r(\ell)$ where $c_r(\ell)\to 0$ as $\ell\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.038019+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e132caf274f8d755b5398164435d9d56a806c8e466be2d6aa69a03888c639a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 (\cite{Nishimori_Tomisaki_Tsuchida_Uemura_2025} Proposition3.10) of arXiv:2606.26496, 'Homeomorphism of the Revuz correspondence under Dynkin class assumptions'] \begin{itemize}
			\item[{\rm (i)}] (monotonicity) Let $\mu$ and $\nu$ be Borel measures on $(E,\mathscr{B}(E))$. Assume that $\mu \leq \nu$, that is, $\mu(A) \leq \nu(A)$ for $A \in \mathscr{B}(E)$. If $\nu \in {\sf S}_0$, then $\mu \in {\sf S}_0$ and $U_\alpha \mu \leq U_\alpha \nu$.
			\item[{\rm (ii)}] (convex cone) If $\mu,\nu \in {\sf S}_0$, $a,b\geq 0$ and $a > 0$, then  $a \mu + b \nu \in {\sf S}_0$ and $U_\alpha (a \mu + b \nu)= a U_\alpha \mu + b U_\alpha \nu$.
			\item[{\rm (iii)}] (ideal) Let $\mathscr{B}_{b,+}(E)$ be the set of all nonnegatiove bounded measurable functions on $E$. Then $\mathscr{B}_{b,+}(E)$ is an ideal in $\mathcal{S}_0$ (i.e. $\mathscr{B}_{b,+}(E){\sf S}_0 \subset {\sf S}_0$) in the sense that $f\mu$ belongs to ${\sf S}_0$ whenever $\mu \in {\sf S}_0$ and $f \in \mathscr{B}_{n,+}(E)$.
		\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.731711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e222684db5e5aac679a86aab3060841923ea86706f2ab12ddb0ecece1658e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Fix $0<\kappa \le \kappa_0$ for $\kappa_0$ sufficiently\footnote{For example, $\kappa_0 = 1/100$ would do} small. We write $\mfT$ for the regularity structure generated by the noise types $\mathfrak{L}_- = \{\<b0>, \<p0>, \<0t>\}$ and kernel types $\mfL_+ = \{\<k>, \<bk>, \<pk>\}$ where the rule (in the sense of \cite[Definition 5.7]{BHZ}) is the one naturally associated to the system \eqref{eq:main_sys}. In particular, each $\mathcal{I}_{\diamond}$ for $\diamond \in \mfL_+$ is a degree $2$ integration operator and
\begin{equ}
	|\<b0>|_\fs = |\<p0>|_\fs = - 3/2 - \kappa, \qquad |\<0t>|_\fs = - 2- \kappa.
\end{equ}
Further, we write $\mcT$ for the canonical basis of the model space of $\mfT$, which is therefore henceforth denoted by $\scal{\mcT}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.634488+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e32af2859a0c918659f692ebc1578497529727fce71c43272f78e9584981df","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2506.17109, 'Limit theorems under nonlinear expectations dominated by sublinear expectations'] Let $\mathbb{\hat{F}}[\cdot]$ be a sublinear expectation on
