{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.007581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e3733ff361c284af73feabb9eafc88c7c5b7526a5233e436d0555fc492ed0c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition L-G Trans of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Given the differential equation (equation W-equa1tr of the paper), let $I_1=(a_1,b_1)$, $I_2=(a_2,b_2)$ be two intervals containing exactly one turning point of $f$, say, $z_1\in I_1$ and $z_2\in I_2$. Define $\zeta$ by
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \frac{2}{3}\zeta^{\frac{3}{2}}(z)= \int_{z}^{z_1}f^{\frac{1}{2}}(t)dt,~z\le z_1\\\\
  \frac{2}{3}(-\zeta)^\frac{3}{2}(z)=\int_{z_1}^z(-f)^\frac{1}{2}(t)dt,~z>z_1
\end{array}
 \right. 
\end{equation}
on $I_1$, and similarly,
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \frac{2}{3}\zeta^{\frac{3}{2}}(z)= \int_{z_2}^{z}f^{\frac{1}{2}}(t)dt,~z>z_2\\\\
  \frac{2}{3}(-\zeta)^\frac{3}{2}(z)=\int_{z}^{z_2}(-f)^\frac{1}{2}(t)dt,~z\le z_2
\end{array}
 \right. 
\end{equation}
on $I_2$. Then for any $z\in I_j$ where $j\in\{1,2\}$, $\zeta':=\frac{d\zeta}{dz}$ is positive when $z>z_j$ and negative when $z<z_j$. Moreover, for any $z\in I_j$, define $W:=(\zeta')^{\frac{1}{2}}w$ when $z>z_j$ and $W:=(-\zeta')^\frac{1}{2}w$ when $z\le z_j$. Further define $\tilde{f}:=\frac{f}{\zeta}$, which equals $(\zeta')^2$. Then the previous equation (equation W-equa1tr of the paper) can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d^2W(\zeta)}{d\zeta^2}=\left(\kappa_N^2\zeta+\Psi(\zeta)\right)W(\zeta)
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
  \Psi(\zeta)=\tilde{f}^{-\frac{1}{4}}\frac{d^2}{d\zeta^2}\left(\tilde{f}^{\frac{1}{4}}\right)+\frac{g}{\tilde{f}}.
\end{equation*}
This $\zeta$ is called the \textbf{Liouville--Green~transformation} of $f$.","status":"unverified"}

