{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.554208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7203122fd3b3487a75a7795703f342630407177ae3dc17d942fe261b94a2618","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma cint of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] For all $x,y\in[n]$ with $x\neq y$  and uniformly over $u\in [0,1]$, we have
\begin{align}
\mathbb E\bigl[u^{d(x)+d(y)}\mathbf{1}_{x,y\in\mathcal{Q}_n\cap \mathcal{T}_n}  \mathbf{1}_{B_{\omega}(x)\cap B_{\omega}(y)\neq  \varnothing}\bigr]  = \frac{c_{\textrm{int}}(w,u)}{n}+ O\Bigl(\frac{1}{n^2}\Bigr),
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
c_{\textrm{int}}(w,u) :=  2\sum_{(i,j)\in \mathcal{M}} \mathbb E^{i,j,\ell_{i,j}}_{\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{Y}'} \bigl[u^{Y_k+Y_k'}\mathbf{1}_{Y_{2k}=Y_{2k}'=0}\bigr].
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb E^{i,j,\ell_{i,j}}_{\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{Y}'} \bigl[u^{Y_k+Y_k'}\mathbf{1}_{Y_{2k}=Y_{2k}'=0}\bigr]$ is explicitly given by Proposition~(prop:cross_monster of the paper), 
	and where $\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}(w^2)$, $\ell_{i,j}=\ell_{i,j}(w^2)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.244813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e725db714098945970087a4bd89b31bf8e1d0a6217ee7c15fcff549b35342c49","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 64 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Recall that $\rho=k/N$, $\alpha=1-2\rho$, as well as $\varepsilon=\varepsilon(N,k)=N^{-3/2}\beta^{3/2}$ for $\beta=\beta(\alpha)$ from \eqref{def:betaASEP}. For all $i\in \lbr M \rbr$, $z\in \R$, $t \geq 0$, and periodic initial data $ h^{\per,\varepsilon}_0$, we define the \textbf{rescaled patched ASEP height function} as 
\begin{align*}
\hfix^{\textup{patch},\delta,\varepsilon}( \hfix^{\per,\varepsilon}_0;z,t) := \sigma(\alpha)^{-1}\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{3}}&\Big(2\mu(\alpha)\varepsilon^{-1}t +\mu^{\prime}(\alpha)\beta(\alpha)z\varepsilon^{-\frac{2}{3}} \\
&- h^{\textup{patch},\delta}( h^{\per,\varepsilon}_0,0;2\alpha t \varepsilon^{-1} + \beta(\alpha)z\varepsilon^{-\frac{2}{3}},2(1-q)^{-1}\varepsilon^{-1}t) \Big) 
\end{align*} whenever the argument for $h^{\textup{patch},\delta}$ is an integer, and linear interpolation otherwise. Here, we obtain $\hfix^{\per,\varepsilon}_0$ from $h^{\per,\varepsilon}_0$ as in \eqref{def:RescaledPeriodicASEPheight}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.441230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e72667d78fc3fc64cac662f8a893593743ee4b2ae3b1864aabaf9cdfe8427ae1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 40 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] Let $p=p(x,\xi)$ be of the form
    \begin{equation}
        p(x,\xi)=\sum_{k\in \Lambda}p_k(x)e^{2i\pi \ps{k}{\xi}}, \quad(x,\xi)\in [0,1]^d\times \T^d,
    \end{equation}
    where $\Lambda=\prod_{j=1}^d\disc{-n_j}{m_j}$, $n_j,m_j\geq 0$, and $p_k:[0,1]^d\to \C$, $k\in \Lambda$ is measurable. Let us denote 
    \begin{equation*}
        S:=\sum_{j=1}^d (n_j+m_j)>0\mm\m\m\text{and}\mm\m\m \Xi:=\prod_{j=1}^d(n_j+m_j+1).
    \end{equation*}
    We assume that: there exists $m>0$ such that for almost every $x\in [0,1]^d$, 
    \begin{equation}
