\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.699628+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7310d58d7e6da586b669e25f7a928fc53f97a8fc419fc4475f90b2302e197b3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 28 of arXiv:2608.30990, 'Convergence of symmetries: nilpotency, dimension, and perfectness'] Let $G$ be a Lie group with Lie algebra  $\mathfrak{g}$, and $C \geq 1$. Assume $\mathfrak{g}$ is equipped with a norm $\Vert \cdot \Vert $ and  
\begin{equation}
      \Vert     [\xx , \yy ]  \Vert  \leq  C \Vert \xx \Vert \Vert \yy  \Vert  
\end{equation}
for all $\xx , \yy \in \mathfrak{g}$. Then for all $\xx , \yy \in B_{1/16C}^{\mathfrak{g}}(0)$, there is $\zz \in B_{1/2C}^{\mathfrak{g}}(0)$ with
\[     \exp (\zz) = \exp (\xx) \exp (\yy) .     \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.045617+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e731907b1d1876f26ef246bf41ffb4cb8dbb2ae0420b0ca4a3d6064cd2c4db07","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_0_limdif of arXiv:2411.13972, 'The Stochastic Bessel operator at high temperatures'] ~\\ \emph{
The limiting diffusion $r_\mu$ is defined as follows:}
\begin{itemize}
	\item \emph{It starts at $0$ at time $0$: $r_\mu(0) = 0$.}
	\item \emph{It then follows a Brownian motion with drift $a/4$  reflected downwards at $0$ (built from $W$)
\begin{align}
	r^+:= W(t) + at/4 -  \sup_{s \leqslant t} \big(W(s) +as/4\big)\vee 0\,
\end{align}
until its first hitting time of the critical line $c_\mu(t)$ from (equation criticalline of the paper).}
\item \emph{If it reaches $t \mapsto c_\mu(t)$ in a finite time, it immediately restarts at $0$ at this time, then follows a reflected downwards at $0$ Brownian motion with another drift $-(a+1)/4$ (also built from $W$) 
\begin{align}
	r^-:= W(t) - (a+1)t/4 -  \sup_{s \leqslant t} \big(W(s) - (a+1)s/4\big)\vee 0\
\end{align}
until its first hitting time of the critical line $c_\mu(t)$ from (equation criticalline of the paper).}
\end{itemize}
\emph{And so on, $r_\mu$ alternating between $r^+$ and $r^-$ each time it hits $t \mapsto c_\mu(t)$ and restarts at $0$.
Note that the probability that $r^+$ reaches the critical line decreases with time. On the other hand, since $a>0$, $r^-$ almost surely hits the critical line in a finite time.
}","status":"unverified"}

