{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.581874+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e782bd2fbc6519f57c0685f02d0a31418b31d942a377e56cd97adc536752ff77","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Quest of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] It would be interesting to study $\dim \Lambda$ and $\dim \mu_\infty$ in the presence of a drift: assume that there is a drift of strength $\lambda \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ away from the origin. If $\lambda>-1/2$, $\mu_\infty$ {should} still be supported on the boundary. For $\lambda <-1/2$, we {should} almost surely have that $\mu_\infty$ is a Dirac mass at the origin, and for $\lambda=-1/2$, $\mu_t$ {should} not converge. 

    If we were to consider drift away from a point $\zeta\in \partial\ensuremath{\mathbb{D}}$, the analysis becomes easy. By isometry, we can choose $\zeta =1${,} which corresponds to the unique boundary point $\infty$ at infinity in $\partial \ensuremath{\mathbb{H}}$. Geodesics going through $\infty$ in $\ensuremath{\mathbb{H}}$ are straight vertical lines. This means drift away from $\infty$ is a simple vertical drift of $\lambda$ (weighted by the hyperbolic metric). This means that {the} calculations in this work and in [lalley_hyperbolic_1997] still apply and we should get for $\lambda>-1/2$,
    \begin{equation*}
    \dim  \mu_\infty(\lambda) = [2\beta/(2\lambda+1)]\wedge 1,
    \end{equation*}
    and
    \begin{equation*}
        \dim \Lambda(\lambda) =
        \begin{cases}
            \frac{1}{2}\left(1+2\lambda - \sqrt{(1+2\lambda)^2-8\beta}\right) \ &\text{for }\beta \leq \frac{(1+2\lambda)^2}{8}, \\
            1 & \text{else}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    We believe the same expressions should still hold for drift away from the origin rather than from the boundary. The reason for this is that for $z\in \ensuremath{\mathbb{D}}$ we can replace drift away from the origin with drift away from $-\frac{z}{\vert z\vert}$. If we start a hyperbolic Brownian motion $Z_t$ at $z$ where $z$ is far away from the origin, then $-\frac{Z_t}{\vert Z_t\vert}$ should not vary much, hence we can replace drift away from the origin with drift away from a boundary point. (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.714215+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7840c023bfef6eae61e4c9c38d55c4cfe9741578e12762d9f3959b078046f7e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Step3 of arXiv:2411.18775, 'Limit Theorems for the Dynamical Foundation of the Fractional Brownian Motion and Related '] Under assumptions of Theorem~(Thm of the paper)~(i), we have $\mathbb{P}$-almost surely 
