=c_{\beta,n,u}(1-\gamma)^u n^{-\beta};\\ &{\mathbb P}\big(\ell(\infty,0)>\beta\gamma^{-1}\log t+u\big)=t^{-\beta}e^{-\gamma u}.    
\end{aligned}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.891141+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b43353227c30e4ba05f14e5376d9ed2360cdc333191904cec565e0f8cfe963","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2506.16873, 'Optimal matchings of randomly perturbed lattices'] Fix $d=1$ and $\alpha\in(0,1)$. Suppose that the distribution of the perturbation $\xi$ is symmetric around the origin and satisfies 
    \begin{equation}
       cr^{-\alpha } \le  \mathbb P (|\xi | \ge r) \le Cr^{-\alpha }
    \end{equation}
    for two constants $C,c>0$ and all $r \ge 1$. Then:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item 
        There is a perfect matching $M:\mathbb Z \to \Pi$ such that $$\mathbb P (|M(0)|>r)\le Cr^{-\frac{1+\alpha }{2}} \, .$$
        \item 
        Any invariant perfect matching $M:\mathbb Z \to \Pi $ has $$\E \big[ |M(0)|^{\frac{1+\alpha}{2}} \big] = \infty \, .$$
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.227648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b80c72887098ea5d2622e2578fef4edbce869a180ced32b631a4ce8d899095","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Bramson-converges-to-1 of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] \footnote{This claim can also be verified in the modern language of extremal point processes $\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_\infty^\lambda$. Notably, the underlying reason for (equation other-main-input-from-Bramson of the paper) to hold is that the number of particles of $\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_\infty^\lambda$ at a bounded distance from $x$ blows up as $x\to -\infty$: it grows like $ e^{-\mu x}$ for $\lambda >\sqrt{2}$ and $\approx (-x)e^{-\sqrt{2}x}$ for $\lambda = \sqrt{2}$.}
    Let $\phi:\mathbb{R}\to [0,1]$ be a measurable function such that $\phi(x) \geq \eta$ for all $x\in (a,b)$ \textcolor{black}{with} some $a,b \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\eta >0$. Let $u_\phi(t,x)$ be the solution to F-KPP equation with initial condition $\phi$. Then, for any $\delta >0$, as $t \to \infty$,
\begin{equation}
u_\phi\left(t, x\right) \to 1
\end{equation}
uniformly in $x$ such that $|x| \le \sqrt{2}t - \bigl(\frac{3}{2\sqrt{2}} + \delta\bigr)\log t$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:11:44.404783+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7ba0ee4780029da9dbfc79bfe7cd38941f714dd1d800f484e92db1ffef6e1c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2609.03187, 'Duality for a Martingale Transport Problem with Moment Constraints'] Let $c=(c_1,\dots,c_N) \in \mathbb R^N$. Then $c\in\operatorname{int}\mathcal C$
