{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.741122+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8c66e2976bf8f5963e50f488a62da1588ce4f66f84a2013048e5d68a48e099b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma prop:cardinality_increasing_dags of arXiv:2606.09386, 'High-degree vertices in uniform recursive directed acyclic graphs with freezing'] Fix a choice sequence $\mathbf x$ such that Assumption~$\hyperref[ass:A]{\mathbf{x}}$(item:geq1 of the paper) is satisfied. For $m,n\in\mathbb{N}$, we have
\begin{equation}
|\mathcal {I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)| =  \prod_{\substack{2\leq i\leq n \\ x_i=1}} {A_{i-1} \choose m\wedge A_{i-1}} \prod_{\substack{2\leq i\leq n \\ x_i=-1}} A_{i-1},
\end{equation}
and the $\mathrm{URD}$ $G^{(n)}$ with choice sequence $\mathbf x$ is a uniform element of $\mathcal {I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)$.
\proof
In the construction of an element of $\mathcal {I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)$, for each step $i\in\mathcal{A}_n\setminus\{1\}$, there are $A_{i-1} \choose m\wedge A_{i-1}$ possible choices of active vertices to which the newly added vertex can be connected. Additionally, in each step $j\in\mathcal{F}_n$, we freeze exactly one of the $A_{j-1}$ active vertices. As the second label entry for frozen vertices is their freezing time (rather than the default value $a$ for active vertices), each frozen vertex's freezing time is uniquely identifiable in the final graph. Hence,
\begin{equation}
|\mathcal{I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)|= \prod_{\substack{2\leq i\leq n \\ x_i=1}} {A_{i-1} \choose m\wedge A_{i-1}} \prod_{\substack{2\leq j\leq n \\ x_j=-1}} A_{j-1}. 
\end{equation}
Since for a URD, independently at each step $i$, if $x_i=1$, the choices of the distinct vertices are uniform among the $A_{i-1}$ active vertices, and if $x_i=-1$, a uniform random vertex among the $A_{i-1}$ active vertices is frozen, a URD is a uniform element of $\mathcal{I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)$.\qed (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.671109+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8c8858ecee7162de5fd52d97bf6ff20c300ababb7782f0f73a65bf199f574b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 (Quantitative estimates for one block) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] The constants in Theorem~\ref{thm:one-block-contraction} can be chosen together
