{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.875730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8e6df4b064081cd4c5d9939cd2414f147dc5f2542ff6dfdffa27f3f6cb937f4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Lnetw of arXiv:2608.27939, 'Entropy and domination for quasi-Hitchin representations'] The matrix $M(t)$ in (equation eq:Tmat of the paper) has a decomposition \begin{equation} 
 M(t)
 =
 X_{i_1}(t)\cdot X_{i_2}(t)
 \cdots
 X_{i_N}(t) \cdot 
 D(t) 
\end{equation}
where $D(t)$ is a diagonal matrix with each entry a positive Laurent monomial in the $t$-parameters, and each $X_i(t)$ is the triangular matrix $I + m_iE_{i+1,i}$ where $m_i$ is a positive Laurent monomial in the triangle $t$-parameters.

Moreover, the unipotent matrix $L_\rho(P)$ for (equation eq:Lfactor of the paper) is obtained by conjugating $M(t)\cdot D(t)^{-1}$ by a diagonal matrix involving the shear parameters $e:=e_\rho(x,P)$. In particular, it is the weight matrix of a transparent weighted planar network whose non-constant edge-weights are positive Laurent monomials in $t$ and $e$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.598951+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8ed02a8dec5c3b0ff357d91531c6eeb48b016c9c2badab909246ec0dcf0f8df","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:pointwise of arXiv:2608.11931, '\texorpdfstring{Nonlinear parabolic characterizations of stochastic completeness at infini'] Let $T>0$ and $u_0 \colon X\to\mathbb R$.  A \emph{pointwise solution of the (eq:GPME of the paper)
on $[0,T]\times X$ with initial datum $u_0$}
is a function $u \colon [0,T]\times X\to\mathbb R$ such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
    \item[\textup{(a)}] $u(t,\cdot)\in\mathcal{F}_\Phi$ for every $t\in(0,T)$;
    \item[\textup{(b)}] $u(\cdot,x)\in C([0,T])\cap C^1((0,T))$ for every $x\in X$;
    \item[\textup{(c)}] $(\partial_t +\Delta\Phi)u(t,x)=0$ for every $(t,x)\in(0,T)\times X$;
    \item[\textup{(d)}] $u(0,x)=u_0(x)$ for every $x\in X$.
\end{enumerate}
The solution $u$ is a \emph{bounded pointwise solution} if $u$ is bounded.","status":"unverified"}

