{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.036306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e9218693c25ddea8b8201c498b163cdecab7df6a2272c7de598b053d266d5432","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:gPAM-reg of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Let $\rho_\varepsilon$ be a smooth spatial mollifier at scale $\varepsilon > 0$ and set $\xi_\varepsilon = \xi * \rho_\varepsilon$. The \emph{renormalised mollified gPAM} is
\begin{equation}
  \partial_t u^\varepsilon = \Delta u^\varepsilon + f(u^\varepsilon)\xi_\varepsilon - C_\varepsilon f'(u^\varepsilon)f(u^\varepsilon), \qquad u^\varepsilon(0) = u_0 \in C(\mathbb{T}^2),
\end{equation}
where $C_\varepsilon := \mathbb{E}\bigl[(K * \xi_\varepsilon)(z)\,\xi_\varepsilon(z)\bigr]$, independent of $z$ by stationarity, is the renormalisation constant, $K$ being a compactly supported truncation of the heat kernel as in [hairer2014theory] (different truncations change $C_\varepsilon$ by $O(1)$); one has $C_\varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2\pi}\log(1/\varepsilon) + O(1)$.\footnote{The coefficient of the counterterm is fixed by a single Wick contraction. Along the Picard expansion of (equation eq:gPAM-reg of the paper), the divergent contribution to $f(u^\varepsilon)\xi_\varepsilon$ is $f'(u^\varepsilon)f(u^\varepsilon)\,(K*\xi_\varepsilon)\,\xi_\varepsilon$, whose expectation is precisely $C_\varepsilon$, and the centred product $(K*\xi_\varepsilon)\,\xi_\varepsilon - C_\varepsilon$ converges in probability in a Hölder space of negative regularity. Displayed normalisations of the renormalisation constants vary across the literature; the invariant statement is that the drift correction $-C_\varepsilon\,f'(u^\varepsilon)f(u^\varepsilon)$ with $C_\varepsilon = \mathbb{E}[(K*\xi_\varepsilon)\xi_\varepsilon]$ yields a mollifier-independent limit, the $\rho$-dependent finite part of $C_\varepsilon$ being exactly the mollifier-dependent adjustment required in [hairer2014theory] for mollifier-independence.} Alongside (equation eq:gPAM-reg of the paper) we introduce its \emph{white-in-time companion model} on the Gelfand triple $V = H^1(\mathbb{T}^2) \hookrightarrow H = L^2(\mathbb{T}^2) \hookrightarrow V^* = H^{-1}(\mathbb{T}^2)$, obtained by replacing the time-independent noise $\xi_\varepsilon$ by white-in-time noise smoothed by the same mollifier:
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{d}u^\varepsilon(t) = \bigl[\Delta u^\varepsilon(t) - C_\varepsilon f'(u^\varepsilon(t))f(u^\varepsilon(t))\bigr]\,\mathrm{d}t + f(u^\varepsilon(t))\,\rho_\varepsilon * \mathrm{d}W(t),
\end{equation}
where $W$ is a cylindrical Wiener process on $U = L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)$. The two equations are distinct. The noise $\xi_\varepsilon$ of (equation eq:gPAM-reg of the paper) is constant in time, whereas $\rho_\varepsilon * \dot W$ is white in time, so no identity of the form $\xi_\varepsilon\,\mathrm{d}t = \rho_\varepsilon * \mathrm{d}W$ holds. The companion model (equation eq:gPAM-variational of the paper) is a well-posed variational SPDE at each fixed $\varepsilon > 0$ and serves below as the variational-subcase representative for the operator-level analysis; no claim is made about its $\varepsilon \downarrow 0$ limit (the two-dimensional multiplicative heat equation with space-time white noise is critical, outside the subcritical scope of [brunedchandrachevyrevhairer2021]). The renormalised solution $u$ of (equation eq:gPAM of the paper) is the limit of the solutions $u^\varepsilon$ of the genuinely mollified equation (equation eq:gPAM-reg of the paper) as $\varepsilon \downarrow 0$, for initial data $u_0 \in \mathcal{C}^\alpha(\mathbb{T}^2)$ with $\alpha \in (1/2, 1)$, in the sense of [hairer2014theory] (convergence in probability of the processes stopped at a large $\mathcal{C}^\alpha$-norm cutoff, for each finite time horizon) and, equivalently, as a special case of [brunedchandrachevyrevhairer2021] (spatial and space-time mollification coincide for the time-independent noise $\xi$; see the discussion of approximation families above); the scalar reduction applies to this family through the time-independent-noise clause of Remark~(rem:abstract-gaussian of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.831376+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e9226d15a188d3bf483d6eddd5ad5f910bd87c9f8d1ee5efbc0f0353c49a53ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:correlation of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] There are some $\lambda_0 > 1$ and $C, \gamma > 0$ such that for all $\lambda \geqslant \lambda_0$
    and all $i, j \in \mathbb{N}$,
    if $\mathbf{X}$ denotes the $\lambda$-tilted stationary line ensemble
    then for all $t > 0$, we have
    \begin{equation}

        0 \leqslant \operatorname{Cov}[X^i(0), X^j(t)] \leqslant C e^{-\gamma t} \lambda^{-(i+j-2)/3}.
    \end{equation}
    The same bound holds if $\mathbf{X}$ is replaced by the stationary
