{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.320426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e93082731857082425e98fddbce2a35232ec4f42ffcb7b2fd4d1218a016e114b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop univ-prop-*-cc of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let ${\mathcal{C}}$ be a cocomplete $\infty$-category with a terminal object. Then $\textup{fgt}\colon{\mathcal{C}}_*\to{\mathcal{C}}$ admits a left adjoint $(-)_+$, and this enjoys the following universal property: for any pointed cocomplete ${\mathcal{D}}$, restriction along $(-)_+$ defines an equivalence
    \[
        {\textup{Fun}}^\textup{cc}({\mathcal{C}}_*,{\mathcal{D}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}{\textup{Fun}}^\textup{cc}({\mathcal{C}},{\mathcal{D}}).
    \]
    \begin{proof}
        It is clear that the forgetful functor admits a left adjoint, given by taking coproduct with the terminal object. To complete the proof, it will then suffice to show that restriction along $(-)_+$ defines a bijection
        \begin{equation}
            \mathop{\pi_0}{\textup{Hom}}_{\CAT_\infty^\text{cc}}({\mathcal{C}}_*,{\mathcal{D}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}\mathop{\pi_0}{\textup{Hom}}_{\CAT_\infty^\text{cc}}({\mathcal{C}},{\mathcal{D}});
        \end{equation}
        the corresponding equivalence of functor categories will then follow by replacing ${\mathcal{D}}$ by ${\textup{Fun}}({\mathcal{K}},{\mathcal{D}})$ for varying $\infty$-categories ${\mathcal{K}}$.

        To prove that $((eq:restr-disj-bp of the paper))$ is injective, let us define ${\textup{Sq}^\textup{cof}}({\mathcal{C}})\subset{\textup{Fun}}([1]\times[1],{\mathcal{C}})$ as the full subcategory spanned by all pushout squares of the form
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                A\arrow[r]\arrow[d]\arrow[dr,"\ulcorner"{very near end},phantom]& B\arrow[d]\\
                1 \arrow[r] & C
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        and similarly define ${\textup{Sq}^\textup{cof}}({\mathcal{D}})$. Note that the functors ${\textup{Sq}^\textup{cof}}({\mathcal{C}})\to{\mathcal{C}}$ and ${\textup{Sq}^\textup{cof}}({\mathcal{D}})\to{\mathcal{D}}$ restricting to the top row are equivalences. 

        If we view the counit $\varepsilon$ of the adjunction $(-)_+\dashv\textup{fgt}$ as a functor ${\mathcal{C}}\to\Ar({\mathcal{C}})$, apply $\Ar$ on both sides, and restrict along ${\mathcal{C}}_*\hookrightarrow\Ar({\mathcal{C}})$, we obtain a functor ${\mathcal{C}}_*\to{\textup{Fun}}([1]\times[1],{\mathcal{C}})$ sending $1\to X$ to the naturality square
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{tikzcd}
                1_+\arrow[d,"\varepsilon"']\arrow[r] & X_+\arrow[d,"\varepsilon"]\\
                1\arrow[r] & X\rlap.
            \end{tikzcd}
        \end{equation}
        This functor factors through ${\textup{Sq}^\textup{cof}}({\mathcal{C}})$; postcomposing with $G$ then yields a functor $G'\colon{\mathcal{C}}_*\to{\textup{Sq}^\textup{cof}}({\mathcal{D}})$ such that ${\ev_{1,1}}\circ G'\simeq G$. On the other hand, the restriction of $G'$ to the top row only depends on $G\circ (-)_+$ by construction, so $((eq:restr-disj-bp of the paper))$ is injective.

        For surjectivity, let $F\colon{\mathcal{C}}\to{\mathcal{D}}$ arbitrary and define $G\colon{\mathcal{C}}_*\to{\mathcal{D}}$ as the composite
        \[
            {\mathcal{C}}_*={\mathcal{C}}_{1/}\xrightarrow{\;F\;}{\mathcal{D}}_{F(1)/}\xrightarrow{\;p_!\;}{\mathcal{D}}_{0/}\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}{\mathcal{D}},
        \]
        where $p_!$ is left adjoint to precomposition with $p\colon F(1)\to 0$, i.e.\ it is given by taking cofibers. This composite is then clearly cocontinuous. We now consider the diagram
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                {\mathcal{C}}\arrow[r,"(-)_+"]\arrow[d,"F"'] & {\mathcal{C}}_{1/}\arrow[d,"F"]\\
                {\mathcal{D}}\arrow[r, "i_!"'] & {\mathcal{D}}_{F(1)/}\arrow[r,"p_!"'] & {\mathcal{D}}_{0/}
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        where the bottom left arrow is left adjoint to the forgetful functor. The left-hand square commutes as $F$ preserves pushouts (so that $\Ar(F)\colon\Ar({\mathcal{C}})\to\Ar (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.036696+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e931a52cb7f8a8f97e873b5093f531dc9c992dd2f558f8465fd60a067abd7f48","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2605.03182, 'Exponential integrability of the solution to the stochastic Burgers equation driven by whi'] The semigroup $P_t$ is Lipschitz improving, in the sense that for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a constant $L_{T,\varepsilon}>0$ such that, for any $\varphi \in {B}_b(H)$, for every $t\in(0,T]$, and for every $x,x'\in H$ we have
