{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.492799+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e9fb4a1ec8e8e9572f8b138f6ef3b0912d0fb1db9489a8301dca487818cc1673","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:41improJ of arXiv:2607.05054, 'Root Dynamics of Differentiated Polynomials with Rotationally Invariant Structure'] Let $ \nu_0 $ be a probability measure on $ \mathbb{R}_+ $ with compact support contained in 
$[0,A]$ for some $ A>0 $.  
For each $ n\ge 1 $ let $ (r_j^{(n)})_{1\le j\le n} $ be an increasing sequence in 
$ \mathbb{R}_+ $ such that
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^n\delta_{r_j^{(n)}}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{\text{weakly}}\;\nu_0 .
\end{align*}
Let $ (m_n)_{n\ge1} $ be a sequence of positive integers satisfying
\begin{align*}
\frac{m_n}{\log n}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\; \infty .
\end{align*}

Define the polynomial of degree $N_n=n m_n$
\begin{align*}
P_{n,m_n}(z)=\prod_{j=1}^n\bigl(z^{m_n}-(r_j^{(n)})^{m_n}\bigr)
           =\prod_{j=1}^n\prod_{k=0}^{m_n-1}\!\Bigl(z-r_j^{(n)}e^{2\pi i k/m_n}\Bigr).
\end{align*}

For a fixed $t\in(0,1)$ consider the $\lfloor n m_n t\rfloor$-th derivative  
$ P_{n,m_n}^{(\lfloor n m_n t\rfloor)} $.

Then the empirical measure of its roots converges weakly to
\begin{align*}
\mu_t=\nu_t\otimes\mathrm{unif},
\end{align*}
where
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathrm{unif}$ is the uniform probability measure on the unit circle,
\item $\nu_t$ is the probability distribution of the random variable
  \begin{align*}
  \Bigl(1-\frac{t}{V_t}\Bigr)q_{\nu_0}(V_t),
  \end{align*}
  with $ V_t\sim\mathrm{Uniform}[t,1] $ and $ q_{\nu_0} $ the quantile function 
  of $\nu_0$, given by 
  $$q_{\nu_0} (\alpha) = \inf \{ y \geq 0, \nu_0 ([0,y]) \geq \alpha \}.$$
\end{itemize}

Moreover, the quantile function of the sub-probability measure $(1-t)\nu_t$ 
(which has total mass $1-t$) satisfies
\begin{equation*}
q_{(1-t)\nu_t}(x)=\frac{x}{x+t}\,q_{\nu_0}(x+t),\qquad 0\le x\le 1-t.
\end{equation*}

\begin{comment}
If in addition $\nu_0$ is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure on $[0,A]$ and has a continuous, strictly positive density $\psi_0$ on 
$(0,A)$, then the measure $(1-t)\nu_t$ is supported on $[0, A(1-t)]$
the distribution function $\Psi_t$ of $(1-t)\nu_t$ being 

and the density $\psi(t,x)=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\Psi_t(x)$ satisfies

\end{comment} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.888745+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e9fe78fadf719db21d2c903d5bb54a7182f7a13919f6e6ebf395dc6c2098f555","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:scalim of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] We say that $E_\ell$ (or $\phi_\ell$) satisfies a $\mathcal{C}^k$ \emph{scaling bound}, with
\emph{translation invariant limit $K$ (or $F$)}, with \emph{parameters}: $\mathsf P_\ell=(K, \sigma_\ell,\lambda_\ell, \rho'_\ell,\rho_\ell)$ if (eq:scalimsetting of the paper) holds. We say that the scaling bound holds with \emph{effective variance $(\sigma_\ell)_\ell$}, with \emph{effective frequency} $(\lambda_\ell)_\ell$, with \emph{remainder function} $(R_\ell)_\ell$, in the \emph{range} $(\rho'_\ell,\rho_\ell)$, with \emph{error} $\delta_\ell$.
If in addition, we have that
\begin{equation} 
\delta_\ell\to_{\ell \to +\infty} 0,
\end{equation}
then we shall say \emph{scaling limit} instead of scaling bound.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.587839+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e9ffd6c2d403b28241960b7287e40075d8b9df2a2741b1ad6889472a365ae0ad","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn defi_kin_measure of arXiv:2608.30548, 'Finite speed of propagation and waiting time phenomena for stochastic porous media equatio'] A kinetic measure is a mapping $q$ from  $\Omega$  to the space of non-negative, locally finite measures on  $\mathbb{T}^d \times (0,\infty)\times[0,T] $ such that
