{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.780345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea7a801d555f84edc3ee31b9bebd1c541637122d85ceb764cd5fef85618ea4c4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Moment Central Limit Theorem of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] Let $M(t)$ be the stationary GOE processes. Then for $k$ fixed times $t_1,\dots ,t_k$ and exponents $j_1, \dots ,j_k$ the vector 
\begin{equation*}
    \left(n^{-j_1/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_1)^{j_1})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_1)^{j_1})]\right),\dots,n^{-j_2/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_k)^{j_k})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_k)^{j_k})]\right)\right),
\end{equation*}
converges both weakly and in the sense of moments to a Gaussian vector, as $n\to\infty$. Moreover, as a process
\begin{equation*}
    \left(n^{-j_1/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_1})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_1})]\right),\dots,n^{-j_k/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_k})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_k})]\right)\right),
\end{equation*}
converges weakly in $C[0,\infty)$ to a Gaussian process. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.424096+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea7d15b9ba4a4ff4b1e9757a256dfa05af1ac27cc1eea844ceab0ca7093f7286","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 150 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Let $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(1)}$ and $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(2)}$ be two families (indexed by a large parameter $\N$) of discrete random $d$-dimensional real vectors. We say that the distribution of $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(1)}$ is asymptotically equal to the distribution of $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(2)}$ if
\begin{equation}

 \lim_{\N\rightarrow\infty} \sup_{f \in \textnormal{Lip}_1} \big|\E\big[f(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(1)})\big]-\E\big[f(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(2)})\big]\big| =0,
\end{equation}
where $\textnormal{Lip}_1$ is the set of $1$-bounded $1$-Lipschitz functions
\[
 \textnormal{Lip}_1=\big\{f:\amsmathbb{R}^d \rightarrow \amsmathbb{R} \quad \big|\quad \forall \boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y} \in \amsmathbb{R}^d \quad |f(\x)-f(\y)| \leq |\!|\x-\y|\!|_{\infty}\,\,\textnormal{ and }\, |f(\x)|\leq 1\big\}.
\]
If \eqref{eq_asymptotic_equivalence} holds, we write $\chi_{\N}^{(1)} \stackrel{\textnormal{d}}{\sim} \chi_{\N}^{(2)}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.902170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea81724dfe94153affde7bb4e39e9cf91671fb566710483d0656cc57bd1ec48e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma normbound:nullspace of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Assume that the linear measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^m$ satisfies the NSP of order $1$ with constant $0 < \eta_1 < 1$. Then it holds that for any $\mathbf{N} \in \ker(\mathcal{A})$, 
