{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.116849+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea9b9fa66edd8644c38b7ee1b133342236e503e0f93abfe7935410f8dc3992c3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:khasminskii-corollary3-1 of arXiv:2608.16946, 'A diffusion model for time-dependent compositional data'] Let $b(t,x)\in\mathbb{R}^d$ and $\sigma(t,x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}$ be continuous and defined on $[t_0,\infty)\times D$, where
$D$ is an open subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$. Let $A(t,x)=(a_{ij}(t,x))=\sigma(t,x)\sigma(t,x)^T$.
Assume that $D=\bigcup_k D_k$, where $\{D_k\}$ is a non-decreasing sequence
of open sets, with the closure of each $D_k$ contained in $D$, and that for each $k=1,2,\dots$, the
following conditions hold:
\begin{equation*}
|b(t,x)-b(t,y)|+\sum_{i,j}|\sigma_{ij}(t,x)-\sigma_{ij}(t,y)|\leq M_k |x-y| \\
\tag{Condition 1}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
|b(t,x)|+\sum_{i,j}|\sigma_{ij}(t,x)|\leq M_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(1+|x|\aftergroup\egroup\originalright),
\tag{Condition 2}
\end{equation*}
where the bound $M_k>0$ depends only on $k$. Assume also that there exists a function
$V(t,x)$ defined on $[t_0,\infty)\times D$ that is continuously differentiable w.r.t.\ $t$ and twice continuously
differentiable w.r.t.\ $x$, satisfying the conditions:
\begin{equation*}
LV \le cV,
\tag{Condition 3}
\end{equation*}
for some constant $c>0$, where $L$ is the differential operator defined by
$$
LV(t,x) = \frac{\partial V(t,x)}{\partial t} + 
\sum_i b_i(t,x)\frac{\partial V(t,x)}{\partial x_i} +
\frac{1}{2} \sum_{ij}a_{ij}(t,x)\frac{\partial^2 V(t,x)}{\partial x_i \partial x_j};
$$
and
\begin{equation*}
\inf_{t>0,x \in D\setminus D_k} V(t,x) \to \infty \hspace{20pt} as \hspace{5pt} k \to \infty.
\tag{Condition 4}
\end{equation*}
Then, given an instance $W(t)$ of standard $d$-dimensional Brownian motion, for every random
variable $X(t_0)$ with values almost surely in $D$, independent from $W(t)$, there exists a
strong solution $X(t)$, $t\geq t_0$, for the equation
$$
dX(t) = b(t,X)dt + \sigma(t,X)dW(t)
$$
that is Markovian, almost surely continuous, and satisfies
$$
P[X(t)\in D \textrm{ for all $t\geq t_0$}]=1.
$$ \qed","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.373064+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea9f7b10656ae77dbe87a72cd73fb3ad62de3789a7db993a79434e780a3c5e7e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 (Tightness criterion check for summable covariance) of arXiv:2606.04179, 'Functional Scaling Limits of Interpolated Correlated Random Walks in Hölder Topology'] Assume that \(\rho\) satisfies the summability assumption of Theorem \ref{thm: functional Breuer-Major}.
