{"assumptions":["M is a compact Riemannian manifold; f is a C^{1+a} diffeomorphism of M with a > 0 (Holder derivative)","mu is an f-invariant Borel probability measure","the conclusion is stated at mu-almost every point having at least one negative Lyapunov exponent","eps > 0 is fixed small relative to the largest negative exponent; C(x) and r(x) are measurable, r tempered","no claim is made at points with only non-negative exponents, in zero-exponent directions, or about absolute continuity of the lamination"],"created_at":"2026-09-12T19:09:43.732688+00:00","domain":"smooth ergodic theory, non-uniform hyperbolicity, Pesin theory","id":"claim_eabc8d2a11c5f96e9cd3d9d0ae8e4274abc626af17b4f59d7b9ce92e1510c03b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let M be a compact Riemannian manifold and let f be a C^{1+a} diffeomorphism of M for some a > 0 (that is, Df is Holder continuous with exponent a). Let mu be an f-invariant Borel probability measure, and for mu-a.e. x let lambda_1(x) > ... > lambda_{k(x)}(x) be the Lyapunov exponents with the Oseledets splitting. Write E^s(x) for the sum of the Oseledets subspaces with NEGATIVE exponent, d_s(x) = dim E^s(x), and lambda^s(x) = the largest negative exponent at x. Fix a small eps > 0.

Then for mu-a.e. x with d_s(x) > 0 there exist a measurable function r(x) > 0 and an embedded C^{1+a} disk W^s_loc(x) of dimension d_s(x), contained in the ball of radius r(x) around x and TANGENT TO E^s(x) at x, such that:
 (i) for all y in W^s_loc(x) and all n >= 0,  d(f^n x, f^n y) <= C(x) e^{(lambda^s(x) + eps) n} d(x, y), with C(x) measurable and finite; in particular limsup (1/n) log d(f^n x, f^n y) <= lambda^s(x) < 0;
 (ii) f( W^s_loc(x) ) is contained in W^s_loc(f x);
 (iii) x -> W^s_loc(x) is measurable, and the size is TEMPERED: r(f^n x) >= r(x) e^{-eps |n|} for all n, so the disks may shrink along the orbit but only subexponentially;
 (iv) on each Pesin block Lambda_l the constants C, r and the estimates are uniform and the disks vary continuously in the C^1 topology with the base point.
The global stable manifold W^s(x), defined as the increasing union over n >= 0 of f^{-n}( W^s_loc(f^n x) ), is then an injectively immersed C^{1+a} submanifold consisting of points whose forward orbit converges exponentially to that of x. The unstable statement follows by applying this to f^{-1}.

SHARP EDGES. (1) The Holder hypothesis on Df is NOT cosmetic: the theorem is false for merely C^1 diffeomorphisms -- Pugh constructed a C^1 diffeomorphism (in dimension 4) with non-zero exponents for which the conclusion fails. The Holder exponent is what makes the nonlinear remainder in Lyapunov charts small enough to run the graph transform against a chart size that shrinks subexponentially. (2) r(x) CANNOT be taken uniformly positive: it is only tempered, and any argument that needs a uniform size must work on a Pesin block and pay for the fact that the block is not invariant. (3) Nothing is asserted at points where all exponents are >= 0, or in directions with ZERO exponent. (4) The family of stable manifolds is measurable, not continuous, on the whole space; continuity holds only along Pesin blocks. (5) Absolute continuity of the stable lamination (needed for Pesin's entropy formula and for ergodic components) is a FURTHER theorem, also requiring C^{1+a}; it is not part of this statement and must be cited separately.","status":"unverified"}

