{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.783681+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaee64333788079387af770ac54d55291d2ac55891c8e701cc411c313c7a9c08","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 10 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $G\in \mathbb C^{n\times n} (s,p)$ be a rational  matrix  function. Then the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]
\item $G$ has no singularities in $\mathbb{GH}$, the matrix $G(s,p)$ has nonnegative real part
for any $(s,p)\in \mathbb{GH}$,
and $\lim_{(s,p)\to\infty} G(s,p)$ exists;
\item $G$ admits a realization of the form
\begin{equation} 
G(s,p)=  D+  \frac12{C} \left(s I+ 2A -
    (s^2-4p) P(s I+ 2A)^{-1} P\right)^{-1}B.
\end{equation}
where the block matrix $\matr{A&B\\C&D} \in\Cmats{(k+n)}{(k+n)}$ is such that
\begin{equation*}
\matr{
A+A^* & C^*- B \\ C- B^* & D+D^*
} \geq 0
\end{equation*}
and $-I\le P \le I$.
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, if $G\in {\mathbb R}^{n\times n}(s,p)$, then the matrices $A,B,C,D$ and $P$ can be chosen as real.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.759219+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaf3db2fa8dabe1cdeddd253e4f0575ed1a7305bc5da6e3887ab41f9983d4d94","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 3 of arXiv:2604.12233, 'An upper bound on the smallest singular value of dense random combinatorial matrices'] Let  $\cH$ be an         $n$-dimensional Hilbert space. 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For any invertible $n\times n$ matrix $A$, the system $\{Ae_k, (A^{-1})^Te_k\}_{k=1}^n$ forms a  biorthogonal system in $\R^n$. 
        \item Given a  linearly independent set of vectors $\{E_k\}_{k=1}^n$ in an 
        $n$-dimensional Hilbert space $\cH$, there exist unique vectors $\{F_k\}_{k=1}^n$ such that $\{E_k, F_k\}_{k=1}^n$ is a  biorthogonal system in $\cH$. 
        \item For a  biorthogonal system $\{E_k, F_k\}_{k=1}^n$ in an 
        $n$-dimensional Hilbert space $\cH$, the Euclidean norm of each $F_k$ satisfies
        \begin{equation}
            \|F_k\|_2=\frac{1}{\mbox{dist}(E_k, H_k)}, \qquad k=1, \dots, n,
        \end{equation}
        where $H_k=\mbox{span}\{E_i\}_{i\neq k}$ denotes the subspace spanned by all $E_i$ except $E_k$, and $\operatorname{dist}(E_k, H_k)$ is the Euclidean distance from $E_k$ to $H_k$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.981889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaf4282e7dfd2ecc38da8adb286e4e56c1b7dcd9d10075ad954147a333a01b5c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:derivative_stability of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] The estimate
