{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.811271+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb01fb112e1c64c0f258888731014a791bb9122e234aefa651d38f2051e2e994","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn d:dl of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] The \emph{directed landscape} $\mathcal{L}$ is the unique $C(\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow})$-valued random variable satisfying the following properties. 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item 
    For any finite collection of disjoint time intervals $\{(s_i,t_i)\}_{i=1,\dots,k}$, the random functions $\{\mathcal{L}(s_i,{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}}; t_i, {\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}})\}_{i=1,\dots, k}$ are independent.
    \item 
    Almost surely, for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ and all $r\in (s,t)$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{L}(s,x;t,y) = \sup_{z\in \mathbb{R}}\left(\mathcal{L}(s,x;r,z) + \mathcal{L}(r,z;t,y)\right).
    \end{align*}
    \item 
    For all $s<t$, the random function $(x,y)\mapsto \mathcal{L}(s,x;t,y)$ has the same law as $(x,y)\mapsto (t-s)^{1/3}\mathcal{L}(0,(t-s)^{-2/3}x;1,(t-s)^{-2/3}y)$.
    \item $\mathcal{L}(0,{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}};1,{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}})$ is distributed as the Airy sheet (see [dov]).
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.244846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb02856c08a965d5ea63d769e574910ea4104edac38bd8fa3804915580d9dbb5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm restate of pnkt and nu of arXiv:2604.23890, 'The Cutoff Profile for Random Transpositions on Repeated Cards in the Full Range of Parame'] Let $c\in\mathbb{R}$ be an absolute constant. Let $(n_N)_{N\ge 1},(m_N)_{N\ge 1}$ and $(l_N)_{N\ge 1}$ be sequences of positive integers, such that 
\begin{enumerate}
\item $m_N,l_N\ge 2$ for all $N\ge 1$.
\item $n_N=m_Nl_N$ for all $N\ge 1$, and $\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}n_N=\infty$.
\end{enumerate}
For all $N\ge 1$, let $t_N=\frac{n_N}{2}(\log n_N-\frac 12\log l_N+c)$. Let $P_{n_N}^{*t_N}$ be defined as in (defi: Pnk and its convolution of the paper), and $\nu_{n_N}^{t_N}$ be defined as in (defi: construction of nu of the paper). Let $\sim_{l_N}\backslash P_{n_N}^{*t_N},\sim_{l_N}\backslash \nu_{n_N}^{t_N}$ be their induced probability measures over $\sim_{l_N}\backslash\mathfrak{S}_{n_N}$. Then, we have
$$\limsup_{N\rightarrow\infty}\log n_N(d_{TV}(\sim_{l_N}\backslash P_{n_N}^{*t_N},\sim_{l_N}\backslash\nu_{n_N}^{t_N}))\le -1.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

