{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.102841+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb19f55a9a4323fafa0238312d477254932cea9d1c7bcd2f1372b9eeb479a9c9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem THM51 of arXiv:2412.08886, 'On domination for (non-symmetric) Dirichlet forms'] Let $({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$ be a quasi-regular Dirichlet form on $L^2(E,m)$, and let $({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$ be another Dirichlet form on $L^2(E,m)$,  not necessarily quasi-regular, such that ${\mathscr E}'\preceq {\mathscr E}$.  Denote by $(\tilde{{\mathscr E}},\tilde{{\mathscr F}})$ the unique Dirichlet form obtained in Theorem~(THM24 of the paper). Then, there exists a locally compact separable metric space $\widehat{E}$ and a measurable map $j:E\rightarrow \widehat{E}$ such that,  by defining $\widehat{m}:=m\circ j^{-1}$,  the operator $j^*$ is a unitary map from $L^2(\widehat{E},\widehat{m})$ onto $L^2(E,m)$,  and the following hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] The image Dirichlet form $(\widehat{{\mathscr E}},\widehat{{\mathscr F}}):=j({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$ is a regular Dirichlet form on $L^2(\widehat{E},\widehat{m})$;
\item[(ii)] There exists an $\widehat{{\mathscr E}}$-quasi-open subset $\widehat{G}$ of $\widehat{E}$ with $\widehat{m}(\widehat{E}\setminus \widehat{G})=0$ such that $j$ is a quasi-homeomorphism that maps $(\tilde{{\mathscr E}},\tilde{{\mathscr F}})$ to $j(\tilde{{\mathscr E}},\tilde{{\mathscr F}})=(\widehat{{\mathscr E}}^{\widehat{G}},\widehat{{\mathscr F}}^{\widehat{G}})$,  the part Dirichlet form of $(\widehat{{\mathscr E}},\widehat{{\mathscr F}})$ on $\widehat{G}$;
\item[(iii)] $j$ is a quasi-homeomophism that maps $({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$ to the quasi-regular image Dirichlet form $j({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$ on $L^2(\widehat{E},\widehat{m})$.  
\end{itemize}
Furthermore,  there exists a Hunt process $\widehat{X}$ on $\widehat{E}$, with $\widehat{G}$ being taken as a finely open set with respect to $\widehat{X}$, and an MF $\widehat M\in \text{MF}_+(\widehat{X}^{\widehat{G}})$,  where $\widehat{X}^{\widehat{G}}$ is the part process of $\widehat{X}$ on $\widehat{G}$, such that $\widehat{X}$ is properly associated with $j({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$,  $\widehat{X}^{\widehat{G}}$ is properly associated with $j(\tilde{{\mathscr E}},\tilde{{\mathscr F}})$, and the subprocess $(\widehat{X}^{\widehat{G}},\widehat M)$ is properly associated with $j({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.873245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb1aa09e8ad36136540172871cbdf716ba755231eaccd661783b0b63b92617fc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 36 (Algebra representations) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Let $\pi:\cA\to\cL(H)$ be an algebra representation, and let
