{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.781089+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb2a0c680e4f5d4e36848e7408a2f44ea358a0ab0bfa6c872d3a2f967cfc8791","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] We define a time--dependent $\beta$-stochastic operator formally expressed as
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{H}_{\beta}(t) := -\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+x+\frac{2}{\sqrt{\beta}}\xi_1(t).
    \end{equation}
    More precisely, for $f \in H^{1,1}_{0}(0,\infty):= \{u \in L^2(0,\infty): u',xu \in L^2(0,\infty), u(0)=0\}$, define $\mathcal{H}_{\beta}(t)f$ as the distribution such that for any $g \in C_0^{\infty}(0,\infty)$,
    \begin{equation}
        \langle \mathcal{H}_{\beta}(t) f,  g \rangle := \int_{0}^{\infty} (f'(x) g'(x)+xf(x)g(x))dx+\frac{2}{\sqrt{\beta}}\int_{0}^{\infty} f(x)g(x) \xi_1(t)[dx],
    \end{equation}
    with $\int_{0}^{\infty} f(x)g(x) \xi_1(t)[dx] = \langle \xi_1(t), f(x)g(x) \rangle$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.596108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb2ab28bd40618213819960fd8e4c0b40bd75b4a0fc113d3ae0885ecaee05892","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2411.09988, 'A Study in Markov Chains, Loop-Erased Random Walk and Loop Soups'] If $\omega = [\omega_0, \omega_1, \ldots, \omega_n]$ is a path, then its \textit{(chronological) loop erasure}, denoted by $LE(\omega)$, is the SAW $\eta = [\eta_0, \eta_1, \ldots, \eta_k]$ defined as follows.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Set $\eta_0: = \omega_0$.
        \item If $\omega_n = \omega_0$, we set $k=0$ and terminate; otherwise, let $\eta_1$ be the first vertex in $\omega$ after the last visit to $\omega_0$, that is, $\eta_1: = \omega_{i+1}$, where $i: = max\{j; \omega_j = \omega_0\}$.
        \item If $\omega_n = \eta_1$, then we set $k=1$ and terminate; otherwise, let $\eta_2$ be the first vertex in $\omega$ after the last visit to $\eta_1$, and so on.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.816337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb2b644cd94fbb04994d22be1e9956d6f5c37cbbbf9e625de119fe5d08ea5e42","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:informal of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots ,  \mathbf{X} _n$ be symmetric matrices with eigenvalues in $[0,1]$ and common conditional mean $ \mathbf{M}  =  \mathbb E [  \mathbf{X} _i |  \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _{i-1} ]$. 
    For an appropriate weighted average $\hat  \mathbf{M} _n$ of $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ and an appropriate sample variance proxy $v_{n,\alpha} =v(\alpha,  \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n) > 0$,
    \begin{equation}
     \mathbb P \left( \lambda_{\max}(\hat \mathbf{M} _n -   \mathbf{M} ) \geqslant \sqrt{\frac{2 \log(d/\alpha) v_{n,\alpha}} {n}}   \right) \leqslant \alpha.
\end{equation}

    Further, if $\{ \mathbf{X} _i\}$ have a common conditional variance $ \mathbf{V} $, $v_{n,\alpha}$ converges almost surely to 
    $\| \mathbf{V}  \|$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.062366+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb2cd1d6094b9fc90858661a259a137d2299149da811ada9e9fd3458c5683878","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $\Omega = \{0, 1\}^E$ with $E$ at most countable. For $\omega \in \Omega$ and $F, G \subset E$ such that $F \cap G = \emptyset$, we denote
  \begin{equation}
    \omega^F_G : e \in E \mapsto \begin{cases}
      1 & \text{ if } e \in F \\
      0 & \text{ if } e \in G \\
      \omega(e) & \text{ otherwise}
    \end{cases}
  \end{equation}
  If $F$ or $G$ is empty, we omit it. If $F$ or $G$ is a singleton $\{e\}$, we write $e$ instead of $\{e\}$\footnote{We always write $\omega(e)$ for the actual value of the $e$-component of $\omega$ in order to avoid confusion.}.
  Consider an increasing event $A \subset \Omega$. We define the event that $e \in E$ is pivotal for $A$ to be
  \begin{equation}
    \{\omega \in \Omega: (\omega_e \notin A) \wedge (\omega^e \in A)\}.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.162017+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb309a5d29c39a9b91348628b934a1914e6cec16e91366a537681e32aebd781f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def_FamilyOfLocalResidueConditions of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] Here is how to consider local residue conditions in families:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item
      The \defined{universal branching locus} is the tame Fréchet submanifold
      \begin{equation*}
        \mathbf{Br}
        \coloneq
        \set[\big]{
          ({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];x) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram} \times X : x \in \mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l})
        }
        \subseteq
        \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}\times X.
      \end{equation*}
      The projection map $p \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{Br} \to \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$ is a fibre bundle.
    \item
      The normal bundle ${\mathbf{N}} \coloneq N\mathbf{Br}$ shall be identified with
      \begin{equation*}
        {\mathbf{N}} = \coprod_{({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}} N\mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}).
      \end{equation*}
      It inherits a Euclidean metric from the metrics on $X$ which are part of ${\mathbf{p}} \in \mathbf{Dir}$.
      This induces an isomorphism $(\Lambda^2 {\mathbf{N}})^{\otimes 2} \cong \mathbb{R}$ and endows
      \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{A} \coloneq \mathbb{R} \oplus i \Lambda^2 {\mathbf{N}}
      \end{equation*}
      with the structure of a bundle of algebras over $\mathbf{Br}$,
      whose fibres are isomorphic to $\mathbb{C}$, but canonically only up to complex conjugation.
    \item
      The construction from  [BeraWalpuski2025] explained in (Sec_ResidueMap of the paper), parametrised by $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$, assembles into an $\mathbb{A}$--module ${\mathbf{N}}^{-1/2}$.      
    \item
      Clifford multiplication endows $\mathrm{pr}_X^*S$ to $\mathbf{Br}$ with the structure of an $\mathbb{A}$--module.    
      The \defined{universal residue bundle} is the rank $r$ Euclidean bundle over $\mathbf{Br}$ defined by
      \begin{equation*}
        \check {\mathbf{S}} \coloneq
        \mathrm{pr}_X^*S \otimes_\mathbb{A} {\mathbf{N}}^{-1/2}
        =
        \coprod_{({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}} \check S_{\mathbf{p}}.
      \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}
  \item
    A \defined{family of local residue conditions} $({\mathbf{B}},f,{\mathbf{V}})$ consists of:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item
      a tame Fréchet manifold ${\mathbf{B}}$,
    \item
      a tame smooth map $f \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace {\mathbf{B}} \to \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$, and
    \item
      a subbundle ${\mathbf{V}} \subseteq (p^*f)^* \check {\mathbf{S}}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Here $f^*p \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace f^*\mathbf{Br} \to {\mathbf{B}}$ is the pullback of the universal branching locus and $p^*f \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace f^*\mathbf{Br} \to \mathbf{Br}$ is the projection map;
    see (Fig_FamiliesOfLocalResidueConditions of the paper).
  \item
    A family of local residue conditions $({\mathbf{B}},f,{\mathbf{V}})$ defines a family of residue conditions $({\mathbf{B}},f,{\mathbf{R}})$
    such that for every $b \in {\mathbf{B}}$ 
    \begin{equation*}
      {\mathbf{R}}_b \coloneq \Gamma(Z_b,{\mathbf{V}}|_{Z_b}) \subseteq \Gamma(Z_b,\check S_{{\mathbf{p}}_b})
      \quad\text{with}\quad    
      ({\mathbf{p}}_b;Z_b,[{\mathfrak l}_b]) \coloneq f(b).
      \qedhere
    \end{equation*}
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.384582+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb3d565ccdeae0a93a818cb8ff981063033d1d406e814a3e3ee66b46c2cc3e0b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:T.bds of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Let $d \geq 2$ and $r>r_c$. Then, there exist $C,c>0$ and $\beta^\dagger >1$ such that, for all $x \in \R^d$ and every $t> \beta^\dagger \|x\|$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \mu\big(D(o,x) \geq t\big)  \leq Ce^{-c t}. 
    \end{equation}

    Additionally, if (equation A2 of the paper) holds, then there exist $c_{1}, c_{2}>0$ and $\beta>1$ such that, for all $x \in \R^d$ and every $t> \beta \|x\|$,
