{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.666566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb9393e6db2956e7e0f7541e99cb89f52826143aaee9a00cc675c877f1dac333","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Chung's LIL) of arXiv:2608.04644, 'Khinchin's and Chung's Laws of the Iterated Logarithm at Time Zero for the Linear Stochast'] Suppose that Condition \ref{hypo} holds, $H<1$,   {$0\le\gamma<1$}, and $\beta+\gamma<2+H$.
	Then, for every  fixed $x\in\RR^d$,
	\begin{equation}
		\liminf_{\varepsilon\downarrow0} \sup_{t\in[0,\varepsilon]}\frac{|u(t,x)|}{\e^{H}(\log\log \e^{-1})^{-H}}
		=\kappa\lambda_H^{H},\ \ \ a.s.,
	\end{equation}
	where   
	\begin{equation}
		\textcolor{black}{\kappa:=
		\Bigg(\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\mathbb{R}}\frac{1-\cos\tau}{|\tau|^{2H+1}}\,d\tau
		\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\frac{|\xi|^{\ell-d}}
		{1+2|\xi|^\alpha\cos(\pi\beta/2)+|\xi|^{2\alpha}}\,d\xi
		\Bigg)^{1/2}.}
	\end{equation}
 \textcolor{black}{Here,} $\lambda_H$ is the small-ball constant of a standard FBM $\{B_H(t)\}_{t\ge0}$ with index $H$ (see, e.g., \cite{LS2001}), defined by
 \begin{equation}
\lambda_H:=-\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\varepsilon^{1/H}
\log\mathbb P\left(\sup_{0\le t\le1}|B_H(t)|\le\varepsilon\right).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:26:26.759404+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb97a9dd13a7f786b078b95611d490399d1a9f2d5add71bb0031968402742db0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:BLPP-kernel-reform of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The multipoint distribution satisfies
