{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.578583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebddf818e93d4e3c96ad0ad66ae97daa150323cfa61b72264fa6bd0a5a08c7f7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $k\in \Nbb_{\leq n}$, $f:\cube^n\to \Rbb$ and $q\in [1,2)$,
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\left|\grad^k f\right|\right\|_{\Leb^2}^{2} \geq \Wop^{\geq k}(f) \left[1+ \frac{q}{2k(2-q)}\ln^+\left(\frac{\Wop^{\geq k}(f)}{\sum_{J:|J|=k} \left\|\Dop_J f\right\|_{\Leb^q}^2} \right)\right]^k,
\end{equation}
and further for $\left\|f\right\|_{\Leb^\infty}\leq 1$ and $q\leq p<2$,
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\left|\grad^k f\right|\right\|_{\Leb^p}^{p} \geq C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Eldan-Gross-Boolean}}}(p,q)^k\Wop^{\geq k}(f) \left[1 + \frac{q}{2k(2-q)}\ln^+\left(\frac{1}{\sum_{J:|J|=k} \left\|\Dop_J f\right\|_{\Leb^q}^2} \right)\right]^\frac{kp}{2},
\end{equation}
with $C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Eldan-Gross-Boolean}}}(p,q) = \left(\frac{2}{2-q}\,M(p)^{-\frac{2}{p}}+\frac{pq}{2\upe(2-p)(2-q)}\right)^{-\frac{p}{2}} \gtrsim \left(2-p\right)\left(2-q\right)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:26:47.198706+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebde298ef8eb55bb41a4aa4352c0237416b2d19de0b0b908da34b3c0e009d067","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 128 of arXiv:2608.29416, 'Algorithmic threshold for high-dimensional projection pursuit I: general theory'] Fix $\tep\ll\lambda\ll 1$, and let $J=J(p,2\tep,\lambda/2)$ as defined by \eqref{e:bad.times.J}. Then there exists a set $\oJ$ such that
$J\subseteq \oJ\subseteq [q_0,1]$,
and $\oJ$ can be expressed as the (not necessarily disjoint) union of at most $L^{O(1)}/\lambda$ open intervals each of width $10\tep$. 
In particular, $\oJ$ has one-dimensional Lebesgue measure
	\[\mu_{\textup{Leb}}(\oJ)
	\leq \frac{L^{O(1)}\tep}{\lambda}
	\ll 1\,.\]

\begin{proof}
Subdivide $[q_0,1]$ into intervals of width $2\tep$. (If $(1-q_0)/(2\tep)$ is not integer-valued, then simply let the last interval have width less than $2\tep$.) For the purposes of this proof let us say an interval is ``bad'' if $p'$ decreases  by more than $\lambda/8$ over the interval; otherwise we say the interval is ``good.'' Since $p'$ is monotone and assumed bounded by $L^{O(1)}$, the total number of bad intervals is at most $L^{O(1)}/\lambda$. Let $B\subseteq[0,1]$ be the union of all the bad intervals, and let $\oJ$ be the $(4\tep)$-neighborhood of $B$. We then claim that $J\subseteq\oJ$: to see this, note that if $x\in \oJ^c$, then the interval $[x-2\tep,x+2\tep]$ is disjoint from the bad intervals $B$, and is covered by at most three good intervals. It follows that $p'$ decreases by at most $3\lambda/8$ over the interval $[x-2\tep,x+2\tep]$, which means $x\in J^c$. This implies the claim.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.535357+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebde9abd786c6616c4176f5e0ee877a61734096962d645b25a287034e5e3d5cc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2603.28170, 'Stabilization time of finite configurations with a second class particle in discrete TASEP'] Let $p=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\lambda}{2\sqrt{n}}$ for some $\lambda\in\R$ be fixed
