{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.398412+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebf53c3bab72eeca6523aeb3545d41b2d9a9c8375c3ab1592a186a0634c14180","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition d: Wasserstein distance of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Suppose that $\Phi, \Psi$ are invariant random (nonnegative) measures with the same finite intensity. For $p\in [1,\infty)$ define
    \begin{align}
        W_p(\Phi, \Psi) := \inf\bigg\{\bigg(\BE_0^\Phi\bigg[\int\|x\|^p\, K_0(dx)\bigg]\bigg)^{\frac{1}{p}}: K \text{ invariant proba} &\text{bility kernel} \nonumber\\
        &\text{with } \Psi=K\Phi\bigg\},
    \end{align}
    and for $p=\infty$ define
    \begin{align}
        W_\infty(\Phi, \Psi) := \inf\bigg\{\sup_{\substack{\omega\in A\\ x\in\supp(K_0(\omega))}} \|x\| : K &\text{ invariant probability kernel}\nonumber\\ &\text{with } \Psi=K\Phi,A\in \cF, \BP_0^\Phi(A)=1\bigg\},
    \end{align}
    then $W_p(\Psi, \Phi)$ is called the $p$- or $L^p$-Wasserstein distance.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.969363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebf6034e2ac81d675b151e78be7eec1f755bc3514ce843c47203bef70bf2d90b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 (Endpoint factorization) of arXiv:2605.11451, '2605.11451'] Let $1\le p<\infty$, $n\ge2$, and $1\le k\le n$. Then
\begin{equation}
 A_{p,n,0}(u^{(k)})
