and $C=C(\alpha,\gamma,p,d, \chi, \lambda,H,\|\sigma\|_{\mathcal{C}^5},C^X_{D, p}, C^X_{S, 2p}, C^Y_{D,p}, C^Y_{S, 2p})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.049374+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebfbc682e7d03b4e5f029d8d5c554f8be82344ef9c4f88e5ffc1bd7818ad79d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mylem 7 (Proposition 4.2 in \cite{Rudelson_aop}) of arXiv:2607.00495, 'Rank deficiency of Bernoulli random matrices for growing corank'] Let $v_1,\dots,v_{l} \in \mathrm{Comp}_{n}(\tau^{2},\tau^{4})$ be an orthogonal system. Then there exists $u_1,\dots,u_{l} \in \mathrm{Sparse}_{n}(4\tau^{2})\cap\frac{\tau}{\sqrt{n}}\mathbb{Z}^{n}\cap \frac{3}{2}B_{2}^{n}\setminus \frac{1}{2}B_{2}^{n} $ are $\frac{1}{2}$-almost orthogonal satisfying 
    \begin{align*}
        \Vert B(v_{j}-u_{j})\Vert_{2} \le 3\tau\sqrt{n} \text{ for all } j \in [l].
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.704408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec009272b374ac9d1dc1426ecf6865966a498175a37501b4fe7da4f99aca9499","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 44 of arXiv:2608.30990, 'Convergence of symmetries: nilpotency, dimension, and perfectness'] For any $\varepsilon > 0 $ there is  $\delta > 0 $ such that for $i$ large enough, any $\ww \in  B_{\delta} (0) \subset \mathfrak{g}_i$ satisfies
