{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:29:21.945679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec08131ea3f234d54ea4b2753890fcdd10e17c8f38b3431ad9112197d9497541","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 9 of arXiv:2608.01860, 'A Variational Characterization of a Gibbs Measure related to the Aviles-Giga Functional'] Denote the canonical variable on $\mathscr{X}\times\mathcal{H}$ by $(\mathbb{X},u)$ and consider the space of probability measures
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{Y}\coloneq \{\mu\in \mathcal{P}(\mathscr{X}\times\mathcal{H})\ |\ \mathbb{E}_\mu[\Vert u\Vert_\mathcal{H}^2]<\infty\}.
    \end{align*}
    We equip this space with the topology induced by the convergence $\mu_n\to\mu$ which is defined as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $(\mu_n)_{n}$ converges to $\mu$ weakly on $\mathscr{X}\times \mathcal{H}_w$, where $\mathcal{H}_w$ is $\mathcal{H}$ equipped with the weak topology,
        \item $\sup_n\mathbb{E}_{\mu_n}[\Vert u\Vert_\mathcal{H}^2]<\infty$.
    \end{itemize}
    Let 
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{X}\coloneq \{\mu\in\mathcal{Y}\ |\ \mu=\Law_\mathbb{P}(\mathbb{Y},u)\text{ for some }u\in\mathbb{H}\}
    \end{align}
    and denote by $\bar{\mathcal{X}}\subset\mathcal{Y}$ the closure of $\mathcal{X}$ in $\mathcal{Y}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.786560+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec08f0cfbdf031fb1a510e1cf8e08dfa060bb3fae2bbddafad144d1d9c126513","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 45 of arXiv:2608.30549, '\vspace{-1cm}Maximally Spread Out Measures and Implications\\{} for Phase Transitions in A'] Let $(\XX,\mathcal{F})$ be a measurable space and let $\mu\colon \mathcal{F}\to [0,1]$
    be a probability measure on $\XX$.
    Let $\SC \subset \XX$ be such that $\om{\mu}(\SC)=1$.
    Then the measure $\nu$ defined via
    \begin{equation}

       \nu(A \cap \SC) := \mu(A)
       \quad \text{ for $A\in \mathcal{F}$}
    \end{equation}
    is a well-defined probability measure on $\mathcal{F}\sigmacap\SC$.
    Furthermore,
    \begin{equation}

        \om{\nu}(B) = \om{\mu}(B)
        \quad \text{ for all $B\subset \SC$}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.666091+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec090f7e7441a5c1b81a9d9554a2ea034819faa810dc64093f52d8e9ac7a1a75","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn eq:tau-col of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] For $v\in\mathds{T}_n$ and $t\geqslant0$, define the one-source passage times and
passage-time balls by
\begin{align*}
 \mathsf{T}_1(v)&:=T_{\mathds{T}_n}^{(\ell)}(0,v),
 &
 \mathcal{A}_1(t)&:=\{v\in\mathds{T}_n:\mathsf{T}_1(v)\leqslant t\},\\
 \mathsf{T}_2(v)&:=T_{\mathds{T}_n}^{(\ell)}(U_n,v),
 &
 \mathcal{A}_2(t)&:=\{v\in\mathds{T}_n:\mathsf{T}_2(v)\leqslant t\}.
\end{align*}
The \emph{original-time collision time} is
\begin{align}
\tau_{\mathrm{col}}:=\inf\Big\{t\geqslant0:
&\text{ there exist }u\in\mathcal{A}_1(t),\ v\in\mathcal{A}_2(t)
\text{ with }\{u,v\}\in\mathcal{E}_n^{(\ell)}
\text{ and} \notag\\
&\go_{u,v}
\leqslant (t-\mathsf{T}_1(u))+(t-\mathsf{T}_2(v))
\Big\}.
\end{align}
Its rescaled version is
\begin{align*}
 \mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{col}}:=\mathfrak{a}_\ell\tau_{\mathrm{col}}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.392058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec0c8bc088638630961c152f00f276b518829871f12c3592bac47244b1e26d45","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:hg of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] Let $U, \tilde{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open sets and consider a diffeomorphism $f:U \to \tilde{U}$. Assume there exists $u_{*} \in U$ such that $f(u_{*}) = u_{*}$ and that the Jacobian matrix $Df(u^{*})$ has no eigenvalue with absolute value one. Then there exist an open neighborhood $V \subset U$ of $u_{*}$, an open neighborhood $\tilde{V} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n}$ of the origin, and a homeomorphism $h: V \to W$ such that $h(u^{*}) = 0$ and
\begin{equation}
f(x) = h^{-1}\Big( Df(u_{*}) h(x) \Big), \qquad \text{for all } x \in V.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.405591+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec10dced39543db8aab25b2a9c7c578e13438566eef9909615582da714fc31d1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 7 of arXiv:2604.06516, 'From stochastic individual-based models to free-boundary Hamilton-Jacobi equations'] Let $x\in\R$. We consider the birth-death-mutation process $(Z^K_t,t\ge 0)$ defined as before but started from a unique individual with trait $x$, i.e.\ $Z^K_0=\delta_{x}$, and we denote the corresponding expectation by $\mathbb{E}_{\delta_x}$.\\ 
(i) Let $\varphi:\R\to\R$ be bounded and measurable. Then, for any $t>0$, we have
		\begin{equation}

			\mathbb{E}_{\delta_{x}}\left[\langle {Z}^K_{t}, \varphi\rangle \right]=\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\exp\left(\int_{0}^{t} R(X^K_s)\ ds \right)\varphi(X^K_{t}) \right],
