\item[\textup{(ii)}] $G^\varepsilon(t):=-e^{-2\kappa t}\,h^{\varepsilon\,\prime}(t)$ is nonincreasing on $(0,\infty)$.
\end{enumerate}
If $h$ is differentiable at some $t_0>0$, then $G^\varepsilon(t_0)\to -e^{-2\kappa t_0}h'(t_0)$, and hence
$h^{\varepsilon\,\prime}(t_0)\to h'(t_0)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:29:27.995230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec23bbff65bef4e53d0edd6143e415ef5b8f7d37bb9a6e62c9c2af0d3d106a74","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 (Distance to the weighted holomorphic space) of arXiv:2608.24863, 'Uniform Logarithmic Sobolev Inequalities for the 2D Coulomb Gas at the Diffusive Temperatu'] For $f\in C_c^\infty(\Omega_N;\C)$,
