{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.305098+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec2928d24b35bd0b021a4e91234988aa5f02c97b0ee802841977c23a61c438fe","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_Gt_asymp of arXiv:2605.27949, 'Yaglom limits of continuous-state branching processes in Brownian random environment \than'] In the weakly subcritical regime $({\bf m}\in(-\sigma^2,0))$, let the time-dependent function $G_t(y, v)$ be defined as:
\begin{eqnarray}
G_t(y, v) := 2^{\frac{\eta}{2}-1} y^{\frac{\eta}{2}-1} v^{\frac{\eta}{2}-1} \mathrm{e}^{- \frac{{\bf m}^2}{2\sigma^2}t} \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{1}{2y} - v} \theta\left( \sqrt{\frac{2v}{y}}, \frac{\sigma^2\beta^2}{4}t \right),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\theta(r, t)$ is defined in Proposition (jtpdf of the paper). Then, for any $y>0$ and $v>0$,
\begin{eqnarray*}
\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} t^{3/2} \mathrm{e}^{\frac{{\bf m}^2}{2 \sigma^2} t} G_t(y, v) = \frac{4\sqrt 2}{\sigma^3\beta^3\sqrt{ \pi }} G(y, v),
\end{eqnarray*}
where $G(y,v)$ is the time-independent kernel defined in (equation G_def of the paper). Furthermore, there exists a constant $C' > 0$ such that for all $t \ge 1$, the scaled function satisfies the uniform bound:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left| t^{3/2} \mathrm{e}^{\frac{{\bf m}^2}{2 \sigma^2} t} G_t(y, v) \right| \le C' G(y,v).
\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.082130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec29651442dedc0f9f07ca0faf82dd3856a42776e6199dc26d0812d3038cabb5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2504.18739, 'discrete analogues of second-order Riesz transforms'] For all  $j,k=1,2,\ldots,d$. with $j\neq k$, we have 
