{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.473862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec68647c3a156672703f11e275726a0cecb4c31a7cce34c89df1f7a4aafed93f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p: d>=1 hyperfluctuationg lattice thinning of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] The hyperplane intersection process $\Psi$, defined as in~(equation e: d>=1 hyperfluctuationg lattice thinning of the paper), is solely \linebreak $(-d+(s-2))$-uniform with
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\BV[\Psi([0,r)^d)]}{r^{2d-(s-2)}} \xrightarrow{r\to\infty} \frac{d\zeta(s)^2\Gamma(s-4)}{2\zeta(s-1)^{2d+1}\Gamma(s)} > 0.
    \end{equation}
    Hence, for any $ d\in\N,p\in(-d,-d+1)$, there is an $s\in(2,3)$ such that $\Psi$ is a solely $p$-uniform thinning of the stationary lattice.
    Further, the distribution of $\Psi$ is not only invariant under translation but also under all isometries of the lattice.
    \begin{proof}
        By Proposition~(p: d=1 hyperfluctuationg lattice thinning of the paper), we know that
        \begin{equation*}
        \frac{\BV[\Phi_1([0,r)])]}{r^{4-s}} \xrightarrow{r\to\infty} \frac{\zeta(s)^2\Gamma(s-4)}{2\zeta(s-1)^3\Gamma(s)} > 0.
        \end{equation*}
        As $\BE[\Phi_1([0,r)])] = \frac{1}{\zeta(s-1)}r$,
        This relation implies that
        \begin{equation*}
            \frac{\BV[\Phi_1([0,r))]}{\BE[\Phi_1([0,r))]^2} \xrightarrow{r\to\infty} 0.
        \end{equation*}
        Therefore, as $r\to\infty$,
        \begin{align*}
            \BV[\Psi([0,r)^d)] &= \BV[\Phi_1([0,r))\cdots\Phi_d([0,r))] \nonumber\\
            &= \BE\big[\Phi_1([0,r))^2\big]^d - \big(\BE[\Phi_1([0,r))]^2\big)^d \nonumber\\
            &= d \big(\BE[\Phi_1([0,r))]^2\big)^{d-1} \BV[\Phi_1([0,r))] + o(\big(\BE[\Phi_1([0,r))]^2\big)^{d-1} \BV[\Phi_1([0,r))]) \nonumber\\
            &= \underbrace{\frac{d\zeta(s)^2\Gamma(s-4)}{2\zeta(s-1)^{2d+1}\Gamma(s)}}_{>0}r^{2d-(s-2)} + o(r^{2d-(s-2)}).
        \end{align*}
        Finally, since $s-2<1$ and $\I_{[0,1)^d}$ is Fourier-smooth with exponent $d-2$ by Example~(ex: fourier smooth of the paper), we can leverage Theorem~(t:asymptotic variance test function of the paper) to obtain that $\Psi$ is solely $(-d+(s-2))$-uniform.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

