{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:06:42.725374+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed0e516cb78c3076a80e63c604b97d216d46d212bba59cd2eb22d0b94b0fbfdf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: holo functions of arXiv:2411.04238, 'Holomorphic jump-diffusions'] (\textit{Definition 1.2.1 in} [SC:05])
    Let $U\subseteq\mathbb C^d$ be an open set and $m\in \mathbb{N}$.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item {\textbf{Complex differentiable functions}} 

        A function $f:U\rightarrow\mathbb C^m$ is called \textit{complex differentiable} at $z_0\in U$ if for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a $\delta>0$ and a $\mathbb C$-linear map
    \begin{align*}
        Lf(z_0): \mathbb C^d\rightarrow\mathbb C^m
    \end{align*}
    such that  for all $z\in U$ with $\|z-z_0\|\leq \delta$, the inequality 
    \begin{align*}
        \|f(z)-f(z_0)-Lf(z_0)(z-z_0)\|\leq \varepsilon\|z-z_0\|
    \end{align*}
    holds.
     \item {\textbf{Holomorphic functions}}

     A function $f:U\rightarrow\mathbb C^m$ is called \textit{holomorphic} on $U$ if it is complex differentiable at all $z_0\in U$. A holomorphic function on the whole $\mathbb C^d$ is called \textit{entire}.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.305382+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed10299cb040eae133663e24ca7f4f15744f4c0a5e033dc5d6225e5ed0e04c29","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2609.02385, 'Kinetic Fokker--Planck Equations with Drifts in a Supercritical Range'] Under Assumption {\bf ($\wt{\text{H}}$)}, let $u$ be a weak subsolution of \eqref{kineticpde} in $Q_2$. Then for all $|t|\leq1$,
