for some finite constants \(K_1, K_2\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.445635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed7f56df449ca80082a19bf85a2ec2ca20141f25360089c23b48da1e7667d03f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 of arXiv:2606.15842, 'A small noise approximation for Muller's Ratchet'] Let $X$ be a solution of \eqref{eq:ratchetSDE} and $X(0) = \text{Poi}(\theta)$. Then, as $\nu\to \infty$,  the first moment $m_1(X(t ))$ of the frequency profile $X(t )$ obeys
    \begin{align}
          \mathbb E\big(m_1( X(t))\big) & = \theta +  \frac{1}{2\nu\alpha}  \Big(\exp\big(\theta (1-e^{-\alpha t })^2\big) - 1\Big) + \mathcal  O\Big(\frac{1}{\nu^2}\Big) ,
          \intertext{while the expected size of the class carrying $k$ mutations satisfies}
          \mathbb{E}\big(X_k(t )\big) & = e^{-\theta}\frac{\theta^k}{k!} + \frac{1}{\nu \alpha} \int_0^{t \alpha} \exp\Big(-\theta e^{-s}\Big)\Big( \exp\Big(-\theta e^{-s}(1 - e^{-s})\Big)\frac{\theta^k}{k!} \\ \notag & \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad - \frac{\theta^k(1- e^{-s}(1 - e^{-s}))^k}{k!}\Big) ds + \mathcal O\left(\frac{1}{\nu^2}\right).
          \intertext{In particular,}
          \mathbb E\big(X_0(t )\big) & = e^{-\theta} - \frac{1}{\nu \alpha} \int_0^{t  \alpha} \exp\Big(-\theta e^{-s}\Big)\Big(1 - \exp\Big(-\theta e^{-s}(1 - e^{-s})\Big)\Big) ds + \mathcal O\Big(\frac{1}{\nu^2}\Big). 
          \intertext{The expected centered second moment of the frequency profile satisfies}
        \mathbb E\big(\bar m_2(X(t) )\big) & = \theta - \frac{\theta}{\nu\alpha}e^{-t \alpha}(1 - e^{-t \alpha}) \exp\Big(\theta(1 - e^{-t \alpha})^2 \Big) + \mathcal O\Big(\frac{1}{\nu^2}\Big) .
        \intertext{Finally, the variance of $m_1(X(t ))$ and its covariance with $X_0(t )$ obey}
        \mathbb V(m_1(X(t) )) & = \frac{1}{\nu\alpha} \int_0^{t \alpha} \theta e^{-2s}\big(1 + \theta(1 - e^{-s})^2 \big) \exp\Big(\theta(1 - e^{- s})^2 \Big) ds + \mathcal O\Big(\frac{1}{\nu^2}\Big),  \\
        \operatorname{Cov} (m_1(X(t) ), & X_0(t )) = \frac{e^{-\theta}}{2\nu\alpha} \Big( \exp\Big( \theta(1 - e^{-t \alpha})^2 \Big) - 2\exp\Big( \theta(1 - e^{-t \alpha}) \Big) + 1 \Big) + \mathcal O\Big(\frac{1}{\nu^2}\Big).           
        \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.647505+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed835627303cfd75eaf8c6ea63f9978ff4761d16f9e8bfc267ad70d7423389ed","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-Par of arXiv:2603.01823, 'on a fractional stochastic heat equation arising from the disordered pinning model'] A random variable $J$ defined on $\Xi$ is called the \emph{partition
function} or \emph{total mass} of the \emph{randomized shift} $m(t)$, if for any bounded measurable function $F:\Xi\to\mathbb R$, we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E[JF(\xi)]=\mathbb E\mathbf{E}_{(t,x)} [F(\xi+m(t))].
\end{equation}
An equivalent formulation is that the marginal distribution of $\xi+m(t)$ is absolutely continuous with respect to that of $\xi$ with density (Radon-Nikodym derivative) $J$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.969623+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed85a500036ca6f754070f50f3147ef08d5d11442d882eb1e59bb9248595e2ad","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:preferableevents of arXiv:2411.07125, 'Mixing on the cycle with constant size perturbation'] Consider the following preferable events:
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{B}_1 &= \{a_i>\log^2 n,~ i=1,\ldots,2k\}\quad \textrm{--- concerning the graph},\\

\mathcal{B}_2 &=\{\beta(t,s) > -\log^2 n,~ 0\le t<s\le n^2\}\quad \textrm{--- concerning the walk}.
\end{align*}
For these we have $\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{B}_1)\geq 1-\lambda_1 \log^2 n/n$ and $\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{B}_2)\geq 1-\exp(-\lambda_2\log^2 n)$ for appropriate $\lambda_1,\lambda_2>0$ as $n\to\infty$.
In particular, no full arc is backtracked once fully passed and thus $\xi_l, l=0,1,\ldots, L$ asymptotically almost surely (a.a.s.) properly represents the trajectory followed.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.205655+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed8785696cf9cc0baf4ff4481817dcb4f34991a6ca0d0251b8005caf44d1de8a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 36 of arXiv:2608.03575, '2608.03575'] There exists a constant \(C<\infty\), depending only on the constants $\eps_0,\eps_1$, such that for every
