{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.307989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee67bab3473508a2638798d08aef65d3e768ba1bc161d45de7e9a132aca18090","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:varianceunderinvariance of arXiv:2411.08848, 'Stationary random measures: Covariance asymptotics, variance bounds and central limit theo'] Let the setting be as in Lemma~(l:exactcov of the paper) and in addition, assume that $f$ satisfies the same assumptions as $g$.   

\begin{enumerate}
\item {\bf Flip invariance:} If ${\bf K}$ is invariant under reflections in the co-ordinate hyperplanes, then $I(\gamma)=0$ unless $\gamma\in (2\mathbb{Z}_+)^d$ and hence,
\begin{align}
Q_{2p}^{{\bf K}}(f,g)=\sum_{|\gamma|=p}\frac{(-1)^p}{(2\gamma)!}I(2\gamma)\langle \partial^{\gamma} f,  \partial^{\gamma}g\rangle

\end{align}

\item {\bf Orthogonal invariance:} If ${\bf K}$ is invariant under orthogonal transformations, we may write ${\bf K}(\mathrm{d} z)= r^{d-1}{\bf K}(\mathrm{d} r)\sigma(\mathrm{d} \omega)$ where $z = r\omega$ with $r>0$, $\omega\in \mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ and $\sigma$ denotes the surface-area measure on $\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$. Here we abuse notation by using ${\bf K}$ for the measure on the radial part as well but that will not cause any confusion. Then 
\begin{align}
Q_{2p}^{{\bf K}}(f,g)  = \begin{cases}(\lambda_D + \sigma_dJ(d-1))\ \langle f,g\rangle & \mbox{ if }p=0, \\
\frac{(-1)^p\sigma_d J(d-1+2p)}{p! \ 2^p \ d(d+2)\ldots (d+2p-2)}\langle \Delta^{p/2}f, \Delta^{p/2}g\rangle & \mbox{ if }p\ge 1. \end{cases}    
\end{align}
Here  $J(p):=\int_0^{\infty}r^{p} \,{\bf K}(\mathrm{d} r)$ and $\Delta$ is the Laplacian, whose $\tfrac{p}{2}$ power is interpreted as $\Delta^{p/2}f:=\nabla(\Delta^q f)$ if $p=2q+1$. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.889546+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee6debea39ce82c64553ca3e91aed2db56daedebe769da6632e2d1587f53b469","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:skew-marginal-hierarchy of arXiv:2606.29557, 'Propagation of chaos for Belavkin equations beyond pure states'] For \(1\le n<N\), the marginal \(\rho^{N:n}\) satisfies
