  \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.330231+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef1475fbf1b0d10fc0f26a595e94dbf7d6c2139f6405de128bea431307d7724e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The localization ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}$ has a fully faithful right adjoint.
    \begin{proof}
        If $G$ is any compact Lie group, then Proposition~(prop:L-gl-Bousfield of the paper) shows that the localization functor ${\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-lvl}={\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}(\mathbb B{G})\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}(\mathbb B{G})={\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-gl}$ admits a right adjoint, which is fully faithful with essential image given by the $G$-global $\Omega$-spectra. It therefore only remains to verify the Beck--Chevalley condition. By full faithfulness of the right adjoints, this reduces to proving that the restriction functors preserve the essential images, which is clear from the definition of $G$-global $\Omega$-spectra. 
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.873565+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef17eb42ef63898fb481b7c560cee1f05377cad196afef30451a5736bcb9b629","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 39 (Composition and interpolation) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Schatten-stable maps are closed under composition, and
\begin{equation}

 \kappa_r(\Psi\circ\Phi)
 \le\kappa_r(\Psi)\kappa_r(\Phi).
\end{equation}
More generally, let
\[
 1\le r_0<r<r_1\le\infty,
 \qquad
 \frac1r=\frac{1-\theta}{r_0}+\frac\theta{r_1},
 \qquad 0<\theta<1.
\]
If $\kappa_{r_0}(\Phi)$ and $\kappa_{r_1}(\Phi)$ are finite, then
\begin{equation}

 \kappa_r(\Phi)
 \le \kappa_{r_0}(\Phi)^{1-\theta}
      \kappa_{r_1}(\Phi)^\theta.
\end{equation}
In particular, interpolation applies on both sides of the Hilbert endpoint;
for $2<r<\infty$ it gives
$\kappa_r(\Phi)\le
\kappa_2(\Phi)^{2/r}\kappa_\infty(\Phi)^{1-2/r}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.143704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef1c568ca98f7e170b8519e226c9c680cde7b9267545d546ebdc56896b9fcedb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 (REM universality) of arXiv:2604.06122, 'REM universality for linear random energy'] Given $\rho\in (0,1)$, and let $\tilde a$ and $\tilde \lambda$ satisfy
\been{

G^*(\tilde a)=\rho\(\log(2)-\log(1+|m|)\,\),\qquad G'(\tilde \lambda)=\tilde a.
}
Then there exists a $h-$measurable random sequence $(A_n(h))$, such that the point process $\Hn$, defined by
\been{
