{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.746669+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef5b2a7ae3d2be20b2e76783374eb87fdebfdc98f2245364c78ab22b493c8e64","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:q_poisson_process of arXiv:2608.28169, 'characteristic polynomial of self-normalized random matrices'] Let $\alpha \in (0, 2)$ and let $(U_r)_{r \geqslant 1}$ be a family of 
    i.i.d. $\operatorname{Beta}(1 - \alpha/2, \alpha/2)$ variables. 
    For $q \geqslant 1$, set $W_q = \prod_{r=1}^q U_r$ and denote its law by $\mu_q^{(\alpha)}$. Let us define the measure $\nu_q^{(\alpha)}$ on $[-1, 1]$ by   
    \begin{equation}
    \nu_q^{(\alpha)}(\,\mathrm{d} x) \coloneqq  \frac{1}{2q|x|} \mu_q^{(\alpha)}(\,\mathrm{d}  |x|) \ ,
    \end{equation}
    that is, the measure for which for every $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ continuous and bounded,
\begin{equation}
    \int f(u) \nu_q^{(\alpha)}(\,\mathrm{d} x) = 
    \frac{1}{2 q} \int_0^1 \frac{1}{u} (f(u) + f(-u)) \mu_q^{(\alpha)}(\,\mathrm{d} u) \ .
\end{equation}
    The \textit{$q$-Poisson process} is the Poisson point process $N_q^{(\alpha)} = \sum_{i\geqslant 1} \delta_{\xi_{q, i}^{(\alpha)}}$ with intensity measure $\nu_q^{(\alpha)}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.647856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef5bf28abab10a8dbc0eb87762d545d4f29073312ed3e0d49cc48bd8db0dea11","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 4 of arXiv:2609.00723, 'An integral Hyodo--Kato isomorphism'] An $F$-pre-log algebra over $\sR$ is a pair $(\sA,F_A)$ consisting of a pre-log algebra 
$\sA=(A,\alpha\colon L \to A)$ over $\sR$ and a $F_R$-homomorphism $F_A\colon A\to A$ such that $F_A(a)\equiv a^p\mod pA$ (so, $F_A$ is a   lift of the absolute Frobenius of $A/p$).

 An $F$-pre-log algebra $(\sA,F_A)$ over $\sR$ is called \emph{special} if there is a decomposition $L=L^o\oplus N$ as monoids such that the structure map $N\to L$ is the inclusion into the second factor and that 
\eq{eq;def;Flogalgebra}{F_A(\alpha(l))=\alpha(l)^p\qfor l\in L^o.}
 A map $(\sA,F_A) \to (\sB,F_B)$ of $F$-pre-log algebras is a map $\sA\to \sB$ of pre-log rings which is compatible with $F_A$ and $F_B$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.963253+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef5cbf97e3b6d2f2b7459f81133eb2b85c50b79e86ea9dadba5973dab1469c2f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2604.00742, 'Delayed Logistic equation as a limit\\ of long memory Markov chains'] Fix $\tau > 0$ and $\mu \in (0,1)$. For each $N \in \N$, let $X^N$ be the continuous-time Markov chain defined in \eqref{chain}. Define the scaled process
	\begin{equation}
		Y^N(t) \;=\;
		\begin{cases}
			\dfrac{X_{- \tau N}^N(N(t+\tau))}{N}\,, & \text{if } t \in [-\tau, 0), \vspace{5pt}\\
			\dfrac{X_0^N(Nt)}{N}\,, & \text{if } t \geq 0.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	Then, for every $T > 0$, the sequence of processes $\{Y^N(t):t\in[-\tau,T]\}$ converge in probability in the Skorohod topology of $D([-\tau,T], \bb R)$ to the unique continuous solution $u:[-\tau,T]\to \bb R$ to the Delayed Logistic  differential equation
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{cases}
			\displaystyle u'(t) \;=\;\frac{1}{2} u(t)\Big(1 - u(t-\tau)\Big)\,, & \text{ for } t > 0,\vspace{4pt}\\
			u(t) \;=\; \mu\,, &  \text{ for } t \in [-\tau, 0].
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

