{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.952631+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_effd4187215a44b993d5d0f6a83cc9db63b35dcc408bb76ff4339914d8420fd6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 (Representation of averaged dynamics) of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] Suppose $\textup{Law}(X)=\bbE[P_{[T]}^N(\bfJ)]$. With $Q$ as in \eqref{eq: averaged dynamics 1}, define
    \[\Lambda^i(t) := \int_0^t D^i_s X_t^\top  Q_s X_s\, ds, \quad i = 1, \ldots, N, \quad t \in [0, T]. \]
    Then $X$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
dX_t^i &= - U'(X_t^i) dt + \beta^2 \bigg[ \int _0^t R_{\hat{\mu}^N}(t, s) dW^i_s \bigg] dt + \frac{\beta^2}{N}\Lambda^i(t)dt +  dW^i_t, \quad i = 1, \ldots, N, \quad t \in (0, T).\\
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
Moreover, for $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$ we have $R_{\hat{\mu}^N}(t, s) = \frac{1}{N} X_t^{\top} Q_s X_s.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.905970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_effd7964ec8aa8e2235d6822c82d82aa5ccb32f31b72df1c5b08ca9680a47f27","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:boolean-commutation of arXiv:2608.26707, 'An Exponential Lower Bound for the Permanent\\of Random Bernoulli Matrix'] For every $0\leq k\leq n-1$,
\begin{equation}
 D_{k+1}U_k-U_{k-1}D_k=(n-2k)\mathrm{Id}_{\mathcal{H}_k}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every $f\in\mathcal{H}_k$,
\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert U_kf\right\rVert_2^2
 =(n-2k)\left\lVert f\right\rVert_2^2+\left\lVert D_kf\right\rVert_2^2.
\end{equation}
In particular, if $k<n/2$, then
\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert U_kf\right\rVert_2^2\geq(n-2k)\left\lVert f\right\rVert_2^2.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.114732+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_effe6e662b2e385c47b175833ec1ed1e2497667bca97765a73151bcae0a3dd8d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm THM3.2 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Let $X$ be a Borel right process on $(E,d)$ satisfying (AC), (H1a) and (H1b), whose associated Dirichlet form $({\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{F}})$ on $L^2(E,\mu)$ is transient. 

Assume further that $X$ admits the following short-time heat kernel lower bound at a fixed point $x\in E$: For some $T>0, \alpha>0$ and $\beta>1$, there exist constants $C_1,C_2>0$  such that
\begin{equation}
p(t,x,y)\ge \frac{C_1}{t^{\alpha/\beta}}\exp\left\{-C_2\left(\frac{d(x,y)}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta -1)}\right\}, \quad \forall t\in (0, T], \, y\in E.
\end{equation}
Let $B\subset E$ be a nearly Borel set of finite $0$-order capacity.
Then, for any $\delta,\rho>0$, 
 \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}^x[\sigma_B\leq t]\geq \mathrm{Cap}_{(0)}(B_{x,\delta})\cdot  \frac{C_1C_3}{ t^{(\alpha-\beta)/\beta}}\exp\left\{-(1+\rho)C_2\left( \frac{\widetilde d(x,B)+\delta}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta-1)}\right\},\quad 0<t\leq T,
    \end{equation}
    where $B_{x,\delta}:=\{y\in B:d(y,x)\leq \widetilde d(x,B)+\delta\}$, $C_1, C_2$ are preserved as in (equation SubGaussianHKLB of the paper), and $C_3=(\beta-1)\rho\cdot \min\{(1+\rho)^{\alpha-\beta-\alpha/\beta}, 1\}>0$ is a constant depending on $\alpha, \beta,\rho$ (but not on $T$ or $\delta$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.745468+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0015b820204186f51791108fefb25eef32b49ad6768c7b54da52456600e1fe2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2606.11994, 'Sample Path Properties of the\\ Fractional Wiener--Weierstrass Bridge II'] \cite{SchiedZhang2024} For $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and $b\in\{2,3,\dots\}$, the stochastic process
\begin{equation}
Y(t):=
\sum_{n=0}^\infty \alpha^nB_H(\{b^n t\}),\qquad 0\le t\le 1,
\end{equation}
is called the \emph{fractional Wiener--Weierstrass  bridge} with parameters $\alpha$, $b$, and $H$.\footnote{The same process
is denoted by $X$ in \cite{SchiedZhang2024}; we use the notation of
\cite{SchiedZhang2026}.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.633586+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0028e3d00818ee5e5ee34f0efbc23c56e4ad4342470f3399d287ef2e976e01a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:equiv-extrem-uniqu of arXiv:2607.00800, 'Brownian motion in Minkowski normed spaces'] Let $(s,z) \in (0,\infty) \times \mathbb{R}^d$. 
  In the setting of the previous proposition, $u^z_{s+}$ is an extreme point in the set of probability solutions to (equation gen:lin-fpe of the paper) with initial datum $u^z(s,x) \,\mathrm d x$ if and only if $u^z_{s+}$ is a unique probability solution to (equation gen:lin-fpe of the paper) with initial datum $u^z(s,x) \,\mathrm d x$ in the set 
    \begin{equation}
        \bigl\{[t\mapsto v(t,x) \,\mathrm d x] \in C_w([0,\infty);\mathcal P)\,|\, \exists C \geq 1, \, v(t,x) \leq C u^z_{s+}(t,x) \,\mathrm d x \mathrm d t\text{-a.e.} \bigr\},
    \end{equation}
    where $C_w([0,\infty);\mathcal P)$ denotes the set of weakly continuous paths $t\mapsto \mu_t \in \mathcal P$ on $[0,\infty)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.103602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f00361560066c8e3a71a70ac64964420202e39518ed48e5f34a1d8d46ab87b01","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 42 of arXiv:2609.03821, 'Condensation and Metastability in the Supercritical Two-Species Zero-Range Process via Res'] For $\alpha>1$ and $\ell'<\ell/2<N/4$,
\[
\sum_{\substack{0\le p_{1},\dots,p_{k}<N-\ell:\\
p_{1}+\cdots+p_{k}=N,\\
p_{j}+p_{j'}<N-\ell',\ \forall j\ne j'
}
}\frac{1}{\mathfrak{a}(p_{1})\mathfrak{a}(p_{2})\cdots\mathfrak{a}(p_{k})}\leq\frac{c}{(\ell+1)^{\alpha-1}(\ell'+1)^{\alpha-1}N^{\alpha}}.
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.556502+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f00a40491175803214184f34fcac327f61f9793f9d97f5885575edf43599466e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main_cycle of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Let $\pi$ be a primitive word and let $w=\pi^d$ for some $d\geq 1$.
For any fixed integer $L\geq 1$,  we have

\begin{align}
	(C^\pi_{1,n},C^\pi_{2,n}, \dots, C^\pi_{L,n})
	\stackrel{(d)}{\longrightarrow}
	(Z_1,Z_2,\dots,Z_L),
\end{align}
	where $Z_1,Z_2,\dots$ are independent random variables with $Z_\ell \sim \text{Po}(1/\ell)$ for $\ell \in \mathbb{N}$. 
Moreover, 
\begin{align}
(C^w_{1,n}, C^w_{2,n}, \dots, C^w_{L,n})
	\stackrel{(d)}{\longrightarrow}
	\Psi(Z_1,Z_2,\dots,Z_{dL})
\end{align}
	where $\Psi(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_{dL})=(y_1,y_2,\dots,y_L)$ with
	$y_i:= \sum_{c\in[d]:c|d\atop \gcd{i}{c}=1} (d/c) x_{di/c}.$

	In both cases, the convergence also holds in the sense of  moments. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.707611+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f00d077ca89389aea44de9fb0f66bdb7d87c87f43bbb89a85dd0a2bb771f1a0e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:milne-factorization-comp of arXiv:2609.00698, 'Kinetic Wedge Layers and Diffusive Limit of Neutron Transport in Polygonal Domains'] For every $0<\kappa<\kappa_1<1$ and every $H\in Y_{\kappa_1}$, there is a unique pair $(E,\rho_{\mathrm{dec}})\in\mathbb R\times Y_\kappa$ such that $(I-K_+)(E+\rho_{\mathrm{dec}})=H$ on $\mathbb R_+$. Moreover the map $H\mapsto(E,\rho_{\mathrm{dec}})$ is linear and
\begin{align}
 \abs E+\nm{\rho_{\mathrm{dec}}}_{Y_\kappa}
 \le C_{\kappa,\kappa_1}\nm H_{Y_{\kappa_1}}.

\end{align}
Uniqueness is asserted only in the class $\mathbb R\oplus Y_\kappa$. Nothing is claimed about arbitrary distributional solutions of the half-line equation.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.922354+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f00d862da4f4c8f13c90939675520985f75312853d9b8b04ba833082dcb5323f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}.$ Assume that $f$ is bounded away from zero and infinity and $H(X|\pi_{T}) < \infty.$ Then, for every $a$ in $[0,1],$ we have 
