{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.087461+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f04d949697df69253dd9e8600d8548ad78b77f54ddc1ea73c964bc6f9916cadc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 24 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] For any continuous deterministic function $(\psi_t)_{t\geq 0}$ taking values in $\mathcal{H}^6_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S})$ such that for any $k\in \mathbb{N}^{*}$, $|\langle \psi,e_k\rangle| \leq C k^{-6}$ and let $(\zeta_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a deterministic curve satisfying conditions $\fbox{{\bf C3}}-\fbox{{\bf C4}}$.
Then, the integral process $(\int_0^t \psi_r\cdot\mathrm{d}\zeta_r)_{t\geq 0}$ is defined almost surely as:
\begin{align*}
\int_0^t \psi_r\cdot\mathrm{d}\zeta_r:=  \int_0^t \psi_r\cdot\mathrm{d}\mathscr{J}_r + \int_0^t \psi_r\cdot\mathrm{d}\eta_r
\end{align*}
Moreover, for any smooth function $\psi$ it satisfies for all $T\geq 0$
\begin{align}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\Big| \int_0^t \psi_r\cdot\mathrm{d}\zeta_r  \Big| \leq C \|\zeta_T\|_{2,-2} \times \sup_{t\in [0,T]}\|\psi_t\|_2.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

