{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:11:35.821906+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0ce3a90c7fa2b9c75d4b7e585c55fb5a5a6491180c4351ef50ef162ac61b470","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition lem:meeting of arXiv:2607.22908, 'Epidemic Phase Transitions in the Zero-Range Process'] Let $(W_t)_{t \ge 0}$ be a continuous-time nearest-neighbor symmetric random walk on $\mathbb{Z}^d$, absorbed at the origin, with a time-dependent total jump rate that is bounded below by $2\Gamma_- > 0$ almost surely. There exist constants $C_{\textnormal{\tiny (C:meeting_distance of the paper)}}(\Gamma_-, d) \in (0, 1)$ and $C_{\textnormal{\tiny (C:meeting of the paper)}}(d) >0$ such that for any initial position $W_0$ with $\|W_0\| \le R$ where $R \ge 1$, and any time $T > 0$ satisfying $R \le \sqrt{C_{\textnormal{\tiny (C:meeting_distance of the paper)}} T}$, the walk hits the origin by time $T$ with probability
