            I_k \coloneqq \{ i  \in I: \hspace{0.25cm} T_{1/k}(f^i) < \infty = T_{1/(k-1)}(f^i)\}.
        \end{equation*}
        We note that for $k \in \mathbb{N}$ that $n(\{f: T_{1/k}(f) < \infty = T_{1/(k-1)}(f)\}) = 1/2$. As $\sum_{i \in I}\delta_{f^i}$ is a Poisson random measure with intensity measure $(\norm{\mu_{\rm d}} + \norm{\mu_{\rm a}})n(\cdot)$ and the sets $I_k$ are disjoint, we have that $|I_k|$, $k \in \mathbb{N}$, are independent Poisson random variables each with mean $(\norm{\mu_{\rm d}} + \norm{\mu_{\rm a}})/2$.
