{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.076715+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1d9baad8d8fc0f9f92212dd2b6bd782fff6843bdd0ac17f7a731febe47a6a23","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:alphaSDE of arXiv:2509.15446, 'On the pair correlation function of the $\Sineb$ process'] Fix $\beta>0$ and $\Re \delta>-1/2$. 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item For any $\nu\in {\mathbb R}$ the system (equation eq:alphaSDE of the paper) has a unique strong solution $\alpha_{\lambda,\nu}(u)$ on $[\nu,\infty)$ with initial condition $\alpha_{\lambda,\nu}(\nu)=0$. For any given $\nu< u$, with probability one the function $\lambda\mapsto \alpha_{\lambda,\nu}(u)$ is analytic and strictly increasing with $\alpha_{0,\nu}(u)=0$ 

    \item There is a unique strong solution $\alpha_\lambda(u)$ of (equation eq:alphaSDE of the paper) on $(-\infty,\infty)$ satisfying the initial condition (equation eq:SDE_initial of the paper), which  can be obtained as the a.s.~limit $\alpha_\lambda(u)=\lim\limits_{\nu\to -\infty} \alpha_{\lambda,\nu}(u)$. The limit is monotone increasing for $\lambda>0$ and monotone decreasing for $\lambda<0$. 

    For any $u\in {\mathbb R}$ with probability one the function $\lambda\mapsto \alpha_\lambda(u)$ is analytic and strictly increasing in $\lambda$ with $\alpha_0(u)=0$. 

    Moreover, $\alpha_\lambda(u)$ satisfies the following scale-invariance property: for any fixed $s\in {\mathbb R}$ 
\begin{align}
    \left(\alpha_\lambda(t), t\in (-\infty,\infty)\right) \stackrel{d}{=}  \left(\alpha_{e^{\beta s/4}\lambda}(t-s), t\in (-\infty,\infty)\right). 
\end{align}  
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.754944+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1dabe1a7c39a3f0f6a3ae1d3e8b3fc0a98fd2d1356eb0a2b2b8275e41b350c6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2605.14254, '2605.14254'] The process $\xi^{\lambda,z}$ is a semi-martingale and it has the following dynamic:
		\begin{equation}
			\xi^{\lambda,z}_{t}=b^{\lambda,z}_t+\lambda\,\displaystyle\int_{0}^{t\wedge\sigma_z^{\lambda} }\text{sgn}(z-\xi^{\lambda,z}_{s})\,\mathrm{d}s,\, t\geq 0,
		\end{equation}
		where $b^{\lambda,z}$ is an $\mathbb{F}^{\xi^{\lambda,z}}$-Brownian motion stopped at $\sigma_z^{\lambda}$. Here, the sgn function is defined as
		$$
		\text{sgn}(x):=\left\{\begin{aligned}
			-1, & \text { for } x<0 \\
			0, & \text { for } x=0 \\
			+1, & \text { for } x>0
		\end{aligned}\right..
		$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:26:26.799649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1ddb6b11a81241c56314ec4f2261bc0cd796531db19ef9c6fcf461f84cd7c80","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:stepwise-advantage-amplification of arXiv:2608.06159, 'Majority Dynamics on Resampled Sparse Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi Graphs:\\[0.5ex] Gaussian Winner '] For every fixed \(K>0\), there are constants
