{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.081045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f26fa307dc226789b4cf07ed413aa85afe1059138ce937b62624c873083b45b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 6 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] Let $(X^n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of $\mathfrak{F}\text{-adapted}$ and $\mathbb{A}\text{-valued}$ processes. The sequence $(\mathbb{P}^n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ of laws of $(X^n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ on $\mathbb{D}([0,T];\mathbb{A})$ is tight if and only if the following conditions are satisfied:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[{\bf[T1]}] For every $t \in [0,T]$, the laws of the random variable $(X^n_t)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ form a tight sequence of law in $\mathbb{A}$. 
    \item[{\bf [T2]}] {For all $\epsilon>0$ and $\delta >0$, there exists} $\gamma_0 > 0$: $$\sup_{n\in \mathbb{N}}\mathbb{P}\Bigl( \bigl\{\omega_{[0,T],\mathbb{A}}(X^n,\gamma_0) > {\delta} \bigr\}\Bigr) \leq \epsilon,$$ where, for ${\bf v} \in \mathbb{D}([0,T],\mathbb{A})$ and $\gamma >0$,\begin{align}
        \omega_{[0,T],\mathbb{A}}({\bf v},\gamma):= \inf_{\Pi_{\gamma}}\max_{ r_i \in \tilde{\pi} }\sup_{r_i\leq s<t<r_{i+1}} \rho({\bf v}(t),{\bf v}(s)),
    \end{align}
$\Pi_{\gamma}$ denoting the set of all increasing sequences {$\tilde{\pi}=\{0=r_0<r_1<\cdots<r_n =T\}$ such that $r_{i+1}-r_i \geq \gamma, \, i=0,1,\cdots,n-1.$}    
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.999255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2736a756a64a49544ed715c37b4ba1a041e720cea7809704e2b2906f52a460b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:linearldp of arXiv:2604.17253, 'Large Deviations for the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation with Randomized Quasi-Periodic Ini'] Replacing \(u\) in (equation eq:rogue of the paper) by linear evolution (equation eq:linearsolution of the paper), we obtain the following large deviations principle:
