{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.355521+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f285f52061314827d518e8545f16a10b8e3b350fa93d1bfe00d08b320c6c45cf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem SupremumInequalityUnderMixing of arXiv:2412.01635, 'On the Weak Convergence of the Function-Indexed Sequential Empirical Process and its Smoot'] (Algebraic mixing conditions)\\

	Let $\mathcal F$ be a set of Borel maps $\mathcal X \to \mathbb R$ and assume the following conditions to hold:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[(i)]  There exist $\lambda> 0$ and an even integer $\nu \geq 2$ such that $$\zeta(\alpha^X,\lambda,\nu) = \sum_{s = 1}^\infty s^{\nu-2} \alpha^X(s)^{\frac{\lambda}{2+\lambda}} < \infty.$$

		\item[(ii)] For $\nu$ and $\lambda$ from (i), it holds $$ \int_0^1 \varepsilon^{-\frac{\lambda}{2+\lambda}} N_{[]}^{\frac{1}{\nu}}(\varepsilon, \mathcal F, \rho_2) d\varepsilon < \infty, $$ and for each $\varepsilon > 0$, the corresponding  ($\rho_2$-)set $\mathcal K$ can be chosen such that $$ \sup_{1 \leq t \leq n, n \in \mathbb N} \E{|b(X_{t,n})|^{l \frac{2+\lambda}{2}}}^{\frac12} \leq \varepsilon ~ \text{ for all } l = 2,...,\nu \text{ and } b \in \mathcal K. $$ 
	\end{itemize}

	Let $\rho(f) := \rho_{\nu(2+\lambda)/2}(f).$ There exists $\kappa > 0$ and a constant $C \geq 0$ that only depends on $\nu, \lambda$ and the mixing coefficients $(\alpha^X(t))_{t \in \mathbb N}$, such that, for each $\eta, \delta > 0$, $n \in \mathbb N$ and $1 \leq i \leq j \leq n$, with $m = j-i+1$, we have 
	\begin{align*}
		& \EOuter{ \sup_{\rho(f-g) \leq \delta} \left|S_{n,i,j}(f) - S_{n,i,j}(g)\right|^\nu} \nonumber \\  
		&\leq C \left[ m \left(N_{[]}^{\frac{2}{\nu}}\left(\eta, \mathcal F, \rho_2 \right) \left(m^{-\kappa} + \delta + \delta^{\frac{\nu}{2}} \right) + \int_0^{\eta} N_{[]}^\frac{1}{\nu}(\varepsilon, \mathcal F, \rho_2) \varepsilon^{-\frac{\lambda}{2+\lambda}} d\varepsilon \right)^2 \right]^{\frac{\nu}{2}}.
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615828+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f286ac4b5bf2ae64fde54b900a312faacb1fb334859ec52680d73331d1995801","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] For each~$t>0$, the weak limit \eqref{E:1.18} exists and defines an a.s.-finite random Borel measure~$Z_t$ on~$[0,1]$. Moreover, there exists a Borel probability measure~$\nu$ on~$\R^\N$ such that for all~$t>0$, all~$f\in\Cloc^+$ and all sequences $\{k_n\}_{n\in\N}$ with $k_n\to\infty$ and $n-k_n\to\infty$, 
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
E^\varrho\bigl(&\texte^{-\langle\zeta_{n,k_n}^{(t)},f\rangle}\bigr)
\\
&\,\,\,\underset{n\to\infty}\longrightarrow\,\, \E\biggl(\exp\Bigl\{-\int Z_t(\textd x)\otimes\texte^{-2\slb\, h}\textd h\otimes\nu(\textd\phi)(1-\texte^{-f(x,h,\phi)})\Bigr\}\biggr),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where the expectation on the right is with respect to the law of~$Z_t$. The measure $Z_t$ obeys \eqref{E:1.13},
$\nu$ is concentrated on~$\{\phi_0=0\}\cap
\bigcap_{x\in\N\smallsetminus \{0\}}\{\phi_x>0\}$ and the set $\{x\in\N\colon \phi_x\le a\}$ is finite~$\nu$-a.s.\ for each~$a>0$. A.e.\ sample of~$Z_t$ is diffuse (i.e., does not charge singletons). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.098225+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f286b6e93d2cc03b61f3245a460038e965619b60dd508a240315deb8de2184b2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Expected Population Evolution) of arXiv:2608.28198, 'Performative Privacy: When Differential Privacy Maximizes Utility'] We define the \textit{marginal leakage probability}, $p^{(t-1)}_\tau$, as the expected fraction of the population that breaches the privacy threshold $\tau$ at round $t-1$, i.e $p^{(t-1)}_{\tau} = \mathbb{E}(\mathds{1}(\Lambda(o_{t-1}|z) > \tau)$. 
Letting $\bar{z}$ denote the record subjected to the clipping and $\mu_{\bar z}$ denote the sample mean of clipped vectors, the marginal leakage probability evaluates to:
\begin{align}
p^{(t-1)}_\tau
&=
\bigint_{\mathbb{R}^{dN_{t-1}}}
\left[
1-\Phi\!\left(
\frac{\tau (N_{t-1}-1)\gamma}
{s\sqrt{N_{t-1}}}
-
\frac{s\sqrt{N_{t-1}}}
{2(N_{t-1}-1)\gamma}
\right)
\right]
\prod_{j=1}^{N_{t-1}} f_{\mathcal{N}}(z_j)\,
d z_{1:N_{t-1}},
\end{align}
Where $s\triangleq\|\bar z_1-\mu_{\bar z}\|_2$ and $d z_{1:N_{t-1}} \triangleq d z_1 \cdot\dots d {z_{N_{t-1}}}$.Under assumption \ref{assump:normal}, the expected population size at round $t$ in \Cref{alg:memoryless_dp}, is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \expectation[N_t \mid N_{t-1}] = N_{t-1} \Big( 1 + r - (q + r) p^{(t-1)}_\tau \Big)
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

