{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.619151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2f07d7682b3e9383fd97e066622ae18545f8cf69307fa1624b989444e227fa6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] Let $B, C \in \widehat{\Bcal}(S)$ be disjoint such that $\pbb(\nu(B) = 0) < 1$ and $\pbb(\nu(C) = 0) < 1$, and let $f_B$ and $f_C$ be sampling functions for $\xi$ on $B$ and $C$, respectively. Then, for some $p \in (0,1)$,
    \begin{align*}
        f_{B \cup C}(x) \coloneqq \begin{cases}
            f_B \left( \frac{x}{p}\right), &\text{for } x \in [0, p) \\
            f_C \left( \frac{x - p}{1 - p} \right), &\text{for } x \in [p, 1]
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    is a sampling function for $\xi$ on $B \cup C$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.352763+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2f372e7977cff3a0bf35d57bbecb44e152a9b60ca6a573ab7e2a93df6e07887","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:abstract-convergence of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Let $f_n:\mathbb{H}^{2+}_\uparrow \to \mathbb{R} \cup \{-\infty\}$ be a sequence of functions satisfying (equation E:MC-lambda of the paper) which converge uniformly on compact sets to continuous limit $f:\mathbb{H}^{2+}_\uparrow \to \mathbb{R}$ which also satisfies (equation E:MC-lambda of the paper). Suppose that additionally $f$ satisfies the following shape bound: 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item For all $c_0 > 0$, there exists a constant $C > 0$ such that for all $u = (x, a, t; y, b; t + s) \in \mathbb{H}^{2+}_\uparrow$ with $s < c_0$ we have that
		$$
		\left|f(u) + \frac{(x-y)^2}{s} \right|\le C s^{1/3} \log^2\left(\frac{{2\|u\|_2}}{s}\right).
		$$
	\end{itemize}
	Suppose $\pi_n$ is a sequence of continuous geodesics for $f_n$ between points $(u_n, s)$ to $(v_n, t)$, where the sequence $(u_n, s; v_n, t)$ is precompact in $\mathbb{H}^{2+}_\uparrow$.
	Then:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item The sequence $\pi_n$ is precompact in the uniform topology on functions from $[s, t] \to \Lambda$.
		\item If $\pi:[s, t] \to \Lambda$ is a subsequential limit of $\pi_n$, then $\pi$ satisfies (equation E:f-equality of the paper) for any finite set $\{s = t_0 < t_1 < \cdots < t_k = t\} \subset [s, t]$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.912368+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2fc0d5fb87f60e53236b0527ada3e2466ed0d1898083c78e5287c8adee451f5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop2 of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},P,T)$  be an ergodic measure preserving system, where $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$ is a standard measurable space. Then the following two statements are equivalent:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item there exists $B\in\mathcal{F}$ with $P(B)>0$ such that for any $C\in\mathcal{F}$ with $C\subset B$,
		\begin{equation}
			\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P^{1/2}(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P^{1/2}(B)P(C);
		\end{equation}
		\item $P$ is a periodic probability, i.e., there exist $r\in\mathbb{N}$ and distinct points $\omega_1,\ldots,\omega_r\in\Omega$ such that $P(\{\omega_i\})=\frac{1}{r}$, $i=1,2,\ldots,r$.
	\end{longlist}
	In this case, $r=\frac{1}{P(B)}\in\mathbb{N}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.474252+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2ff0cc57bef64bffb525ec609fe5dc7fd4f89a11febb3d60088b5674c783f26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:optimalliftgns of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a finite-dimensional von Neumann algebra. For a symmetric QMS generator $\mathcal{L}_O$ satisfying the $\kappa$-intertwining condition for $\kappa \in \mathbb{R}$, there exists a second-order lift $\mathcal{L}$ with convergence rate:
    \begin{align*}
         \Omega\left(\frac{\sqrt{\lambda_O}}{  1 +  \sqrt{\max\{0,-\kappa\}\lambda_O^{-1}}}\right) \le \nu(\mathcal{L}) \le \mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{\lambda_O}\right)\,.
    \end{align*}
    Such lift is optimal if $\max\{0,-\kappa\}\lambda_O^{-1} = \mathcal{O}(1)$, which always holds if $\kappa \ge 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.522155+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f300224a41601ab4ba50338dd3aecbc8f726c79e7578c5998281b1e5daa0fb50","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem T2.3 of arXiv:2512.18990, '\bf Hybrid Stochastic Functional Differential Equations with Infinite Delay: Approximation'] Under Assumptions (A2.1 of the paper) and (A2.2 of the paper), the SFDE ((sfde of the paper)) with the initial data ((id of the paper))  has a unique solution $x(t)$ on $t\in (-\8,\8)$, which has the property
