where $k = K_\ep(x)$, $B_x(\ep)$ is the closed ball of radius $\ep$ centered at $x$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.727532+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f303c098fbcbd11cd2daa366ed0e40a4776cd8dac018a9b58578e4b2148ff503","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:many-PDEs_new of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] Let $(u(t,\cdot))_{t>0} \subseteq  \mathcal P_* $ be a distributional solution to the FPE (equation eq:Main_FPE_our_paper of the paper) with coefficients $(a, b): \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal P_* \to \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^d$ for some $\mathcal P_* \subseteq \mathcal P_{\textup{ac}} $. Let $f: \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal D(f) \to \mathbb{R} $ with $\mathcal D(f) \subseteq \mathcal P_{\textup{ac}}$ such that $(u(t,\cdot))_{t >0} \subseteq\mathcal D(f)$,
        \begin{equation}
            f(x,u(t,\cdot)) + a(x,u(t,\cdot))u(t,x) \geq 0 \quad u(t,x)dxdt\text{-a.e.,}
        \end{equation}
        and \begin{equation}
            \big[(t,x)\mapsto f(x,u(t,\cdot))\big] \in L^1_{\textup{loc}}(\mathbb{R}_+;W^{1,1}_{\textup{loc}}(\mathbb{R}^d)).
        \end{equation}
        Then $(u(t,\cdot))_{t> 0}$ is a distributional solution to the FPE with new coefficients $(\hat{a},\hat{b}): \mathbb{R}^d \times \hat{\mathcal P}_*   \to \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^d$ given by 
        \begin{equation}
             \hat{a} (x,u) \coloneqq  a (x,u) + \frac{f(x,u)}{u(x)},\quad \text{and}\quad \hat{b} (x,u) \coloneqq  b (x,u) + \frac{\nabla f(x,u)}{u(x)},
        \end{equation}    
        on $\{u > 0\}$, and 
        $\frac {f(\cdot,u)} {u} := 0, \,\frac{\nabla f(\cdot, u)}{u} := 0$ on $\{u = 0\}$, defined on
        \begin{equation}
            \hat{\mathcal P}_* \coloneqq \mathcal P_* \cap \{ u \in \mathcal D(f): \, f(\cdot,u) \in W^{1,1}_{\textup{loc}}(\mathbb{R}^d)\}.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.607095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f30472665ae62f5b1711b5079efac49775fd90e31f4d6117b46eddfd4f1b7b1a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2506.08775, 'Markovian multivariate Hawkes population processes: Efficient evaluation of moments'] Fix $t\in\rr_+$, and let $\bm{Q}(0) = \bm{0}$, $\bm{\lambda}(0) = \bm{\overline{\lambda}}$.
Then, for any $\bm{z}\in[-1,1]^d$, $\bm{s}\in\rr_+^d$,
\begin{align}

    \zeta(t,\bm{s},\bm{z}) = \prod_{j=1}^d \exp\Big( - \overline{\lambda}_j \tilde{s}_j(t)- \overline{\lambda}_j\alpha_j \int_0^t \tilde{s}_j(v)\mathrm{d} v \Big).
\end{align}
Here, for $j\in[d]$ and $v\in[0,t]$, the functions $\tilde{s}_j(\cdot)$ satisfy, with boundary condition $\tilde{s}_j(0) = s_j$,
\begin{align}

    \frac{\mathrm{d} \tilde{s}_j(v)}{\mathrm{d} v} + \alpha_j\tilde{s}_j(v) + (1 + (z_j - 1)e^{-\mu_j v})\beta_j(\tilde{\bm{s}}(v)) - 1 = 0.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.351763+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3055799fae2ec86e418e53db592c3d914f335bf13e2f057cfedf2101eb114fb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2511.07350, 'Decoupling of clusters in independent sets in a percolated hypercube'] There is some $\gamma > 0$ such that for $p > \fr{1}{2} - \gamma$ the following holds.
There are random variables $\Psi^\cE, \Psi^\cO$, which can be expressed explicitly as functions of
the percolation configuration, such that
\begin{equation}
    \fr{\Cnt}{2^{2^{d-1}}} \psim e^{\Psi^\cE} + e^{\Psi^\cO}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, there are explicit deterministic quantities $\mu = \mu_{d,p}$ and $\sigma = \sigma_{d,p}$ (defined below)
such that
\begin{equation}
    \Rnd{ \fr{\Psi^\cE - \mu}{\sigma}, \fr{\Psi^\cO - \mu}{\sigma} }
