{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.058968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f314990e8a59044c435b3b8032db5b009bded4efa2ce62daf7912e09acb89030","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2606.19313, 'Stability of Khintchine-type inequalities via log-monotonicity'] Fix $p\geq 1$, and let $a,b\in \mathbb{R}^n$. Let $X$ be a random variable and $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n$ iid copies of $X$. We say $X$ has the \emph{Schur-concave property for $p$} if 

\begin{align*}
(a_1^2, a_2^2,\ldots, a_n^2)\prec (b_1^2, b_2^2, \ldots, b_n^2)\Longrightarrow \|  \sum_i a_i X_i \|_p\geq \|  \sum_i b_i X_i \|_p,
\end{align*}in which $\| \cdot \|_p=(\mathbb{E}|\cdot |^p)^{\frac{1}{p}}$. We say $X$ has the \emph{Schur-convex property for $p$}\footnote{The implication $a\prec b\Longrightarrow \|  \sum_i a_i X_i \|_p\leq \|  \sum_i b_i X_i \|_p$ always holds for $p\geq 1$; see [Chavez1], for example.} if 

\begin{align*}
(a_1^2, a_2^2,\ldots, a_n^2)\prec (b_1^2, b_2^2, \ldots, b_n^2)\Longrightarrow \|  \sum_i a_i X_i \|_p\leq \|  \sum_i b_i X_i \|_p.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.314252+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f31873ff621aaae7544780bb7b57b6bac70aedf18cd4751926e0093251010a9a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 13 (Variational identity) of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] Let $a > 0$ and $c > 0$. Then $X \lecx c\Lap$ holds for every $X \in \SE(1,a)$ if and only if
\begin{align*}
    p(1-p)\,\nu_{\max}(p,a) \le c\,\Lambda(p)
    \qquad \text{for all } p \in (0, 1/2],
\end{align*}
with $\Lambda$ as in \eqref{eqn:Lambda}. Consequently,
\begin{align}
    c^{\SE}(1, a) = \sup_{p \in (0,1/2]} R(p,a),
    \qquad
    R(p,a) \coloneqq \frac{(1-p)\,\nu_{\max}(p,a)}{1 + \log\frac{1}{2p}},

\end{align}
and the infimum in \eqref{eqn:defc} is a minimum: $c^{\SE}(1,a)$ is itself admissible.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.391109+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f319678434bac2df59071d0b33b0b8279fe8c6f9cec235e9846277ee14fa7494","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:uniqueness of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] For all $p > p_{c}$,
