 where the last product is interpreted as~$1$ when~$j=k+1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.498437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f343e4d93309868c71dff177c2fddb864053ced7e1da9f08025069e60a16b01c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2605.09706, 'A dyadic construction of a three-dimensional attractive point interaction Markov family'] Fix $T,\beta,\varepsilon>0$ and $\delta>0$. There exist constants
$C,c>0$, depending only on $(T,\beta,\varepsilon,\delta)$, such that the following hold.

\begin{enumerate} [(i)]
    \item For every $0\le s<t\le T$ and every $x\in E_\varepsilon$ satisfying
$|x|-\varepsilon\ge \delta$, we have
\begin{align}
