{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:28:33.870112+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f49a6885d506e55045e73526e83f93e47cf518b55b59e73598f87764d0756efe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:question of arXiv:2504.19367, 'Reduced Random Walks in the Hyperbolic Plane\textinterrobang'] {MR7929}}]
    Minkowski's question-mark function satisfies the following properties for all $x \in [0, 1]$.
    \begin{lemenum}
        \item The functional equation \[\?(x) = \begin{cases}
    0 & \mbox{if $x = 0$} \\\noalign{\vskip5pt}
    \displaystyle\frac{1}{2^{\lfloor 1 / x \rfloor}}\left(2 -  \?\left(\left\{\frac{1}{x}\right\}\right)\right) & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] from (def:question of the paper) holds (even if $x$ is irrational).
        \item If $x$ is a quadratic irrational, then $\?(x)$ is an explicitly computable rational number.
        \item We have $\?(1 - x) = 1 - \?(x)$.
