{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.802622+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4a2ae8e316e091ca3f6a4785e190ec594eb28bda3ad9043a6fc523148983ef8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm l:tail-and-comparison-mesh of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Let $t_0,t_1,\dots,t_{n+1}$ be as in (equation e:eps-mesh-times of the paper).
    For all sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$ and all $m\geqslant 0$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}\left(
            \max_{0\leqslant k_1 < k_2 \leqslant n+1}\,
            \sup_{z,w\in J^{\delta}}
            \left|\mathcal{L}(t_{k_1},z; t_{k_2},w) 
            - 
            \int_{t_{k_1}}^{t_{k_2}} d\mathcal{L} \circ \widehat{\Pi}_{(t_{k_1},z),(t_{k_2},w)}
            \right|
            > m \varepsilon^{10}
        \right)
        \leqslant Ce^{-cm^{6/7}},
    \end{align*}
    where $\widehat{\Pi}_{(t_{k_1},z),(t_{k_2},w)}$ is the path obtained by rounding the geodesic $\Pi_{(t_{k_1},z),(t_{k_2},w)}$ to $\varepsilon^{50}\mathbb{Z}$ as in (def:rounding of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.184519+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4a40847aa51b8b01ebae5d09b77093976e2452f7b48fed33263dedbd4dfcb85","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 6 of arXiv:2608.26974, 'Why not to use the Gaussian kernel'] Consider the Gaussian kernel in~\eqref{eq:gaussian} and $X_{n+1} = X_n \cup \{x_{n+1}\}$.
  Then
  \begin{equation*}
    \mathrm{cond}( \b{K}_{n+1} ) \geq \frac{1}{2} \bigg( \frac{\sqrt{2}(b-a)}{ \ell} \bigg)^{-2n} n! \quad \text{ and } \quad \mathrm{cond}( \b{K}_{n+1} ) \geq \frac{1}{2d} \bigg( \frac{\sqrt{2}(b-a)}{ \ell} \bigg)^{-2m} m!
  \end{equation*}
  in the settings of \Cref{thm:interpolation-1d} ($d = 1$) and \Cref{thm:interpolation} ($d \geq 1$), respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.231068+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4aa664519776fa32340a2c970c77f9efe8176a51cc36fd0bca83e72df1fcae8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 24 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] For the \textbf{TASEP height function} $(\hTASEP(\lambda;y,t))_{y\in \Z,t\geq 0}$, assume that $(0,0) \in \lambda$ for a fixed down-right path $\lambda \in \Lambda$. For all $t\geq 0$, consider the down-right path
\begin{equation}
\lambda_t= (z^{t}_i)_{i \in \Z} := \{ w \in \Z^2 \, \colon \,  T_{\lambda,w} \leq t < T_{\lambda,w+(1,1)} \} 
\end{equation} such that $z^{t}_{0}=(x_t,x_t)$ for some $x_t \in \Z$. For $y \in \Z$ and $t > 0$, set $h_t(0)=x_t$ and recursively
\begin{equation}
h_t(i) - h_t(i-1) = \begin{cases} -1 & \text{ if } z^{t}_i-z^{t}_{i-1} = \eone , \\
 0 & \text{ if } z^{t}_i-z^{t}_{i-1} = -\etwo . 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
 The \textbf{periodic TASEP height function} $\hpTASEP$ is defined as in \eqref{eq:CornerExtremalBasic} and \eqref{eq:CornerExtremalBasic2}, with respect to the periodic last passage times $\Tper$ from Definition~\ref{def:LastPassageTimes} and a down-right path $\lambda^{\per}_N \in \Lambda_{N,k}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.211653+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4ae21a8ecd3d0d9ac90a054571e45c01d05750ebd0cf0f50406fff1136c9afe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem convex-opt of arXiv:2608.29018, 'Sharp Restricted Isometry Thresholds for\\ Global Minima of Rank-Restricted Matrix LASSO'] For $X\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}\times d_{2}}$, let $X=U\Sigma V^{T}$
denote its compact SVD. Suppose there exists $W$ such that 
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{A}^{*}[\mathcal{A}(X)-b]+\lambda(UV^{T}+W)=0,\nonumber \\
U^{T}W=0,\quad WV=0,\quad\normop W\le1.
\end{gather}
Then $X$ is a global minimizer of ((eq:matrix-lasso of the paper)) for every
$r\ge\operatorname{rank}(X)$. If, in addition, 
