{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.328345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4d5eb87752a0ead55d2a39ab96842561c7672628633df89a7d85f0e69768782","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:free-energy-typical-truncation of arXiv:2411.09075, 'Weak Poincar{\'e} Inequalities, Simulated Annealing, and Sampling from Spherical Spin Glas'] There is a $H_{N,2}$-measurable event with probability $1-e^{-cN^{1/5}}$ on which the following holds.
    For
    \begin{equation}

        {\widetilde T} = {\widetilde T}(H_{N,\sim2}) \coloneqq \lt\{
            \sigma \in S_N :
            \la
                \Ind[|R(\sigma,\tau)| \ge N^{-2/5}]
                e^{H_{N,\sim2}(\tau)}
            \ra_2
            \le e^{N\xi_{\sim2}(1)/2 - cN^{1/5}}
        \rt\},
    \end{equation}
    where the Gibbs average is with respect to $\tau \sim \la \cdot \ra_2$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \E_{\sim2} \lt\la
            \Ind[\sigma \not\in {\widetilde T}] e^{H_{N,\sim2}(\sigma)}
        \rt\ra_2
        &\le e^{N\xi_{\sim2}(1)/2 - cN^{1/5}}, \\
        \E_{\sim2} \lt\la
            \Ind[\sigma \not\in {\widetilde T}]
        \rt\ra_2
        &\le e^{-cN^{1/5}}, \\
        \E_{\sim2} \lt\la
            \Ind[\sigma^1 \not\in {\widetilde T}, |R(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)| \le 3N^{-2/5}]
            e^{H_{N,\sim2}(\sigma^1) + H_{N,\sim2}(\sigma^2)}
        \rt\ra_2
        &\le e^{N\xi_{\sim2}(1) - cN^{1/5}}, \\
        \E_{\sim2} \lt\la
            \Ind[\sigma^1 \not\in {\widetilde T}, |R(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)| \le 3N^{-2/5}]
            e^{H_{N,\sim2}(\sigma^2)}
        \rt\ra_2
        &\le e^{N\xi_{\sim2}(1)/2 - cN^{1/5}}.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.612327+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4d876f0a6e6a88fca48ae83b59375fff95fa88bd296152a254fc9735f640165","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2510.27451, 'Bi-martingale optimal transport and its applications'] The set of optimal bi-martingale plans in the quadratic case equals,
	\begin{equation}

		\ov{\Gamma Q}(\mu,\nu) = \Big\{ (\gamma,q) \,  : \,  \gamma \in \ov\Gamma(\mu,\nu),  \ \  q = \ov{\zeta} \gamma  \Big\},
	\end{equation}
	where $\ov\zeta$ and $\ov{\Gamma}(\mu,\nu)$ can be defined for any solution $\ov{u}$ of the Zolotarev problem \eqref{eq:Z2_again}. In particular, the set  $\ov{\Gamma}(\mu,\nu)$ is non-empty, and it does not depend on the choice of the possibly non-unique maximizer~$\ov{u}$.

	Moreover, for any $\gamma \in \ov{\Gamma}(\mu,\nu)$, 
	\begin{align}

		Z_2(\mu,\nu) &= \int_X \! \int_Y \frac{1}{2} \Big( \big| \ov{\zeta}(x,y) - x \big|^2 + \big| \ov{\zeta}(x,y) - y \big|^2  \Big)   \gamma(dxdy), \\

		\mathcal{C}(\mu,\nu) &= \int_X \! \int_Y   \big| \ov{\zeta}(x,y) \big|^2 \gamma(dxdy).
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.956896+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4ddcee765475b441889961eae5982e65664db253fb4bcda679df28875734d8d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stable-four-dimensional-restated of arXiv:2608.23135, 'Graph distance and effective resistance of the random walk trace in four and five dimensio'] Recall that $b_X$ is the constant in (equation Eq2 of the paper). Then
