{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.477491+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5d46b14b4d0c50f28d32e9b797acd1afd43799c529a73a867637f50d7ef2b82","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:simplex of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] The convex set $K$ is a \emph{simplex}

\footnote{Simplices are called \emph{Choquet simplices} in [winkler85].}

if the convex cone 
\[
\{\lambda(x,1): \lambda\geq 0, x\in K\}\subset V\times\mathbb{R}
\] 
is a lattice in its own order [phelps01]. A \emph{$k$-simplex}, $k\in\mathbb{N}_0$,
is defined as the convex hull of any $k+1$ affinely independent points.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:54:15.658769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5d7dbe991cd9de17df3e5758031098a29654b82d9a3ae881f0d264efc678b51","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2608.09419, 'Left-tail expansions for Schr\"oder branching processes with explicit convergence rates'] \lb{T4} Suppose that the probability generating function $P(z)$ satisfies $P(0)=0$ and $0<p:=P'(0)<1$. Then, the unique solution of the Shr\"oder functional equation 
\[\lb{118}
 \Phi(P(z))=p\Phi(z),\ \ \ \Phi(0)=0,\ \ \ \Phi'(0)=1
\]
exists in some neighborhood of $z=0$. It can be extended analytically to the open connected component of the filled Julia set $\cJ_P(0)$. For its computation, one may use
\[\lb{118a}
\Phi(z)=\lim_{t\to+\iy}p^{-t}\underbrace{P\circ...\circ P}_{t}(z).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:36:49.941522+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5d82174081446a5de6a0fe9d6fcbce7eeb03655df14a26a3dd1a5d165f17093","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn restricted of arXiv:2609.01745, 'The Directional Localization Game on Graphs'] Given a tree decomposition $(T, \mathcal{B})$ of width $k$, we call $(T, \mathcal{B})$ a \textit{restricted smooth tree decomposition} of $G$ if:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\Delta(T) \le 3$;
\item $\left \vert B_a \right \vert \in \{k, k+1\}$ for all $a \in V(T)$; and
\item $\left \vert B_a \cap B_b \right \vert = k$ for all $ab \in E(T)$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.659335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5d997162c9f549ac0a5ce75d764f586d3959a7b7e9b5ca69b18e00078d4304e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 22 (Approximate scaling of the comparison model) of arXiv:2604.23461, 'Scaling limit of Sinkhorn-rescaled Random Matrices \\ via Stability of Static Schrödinger '] Let $(\r, \c)$ be an $m \times n$ positive margin. Under Assumption \ref{assump:random_matrix}, let $(\balpha, \bbeta)$ denote the deterministic Schr\"{o}dinger potentials scaling $\bLambda$ to $(\r, \c)$, and let $\hat{\X}^{\r,\c} = \D(e^{\balpha})\X\D(e^{\bbeta})$ be the comparison model. Define the variance and scale proxies
	\begin{align}
		V_{r} := n\sigma^2 e^{4K}\,\frac{N^2}{\|\bLambda\|_1^2}, \qquad V_{c} := m\sigma^2 e^{4K}\,\frac{N^2}{\|\bLambda\|_1^2}, \qquad B := e^{2K}\,\frac{N R}{\|\bLambda\|_1}.
	\end{align}
	Then, for every $\eps > 0$, $i \in [m]$, and $j \in [n]$,
	\begin{align}
		\P\!\left( \bigl|r_i(\hat{\X}^{\r,\c}) - \r(i)\bigr| \ge \eps\, \r(i) \right) &\le 2\exp\!\left( -\frac{\eps^2 \r(i)^2 / 2}{V_r + B\,\eps\, \r(i)} \right), \\

		\P\!\left( \bigl|c_j(\hat{\X}^{\r,\c}) - \c(j)\bigr| \ge \eps\, \c(j) \right) &\le 2\exp\!\left( -\frac{\eps^2 \c(j)^2 / 2}{V_c + B\,\eps\, \c(j)} \right).
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.721453+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5dd9a183f7ea948b8ec01ec3b032863b90bdc649ff9d62258eb1ded5ff4b5cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 31 of arXiv:2511.13404, 'Asymptotic stability and mean ergodicity of Feller processes on Polish spaces'] Let $\{\xi_t\}_{t\ge 0}$ be a family of random variables and $g:\R_+\rightarrow\R_+$ satisfying $\lim\limits_{a\to\infty}g(a)/a=\infty$. If 
    \begin{equation}
        \sup_{t\ge0}\E(g(|\xi_t|))<\infty,
    \end{equation}
then $\{\xi_t\}_{t\ge 0}$ is uniformly integrable. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.441717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5e0fad04444ec23b8c3bf8914e4de1b4379b17ff9cde862f4c04bc068e55f18","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2604.07478, 'Mixing Times and Cutoff for the Rook's Walk'] Let $\varphi$ be defined as in Definition \ref{defphi}. Then,
   \[\varphi(i) = {d \choose i}(n-1)^i\]
   \begin{proof}
      Fix $i \in \{0,\dots,d\}$. Now, $\varphi(i)$ counts the number of elements in $\mathbb{Z}_n^d$ with Hamming distance of exactly $i$ from $\hat{1}$. That is, the number of elements with $i$ non-one elements. There are ${d \choose i}$ different choices for which dimensions should be non-one, and $(n-1)$ choices for each of these dimensions. 
   \[\therefore \quad \varphi(i) = {d \choose i}(n-1)^i,\quad \forall i \in \{0,\dots,d\}\]
   \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.264955+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5e57e09dc31d98fd9d703ba38e4442cd158925e18fc93c97ed22bcd57c00b77","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2604.19924, 'Fluctuation theory for spectrally negative Lévy processes killed by additive functionals'] For {$q\geq0$} and $x \in (c,b)$, we have 
		\begin{align}
			\E_x\left[e^{-q\tau_b^+-\sum_{i=1}^np_iL^{a_i}_{\tau_b^+}};\tau_b^+<\tau_c^-\right]=\frac{W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(x,c)}{W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(b,c)},

		\end{align}	
		and	
		\begin{align}
			\E_x\left[e^{-q\tau_c^--\sum_{i=1}^np_iL^{a_i}_{\tau_c^-}};\tau_c^-<\tau_b^+\right]=Z^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(x,c)-\frac{W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(x,c)}{W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(b,c)}Z^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(b,c).
		\end{align}	
		{Additionally, for $x\in(c,b)$ and any bounded measurable function $f:\R\to\R$,
			\begin{align}
				\E_x\Bigg[\int_0^{\tau_b^+\wedge\tau_c^-}&e^{-qt-\sum_{i=1}^np_iL^{a_i}_{t}}f(X_t)dt\Bigg]\notag\\
				&=\int_c^bf(y)\left(\frac{W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(x,c)}{W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(b,c)}W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(b,y)-W^{(q,p_1,\dots,p_n)}_{(a_1,\dots,a_n)}(x,y)\right)dy. 
		\end{align}} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.444639+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5e8be5299da23729178990368ab1f4167c1278061cbef636221e3a493a07a8c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Wells' disorder of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Given an integer $L \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}}$, an integer $n \geq 2$, we define the two Wells' disorders: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Given an inverse temperature $\beta \geq 0$, we define the Wells' disorder associated with the model~(equation def:XYgeneralpercsite of the paper): for any $r \in \{0,1\}^{\Lambda_L},$
    \begin{equation*}
        \nu_{L , \beta} \left( \left\{ r \right\} \right) := \frac{Z_{\Lambda_L,\beta, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}}}{\bar{Z}_{\Lambda_L,\beta}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}}} ~~\mbox{with}~~ \bar{Z}_{\Lambda_L,\beta}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}} := \sum_{r \in \{0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda_L} } Z_{\Lambda_L,\beta, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}}.
    \end{equation*}
    \item Given two inverse temperatures $\beta_1 , \beta_2 \geq 0$,  we define the Wells' disorder associated with the model~(equation def:IVGFFmultigeneral of the paper): for any $r \in \{0,1\}^{\Lambda_L},$
    \begin{equation*}
        \nu_{L , n, \beta_1 , \beta_2} \left( \left\{ r \right\} \right) := \frac{Z_{\Lambda_L , n, \beta_1 , \beta_2, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}}}{\bar{Z}_{\Lambda_L , n, \beta_1 , \beta_2}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}}} ~~\mbox{with}~~ \bar{Z}_{\Lambda_L , n, \beta_1 , \beta_2}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}} := \sum_{r \in \{0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda_L} } Z_{\Lambda_L , n, \beta_1 , \beta_2, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}}.
    \end{equation*}
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.776930+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5eaf049cc8b64d7cadbb1e8d334413ff48fb5c1b5dbf93d169292fac1695470","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 5 of arXiv:2603.25459, 'Cram\'{e}r-type moderate deviation for double index permutation statistics'] Assume $W$ is defined as (\ref{statistic-mann-whitney-wilcoxon}), we have
\begin{align}
     \left|\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(W>z\right)}{1-\Phi(z)}-1\right| = O(1)(1+z^3)/(1/n_{1}+1/n_{2})^{1/2},
\end{align}
for $0\leq z \leq \min(n_{1},n_{2})^{1/6}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.379306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5eb74fe66b248fdb9fc6353263533064b315cd0489cf45a8072b9918314d869","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 19 (Safe subdivision) of arXiv:2607.27535, 'Universal topological statistics on \\ triangulated singular spaces'] Let $\phi: K \rightarrow M$ be a purely $d$-dimensional $C^2$ triangulation of a compact set $M \subset \RR^D$. Let $\eta: K \rightarrow L$ be a subdivision of simplicial complexes and let $\psi = \phi \circ \eta^{-1} : L \rightarrow M$ be an induced triangulation. Assume that $\Theta_{\phi} > 0$, and denote $c_\phi = 2/\sin(\Theta_\phi/4)$. Then there exists $r_0 > 0$ such that the following holds for all $r \in [0, r_0]$:
    \begin{align*}
        \bigcup_{\substack{\tau_i \neq \tau_j \in L \\ \dim \tau_i = \dim \tau_j = d}}\ball(\tilde\tau_i, r) \cap \tilde \tau_j \subseteq \ball(\partial_{\psi} M, c_{\phi} r)
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.741922+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5ec3a03bb01475a9197377a1fd886cd2398005a5bebb0a0370ce8374fbead58","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lower_bound_tail_vertex_degrees of arXiv:2606.09386, 'High-degree vertices in uniform recursive directed acyclic graphs with freezing'] Let $n\in\mathbb{N}$, recall $\theta$ from~(equation eq:theta of the paper), and suppose the choice sequence $\mathbf x$ satisfies Assumption~$\hyperref[ass:A]{\mathbf{x}}$(item:geq1 of the paper) and $A_i\geq m+1$ for $i\in\{I,\ldots,n\}$ with $I\in[n]$. For any $d_{a_1},\ldots,d_{a_k},d_{f_1},\ldots,d_{f_\ell} \in [n-1]$,
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
&\P{\deg_n(a_v)\geq d_{a_v}\text{ for all }v\in[k],\, \deg_n(f_w)\geq d_{f_w}\text{ for all }w\in[\ell]} \\
