{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.639117+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f604652364758d42a97508cc7a78552af3c2fbbb55b8a39da60b6528268e242c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 15 (Sub/Super-harmonicity in the weak sense) of arXiv:2506.17979, '\large\bf A remark on subharmonicity for symmetric Dirichlet forms'] {\rm Let $D$ be an open set in $E$. We say that a nearly Borel
$\overline{\R}$-valued function $u$ defined on $E$ is \emph{subharmonic {\rm(}resp.~superharmonic{\rm)} in $D$ in the weak sense} if $u$ is q.e.~finely continuous on $D$ and for any relatively compact open subset $U$ with $U\Subset D$, $\mathbb{E}_x[|{u}|(X_{\tau_U})]<\infty$ for q.e.~$x\in E$ and ${u}(x)\leq\mathbb{E}_x[{u}(X_{\tau_U})]$ (resp.~${u}(x)\geq\mathbb{E}_x[{u}(X_{\tau_U})]$) holds for q.e.~$x\in E$.
A nearly Borel measurable $\overline{\R}$-valued function $u$ on $E$ is said to be \emph{harmonic in $D$ in the weak sense} if $u$ is both superharmonic and subharmonic in $D$ in the weak sense.
If $u$ is finely continuous (nearly) Borel $\overline{\R}$-valued function on $D$ and for any relatively compact open subset $U$ with $U\Subset D$, $\mathbb{E}_x[|{u}|(X_{\tau_U})]<\infty$ for all $x\in E$, and ${u}(x)\leq\mathbb{E}_x[{u}(X_{\tau_U})]$ holds  for all $x\in E$, then $u$ is called \emph{subharmonic in $D$ in the weak sense without exceptional set}.
The \emph{subharmonicity/harmonicity in $D$ in the weak sense without exceptional set} is analogously defined.
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.650454+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6075e2bc5cb6915453b48e7f58e709ecf822b1229e160a0e90a20856cc50769","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:bw of arXiv:2607.06249, 'Extreme least singular values of Gaussian row submatrices and a phase retrieval stability '] Let $A_m\in\mathbb{R}^{(2m-1)\times m}$ have independent standard Gaussian entries. Then
\begin{equation}
  \frac{1}{m}\log\omega(A_m)\xrightarrow{P}-\log4,
\end{equation}
with the rate
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{P}\left\{\left|\frac{1}{m}\log\omega(A_m)+\log4\right|>\varepsilon\right\}=O_\varepsilon(m^{-1})
\end{equation}
for every fixed $\varepsilon>0$. Equivalently, $\omega(A_m)=4^{-m+o_P(m)}$.

Let $R_m=\max_{1\le i\le 2m-1}\|a_i\|$. For every $b>1/4$, $\mathbb{P}\{\omega(A_m)\le R_m b^m\}\to1$. For
every $b<1/4$ and every fixed $C<\infty$, $\mathbb{P}\{\omega(A_m)\le C R_m b^m\}\to0$. Thus the Gaussian
critical base in the Balan--Wang question is $1/4$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.986314+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6082100e78d36d1ec41191c4564713db0d55a8e9bd9276a9e7a9e754d2d714c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:correction_exact_derivative of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] If $y\in \mathbb Z^d$ and $r\geq 1$ then 
  \begin{multline*}
\frac{1}{\beta_c|J'(r)|} \frac{d}{dr} \mathscr{D}_r^{(1)}(0,y)=
2\mathbb E_r \sum_{x\in K} \sum_{z \in B_r(x)} \mathbbm{1}(0\nleftrightarrow z) |K_z| (\mathbb E|K_y|-\mathbbm{1}(y\nleftrightarrow 0,z)|K_y| )
\\+
\mathbb E_r \sum_{x\in K} \sum_{z\in K_y} \mathbbm{1}(0\nleftrightarrow y, \|x-z\| \leq r) |K||K_y|
\end{multline*}
and
\begin{align*}
  \frac{1}{\beta_c|J'(r)|}\frac{d}{dr} \mathscr{D}_r^{(2)}(0,y) &= 
  \mathbb E_r \sum_{x\in K}\sum_{z\in B_r(x)} \mathbbm{1}(0\nleftrightarrow z)(|K_z|^2+2|K||K_z|) ( \mathbb E_r|K_y|-\mathbbm{1}(y\nleftrightarrow 0,z)|K_y|)\\ &\hspace{2.7cm}+ \mathbb E_r \sum_{x\in K} \sum_{z\in B_r(x)} \mathbbm{1}(0\nleftrightarrow z)|K_z|  (\mathbb E_r|K_y|^2-\mathbbm{1}(y\nleftrightarrow 0,z)|K_y|^2)\\
  &\hspace{2.7cm}+\mathbb E_r \sum_{x\in K} \sum_{z\in K_y} \mathbbm{1}(0\nleftrightarrow y, \|x-z\| \leq r) |K||K_y|^2.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.612279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f60ce484550f757b1f8c23ade17c66e183c6320bf9e5aa1754aa6efb709a8ffd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2506.09264, 'The bunkbed conjecture still holds for cactus graphs\\ and for graphs with certain biconne'] Let \(G\) be a graph and \(\vec{p} = (p_e)_{e\in E(G)} \in [0,1]^{E(G)}\) be a percolation vector.\\
Then, the statement that
\begin{center}\(\forall T\subseteq V(G)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v,w\in V(G)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(G, T, \vec{p}, v, w)\) holds\end{center}
is equivalent to
\begin{center}\(\forall H\in\mathbb{B}(G)\)\emph{: } \(\forall T\subseteq V(H)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v,w\in V(H)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(H, T, (p_e)_{e\in E(H)}, v, w)\) holds.\end{center}
In particular, any given graph satisfies the \emph{(}weak/strong\emph{)} bunkbed conjecture if and only if all of its biconnected components satisfy the \emph{(}weak/strong\emph{)} bunkbed conjecture.\vspace{6pt} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.721730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f60d6461f7c53f0316db1a2c318039624cbe8e28c88bcac4f4844f4bc65dd3f2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2608.29747, 'Reduced Khovanov-Rozansky homology'] \cite{soergel1990kategorie}
In the category of Soergel bimodules $\mathrm{SBim}_n$, we have the following isomorphisms:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\B_i\B_j \cong \B_j\B_i$ for all $i, j$ with $|i-j| > 1$,
    \item $\B_i\B_{i+1}\B_i \oplus \B_{i+1} \cong \B_{i+1}\B_i\B_{i+1} \oplus \B_i$, and
    \item $\B_i^2 \cong (q+q^{-1}) \B_i$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.141680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f60eb92fbc623f50e254dcf98fc6420b79aeffa1cfe2d77294ab01a5087aeab4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (Bernoulli width dominates the rate--distortion area) of arXiv:2608.11031, 'A Bayesian Proof of the Bernoulli Theorem'] For every finite $T\subset\mathbb R^n$ and every 
