{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.102529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f614085969e971f0c8a02d1451bdf2889e10b4e3a7186b30f7b7bcfecf5e0128","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem THM41 of arXiv:2412.08886, 'On domination for (non-symmetric) Dirichlet forms'] Let $({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$ and $({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$ be two quasi-regular Dirichlet forms on $L^2(E,m)$ such that ${\mathscr E}'\preceq {\mathscr E}$, and let $(\tilde{{\mathscr E}},\tilde{{\mathscr F}})$ denote the unique Dirichlet form obtained in Theorem~(THM24 of the paper).  Then, there exists a Borel right process $X$ properly associated with $({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$,  a finely open {Borel set $G$} with respect to $X$ satisfying $m(E\setminus G)=0$,  and an MF $M\in \text{MF}_+(X^G)$, where $X^G$ is the part process of $X$ on $G$, such that
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] $X^G$ is properly associated with $(\tilde{{\mathscr E}},\tilde{{\mathscr F}})$.
\item[(2)] The subprocess of $X^G$ killed by $M$ is properly associated with $({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$.  
