{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.712633+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6548acc27fcd466ae7571406470574a090239b10a3d4b2748a609658a974f25","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Coupling of arXiv:2411.04778, 'Coupling between Brownian motion and random walks on the infinite percolation cluster'] Let $(\mathcal{X},\mu)$ and $(\mathcal{Y},\nu)$ be two probability spaces. A coupling of $(\mu,\nu)$ is a pair of random variables $(X,Y)$ living in a common probability space $(\mathcal{X}\times \mathcal{Y}, \mathbb{P})$, such that $\mathrm{Law}(X)=\mu$ and $\mathrm{Law}(Y)=\nu$. The collection of all the couplings is then denoted by
