where the ratio is interpreted by continuity as $T$ when $p_\theta(x,\tau)=0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.328642+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70b3853ecc924d03a28a9fed8e8d22ebe42284a3e1953ef66022d2edcbde331","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:kipnisvaradhan of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Let $(X_t)_{t\ge 0}$ be a Markov process evolving on a state-space $\mathscr{E}$, with generator $\mathscr{L}$ and invariant measure $\pi$. Then, there exists a universal constant $C_4>0$ such that for any smooth mean-zero function $f:[0,T]\times\mathscr{E}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$, we have 
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[ \sup_{0\le t\le T}\left(\int_0^t f(s,X_s)\,\mathrm{d} s\right)^2\right] \le C_4\int_0^T \|f(t,\cdot )\|_{-1,\pi}^2\,\mathrm{d} t
    \end{equation*}
    where the variational norm $\|\cdot\|_{-1,\pi}$ is defined by
    \begin{equation*}
        \| f\|_{-1,\pi}^2 = \sup_g\left\lbrace 2 \langle f,g\rangle_\pi -\langle g,(-\mathscr{L})g\rangle_\pi \right\rbrace.
    \end{equation*}
    Above, the supremum is taken over all local functions $g:\mathscr{E}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$, and $\langle\cdot ,\cdot\rangle_\pi$ stands for the scalar product in $L^2(\pi )$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.122691+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70c8752fc283a433a9ce4a013de9c39c5c4afab6fd32f657ddc6bbf8985eec8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 24 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Assume that the pair $(C,f)$ satisfies the Fredholm equation (\ref{main-Fredeq}) with the normalization condition (\ref{main-Frednorm}). Then the function $f$ satisfies the growth condition (\ref{main-SLgrowth}). More precisely, we have
\begin{align}

f(x)= h(x)+C\mp \frac{1}{\P_0[X_1^2]}\int_K yf (y)\,\mu(dy)+o(1)\qquad \text{as}\ x\to \pm \infty.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.629495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70dd960efba586de3a8982fd21e0e6897d4b22c059f4fc1f2c610da991198e9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_drift_como_conv of arXiv:2411.03609, 'Asymptotically optimal Wasserstein couplings for the small-time stable domain of attractio'] \noindent\normalfont{(a)} Let $\alpha \in (1,2)$, and $p,\delta>0$ where $p\ne\alpha-1$ and $\delta\ne(\alpha-1)/\alpha$. Then, under Assumptions~(\nameref{asm:S}) \& (\nameref{asm:C}), we have, as $t \downarrow 0$,
    \begin{align*}
        |\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{S}^t}-\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{Z}}|&=\mathcal{O}\big(G_2(t)+t^{1-1/\alpha}G(t)^{-1} 
    +(1+G_1(t))t^{\min\{1-1/\alpha,\delta\}}
    \\
    &\qquad + G(t)^{p}(1+G_1(t))^{1+p}t^{\min\{1-1/\alpha,p/\alpha\}}\big).
    \end{align*}
\noindent\normalfont{(b)} Let $\alpha \in (0,1)$. Then, under Assumptions~(\nameref{asm:S}) \& (\nameref{asm:C}) we have, as $t \downarrow 0$, 
\begin{equation*}
|\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{S}^t}-\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{Z}}|
=\mathcal{O}\big(G_2(t)+t^{p/\alpha}G(t)^{p} +t^{\delta}\big).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.576788+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70e8d9346274738a0dba08cc7e64728eb558861fef8f57615b0745632a38d1e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:balan of arXiv:2602.05317, 'Strong local nondeterminism for stochastic time-fractional\\ slow and fast diffusion equat'] For all $t > 0$, $a \in [b, \infty) \subseteq (0, \infty)$, and $H \in (0,1)$, there exist strictly
  positive constants $C^{(1)}$ and $C^{(2)}$ depending on $t$, $b$, and $\eta$, such that
  \begin{align}
    \frac{C^{(2)}}{t \sqrt{a^2 + t^2}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{|\tau/t|^{1 - 2H}}{\tau^2 + a^2 + t^2} \;\ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } \tau
    \leq A_{\eta}(t,a) \leq
    \frac{C^{(1)}}{t \sqrt{a^2 + t^2}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{|\tau/t|^{1 - 2H}}{\tau^2 + a^2 + t^2} \; \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } \tau.
  \end{align}
  In particular, when $\eta = 0$, inequality~(equation E:balan of the paper) holds for all $a \in (0, \infty)$, with constants $C^{(1)}$ and $C^{(2)}$ depending only on $t$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.223215+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70fbe435a238cf294e14edea7a7bd1b4612de892f6efaea69a8664800350892","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 12 (Parametrization of the unique toroidal limit) of arXiv:2609.00501, 'Uniqueness of Finite-Time Varifold Limits for the M\"obius-Invariant Willmore Flow'] Assume the hypotheses of Corollary~\ref{cor:classification} and suppose that
$\Var_T\neq0$ and $\spt\Var_T$ has genus one.  Then there exists a
uniformly conformal bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism
\[
 f\in W^{2,2}(\Sigma,\R^4)\cap W^{1,\infty}(\Sigma,\R^4),
 \qquad
 f\colon\Sigma\longrightarrow M:=\spt\Var_T,
\]
and there are a smooth zero-scalar-curvature, unit-volume metric
$g_{\mathrm{poin}}$ on $\Sigma$ and a function $u\in L^\infty(\Sigma)$
such that
\begin{equation}

 f^*g_{\mathrm{euc}}=e^{2u}g_{\mathrm{poin}}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

 \Var_T=v(M,1),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

 \lVert\Var_T\rVert
 =f_\#\mu_{f^*g_{\mathrm{euc}}}
 =\mathcal H^2\llcorner M.
\end{equation}
In particular, formula \eqref{eq:limit-measure} is independent of the sequence
used to construct the parametrization $f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.271439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f710f255b13ebcc1e84354b775629b966bc938d2b6ec605ac61e272e694ac87b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 (Resolution invariance) of arXiv:2609.01116, 'Vector-Carleson, Calder\'on, and Poisson--Atomic Criteria for\\ Generalized Hilbert Operat'] Let $2<p<\infty$ and let $\sigma$ be an $(L,M,p)$-admissible analytic
resolution. Then
\[
 \boxed{
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma}:\ell^p\to H^p\|
 \asymp_{p,L,M}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}:\ell^p\to H^p\|.}
 \tag{3.10}
\]
For the nonconstant coordinate tails,
\[
\begin{split}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma,>N}\|
 &\lesssim_{p,L,M}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{>N-L}\|,\\
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{>N}\|
 &\lesssim_{p,L}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma,>N-L}\|,
\end{split}
 \tag{3.11}
\]
where a negative cutoff means the full nonconstant synthesis.
Consequently,
\[
 \|T_\phi\|_{\mathrm e}
 \asymp_{p,L,M}
 \lim_{N\to\infty}\|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma,>N}\|,
 \qquad
 T_\phi\text{ is compact}
 \iff
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma,>N}\|\longrightarrow0.
 \tag{3.12}
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.781170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f71481e9a4b87299c3c4f2780c3e173e7aaacc8c16a06cf6b86e774cc86c1895","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lm of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] For any $f, g\in H_0^{1,1}(0,\infty)$, the quadratic form,
    \begin{equation}
        \langle f, \mathcal{H}_{\beta}(t) g\rangle,
    \end{equation}
