{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.618282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f81f5c945e6d305b8346ba6639c78305a74ec4c67ac18d55aea0b5439ea6291e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] Let $\mathbf{A} = (A, N)$ be a random element in $\Rcal$ such that $\pbb(N = n) > 0$ for every $n \in \nbb$. Then $\mathbf{A}$ satisfies
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item (Finite exchangeability) For all $n \geq 1$ and all permutations $\pi$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$:
        \begin{equation*}
            A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A \circ \pi \mid (N = n)
        \end{equation*}
        \item (Compatibility) For all $n \geq 1$ and all $m \geq n$:
        \begin{equation*}
            A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A[n] \mid (N = m)
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}
    if and only if there exist an infinite jointly exchangeable matrix $A^{(\infty)} \in \rbb^{\nbb \times \nbb}$ such that for all $n \geq 1$:
    \begin{equation} 
        A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A^{(\infty)}[n]
    \end{equation}
    In that case, $A^{(\infty)}$ is unique in distribution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.317682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8214779b5c585ce70b8ab15fe9f4247f84b5ef26dec1f0fd44eb292c016b14e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:592 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] \textnormal{(i) (Lower bound)} There exist $c=c(u_1)>0$ and $n_1(u_1)>0$, such that for all $z\in S_i$ and $n>n_1(u_1)$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}_z\Big(\tau_{T_{i,j}}<\tau_{\partial \widetilde{B}(z)}\Big)>\frac{c}{n}.
    \end{equation}
    \textnormal{(ii) (Upper bound)} There exist $c'=c'(u_1)>0$ and $n_2(u_1)>0$, such that for $z\inS_i$, $z'\in\widetilde{B}(z)$ and $n>n_2(u_1)$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}_{z'}\Big(\tau_{T_{i,j}}<\tau_{\partial \widetilde{B}(z)}\Big)<\frac{c'(\log(r(z')/|z'-|z'|v_{i,j}|)+1)}{n}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.889056+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f828198881cebaaf9ab2f98882e4793468e2c5b13176240406ef73feffd3c533","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 37 (Ergodicity of the Palm version) of arXiv:2504.02435, 'Poisson-Voronoi percolation in higher rank'] Let $G$ be a non-compact connected semisimple real Lie group and $(X,d_X)$ be its symmetric space. Let $\mathbf Y$ be a Poisson point process on $X$ with intensity $c \, {\rm vol}$ for some $c>0$ and with iid ${\rm Unif}[0,1]$-marks. Let $\mathbf Y_0=\mathbf Y \cup \{\mathbf o\}$, where $\mathbf o$ is equipped with an independent ${\rm Unif}[0,1]$-mark. Then the following hold:
\begin{itemize} 
\item[{\rm(1)}] Let $A$ be a $G$-invariant event and let $A_0$ be the restriction of $A$ to configurations containing the origin. Then $\mathbb P(\mathbf Y_0\in A_0)=\mathbb P(\mathbf Y\in A)\in \{0,1\}$.
\item[{\rm(2)}] Let $B_0$ be an event such that $B_0=B'$, where $B'$ is the restriction of the event $B:=GB_0$ to configurations that contain the origin.
Then $\mathbb P(\mathbf Y_0\in B_0)=\mathbb P(\mathbf Y\in B)$. In particular, $\mathbb P(\mathbf Y_0\in B_0)\in\{0,1\}$.
\end{itemize}
The same conclusions hold when $\mathbf Y$ is replaced with the unmarked Poisson point process $Y$ and $\mathbf Y_0$ is replaced with $Y_0=Y\cup\{o\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.608422+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f82a0995c26fa2959e12f73a10473060b55dc6064e8d0c01f74322259347e542","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] An abstract operator algebra, or $C^*$-algebra, is a complex algebra $A$ having a norm $||.||$ and an involution $*$, subject to the following conditions:
