{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:20:55.482891+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f86544eba372a9c4f37270d700c5b05984fb1dab6a1c0f34a64253b3444a2733","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 1.3 of arXiv:2411.07158, 'Markov chains on trees: almost lower and upper directed cases'] Consider  ${\sf U}$  an irreducible {\sf AUD}\xspace transition matrix of a finite or infinite tree $T$.  For any node $u$ of $T$, consider the weighted graph $({}^uG,{}^{u}{{\sf U}})$ with set of nodes ${}^uV:=\cro{\varnothing,u}$, and in which the weight of
\[{}^{u}{{\sf U}}_{a,b} = ({\sf U}_{a,T_b}-{\sf U}_{a,T_{{\sf s}(b,u)}})\1_{b\in \cro{p(a),p(u)}} +\1_{b=u}{\sf U}_{a,T_u}\]
is defined for all $a\in\cro{\varnothing,p(u)}$ and $b\in\cro{\varnothing, u}$. Set
\begin{eqnarray}
\pi(u) = \pi(\varnothing)\frac{{\sf Weight}( \cro{\varnothing,u}, {}^{u}{\sf U}, u)}{{\displaystyle\prod_{v \in \cror{  \varnothing,  u}} {\sf U}_{v,p(v)} }}=\pi(\varnothing)\frac{\det (({\sf Id}-{}^{u}{{\sf U}})^{(u)})}{\displaystyle\prod_{v \in \cror{  \varnothing,  u}} {\sf U}_{v,p(v)} }.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{itemize} \topsep0pt   \itemsep=0pt   \partopsep=0pt   \parsep=0pt \setcounter{c}{0}
\addtocounter{c}{1}\item[($\roman{c}$)] The measure $\pi$ is an invariant measure of ${\sf U}$ (with positive coordinates).
\addtocounter{c}{1}\item[($\roman{c}$)] An {\sf AUD}\xspace transition matrix is positive recurrent if and only if $\sum \pi(u)<+\infty$.\end{itemize}\vspace{-2em}~ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.974149+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8660bf0d897f2b880cd0ae2e520c48d66f09667051551383b7a348c21a2e0fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop remove plaquette from loop of arXiv:2508.13827, 'Surface sums in two-dimensional large-$N$ lattice Yang--Mills: Cancellations and explicit '] Suppose that $(\ell, K)$ is a balanced loop plaquette assignment, and let $e$ be an edge contained in $\ell$. Assume that $e$ is connected to infinity via a path of plaquettes $\mathfrak{p} = \{p_i\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$, where the first plaquette is given by $p_1 = e\, e_1\, e_2 \, e_3$. Then, each string plaquette assignment $(s,\mathcal{K})= \{(\ell_1, K_1), \dots, (\ell_m , K_m)\}$ labeling a leaf of $\mathcal{T}_{e,\mathfrak{p}}(\ell,K)$  satisfies the following properties:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item $\supp(s)\cap\mathfrak{p} = \emptyset$;
            \item $\mathcal{K}(q) = K(q)$ for all $q\in \mathcal{P}\setminus \mathfrak{p}$;
            \item $h_{s}(q)= h_{\ell}(q)$ for all $q\in \mathcal{P}\setminus \{p_{1}^{\pm 1}\}$;
            \item $n_{\Tilde{e}}(s) \leq n_{\Tilde{e}}(\ell)$ for all $\Tilde{e}\in E\setminus \{e_1^{\pm 1}, e_2^{\pm 1}, e_3^{\pm 1}\}$; 
            \item $n_{\Tilde{e}}(s) + n_{\Tilde{e}^{-1}}(s)\leq \Big(n_{\Tilde{e}}(\ell) + n_{\Tilde{e}^{-1}}(\ell)\Big) + \Big(n_e(\ell) + n_{e^{-1}}(\ell)\Big)$ for all $\Tilde{e}\in \{e_1, e_2, e_3\}$.
        \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.662330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8664a34a65bd380c2198fbeeadcc293a5fa38c05adb1d86f2451220a0af1c92","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm5 of arXiv:2412.05521, 'Random attractors for the stochastic Nernst-Planck-Navier-Stokes system with multiplicativ'] Let $v^\varepsilon(0)$ $\in$  $H^1$ and $c_i(0) \in H^1$ be nonnegative with $c_i(0) \geq 0 $. Let $T > 0$. Then, there exists a unique solution $(v^\varepsilon,c_1,c_2)$ satisfying $v^\varepsilon \in 
		L^{\infty}(0,T;H^1) \cap L^2(0,T;H^2)$ and $c_i \in L^{\infty}(0,T;H^1) \cap L^2(0,T;H^2) $. Moreover $c_i(t) \geq 0$ holds on $[0,T]$. 
		\begin{proof}
			 By the local existence theorem (Theorem (thm2 of the paper)), there exists $T_0 \geq 0$ depending only on the norms of initial data in $H^1$ such that the solution exists and belongs to $H^1$. By Remark (Remark 1 of the paper), we find $c_i(t) \geq 0$. Moreover, the inequality (equation b33 of the paper) holds on $[0,T_0]$. It follows from Theorem (thm4 of the paper) that the solution is bounded in $H^1$. Applying the local existence theorem again and starting from $T_0$, we deduce that the solution can be extended for $T_1$ $\geq T_0$. The inequality (equation b33 of the paper) holds on $[0,T_1]$. Since the inequality holds as long as $c_i$ $\geq 0$, reasoning by contradiction we conclude that the solution can extend to the whole interval $[0,T]$.
		\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.739716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f86942a2c59c0a606883139ae4499f90a8b556dcf19959879c9efff0d63ccfe6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 28 of arXiv:2609.02197, '2609.02197'] Let $\theta \in \left]-\infty,2-\frac{d}{2}\right[.$
