Then $X_\infty=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \widetilde{X}_n$ and $X_\infty^+=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\widetilde{X}_n^+$ almost surely and in $L^p$ for every $p<3$.\footnote{It should be possible to prove $L^p$ convergence for all $p>0$, but we do not need it} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.330205+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8aa9fdcb989dc9def209610db1d6149adc3716bca95d3335820f3efa8a50235","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem ref:MainThm of arXiv:2601.19630, 'Mass generation for the two dimensional O(N) Linear Sigma Model in the large N limit'] For all $\lambda>0$ and $\beta \geq 0$, there exists a mass $m_{*}=m_{*}(\lambda,\beta)$ and a constant $C=C(\lambda,\beta)$ independent of $N$ with the following property.  Any infinite volume limit $\nu^{N}=\nu^{N,\lambda,\beta}$ of the Linear Sigma Model on $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ obtained from periodic b.c. satisfies 
