\bigg | \int_{\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^{2})}F(\Psi)d( P_{i} \nu^{N}-\mu_{m_{*}} )(\Psi)  \bigg | \lesssim_{F}\frac{C}{N^{\frac{1}{2}}}   
\end{equation}
for all $i \in [N]$ and suitable cylindrical functionals $F: \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^{2}) \mapsto \mathbb{R}$, where the limiting mass $m_{*}$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\ln m_{*}=-2\pi \beta+O\big (\lambda^{-1} \big). 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.643993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8ab3ccf772fb8fa97d779058606f5971d90b000669945415b1e20cb1b9d5b0f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.19179, 'A random walk on $p$-groups with a symmetric perfect pairing'] Suppose $1 \leq k \leq n$. If $M_{k,k}$ is non-singular, then there is a canonical isomorphism of groups equipped with a symmetric pairing:
\begin{equation}

V_k^{\perp} \Big/ V_k \cong \Ker(M_{k,k}) \times \Q_p^{n-k} 
\end{equation}
where the pairing on the right hand side is the sum of the pairing on $\Ker(M_{k,k})$, and some symmetric pairing on $\Q_p^{n-k}$. The pairing on the left hand side is the pairing induced by $M_{n,n}$.","status":"unverified"}

