{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.674475+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8d2962747d597cf784a747cda4153bbf3cf7c7ba1e0315ea8bf1e63aae78f0e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2608.04937, '2608.04937'] We say that a stochastic process $X$ is a solution to the mean-field stochastic differential equation on $[0,T]$ with initial condition $\xi$, generator $B$, diffusion coefficient $\sigma$, and given processes $Y,Z$ (\textnormal{MF-SDE}$^T(\xi,B,\sigma,Y,Z)$ for short) if
