{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:44.008346+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f98742d8c418d0f5bf7cfe420e36c9b44b8f510ff384f7ed80a239cc3592d849","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:DE of arXiv:2412.04579, 'Solvable Families of Random Block Tridiagonal Matrices'] Fix $\beta>0$ and an integer $n\ge 1$. Let $a_1,\dots, a_n$, $b_1, \dots, b_{n-1}$ be independent random variables with $a_j\sim N\bigl(0,\frac{2}{\beta}\bigr)$, \smash{$b_j\sim \frac{1}{\sqrt{\beta}}\chi_{\beta (n-j)}$}. Then the symmetric tridiagonal matrix $\mathbf{T}$ with diagonal $a_1,a_2,\dots$ and off-diagonal $b_1,b_2, \dots$ has a joint symmetrized eigenvalue density on ${\mathbb R}^n$ given by
\begin{align*}
 \frac{1}{Z_{n,\beta}} \left|\Delta(\lambda)\right|^\beta {\rm e}^{-\frac{\beta}{4} \sum_{j=1}^n \lambda_j^2},
\end{align*}
with
\begin{align}
 Z_{n,\beta}={n!} (2\pi)^{n/2} (\beta/2)^{-\frac{\beta}{4}n(n-1)-\frac{n}{2}} \Gamma(\beta/2)^{-n} \prod_{j=1}^n \Gamma(\beta j/2).
\end{align}
Moreover, the spectral weights of $\mathbf{T}$ corresponding to the first coordinate vector have joint distribution given by $\mathrm{Dirichlet}(\beta/2,\dots, \beta/2)$, and this weight vector is independent of the eigenvalues.","status":"unverified"}

