is nondecreasing wherever its denominator is positive.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.424517+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9e5598a68c37784f67ddf38b31141645b3689d731c13fdb8b2ba18d669148c1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:counter-match of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[theorem]{thm:counterexample}
Fix \(p\ge3\) and \(\sigma>1\).  There exists a symmetric probability law \(\mu=\mu_{p,\sigma}\) on \(\mathbb R\) with the following properties.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=2em]
\item \(\mu\) has a strictly positive \(C^\infty\) density and is sub-Gaussian.
\item If \(\xi\sim\mu\) and \(G\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\), then
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E\xi^r=\mathbb E G^r,
 \qquad r=1,\ldots,2p.
\end{equation}
\item There is a compact interval \(B\subset(0,\infty)\) such that
\begin{equation}
 \liminf_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log\mathbb E_\mu\operatorname{Crt}_N^J(B)
 >\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log\mathbb E\operatorname{Crt}_N^G(B).
\end{equation}
Moreover, the same interval satisfies
\begin{align}
 \liminf_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log\mathbb E_\mu\operatorname{Crt}_{N,\max}^J(B)
 &>\limsup_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log\mathbb E\operatorname{Crt}_{N,\max}^G(B),
 \\
 \liminf_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log\mathbb E_\mu\operatorname{Crt}_{N,\min}^J(-B)
 &>\limsup_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log\mathbb E\operatorname{Crt}_{N,\min}^G(-B).

\end{align}
\end{enumerate}
In particular, sub-Gaussianity together with Gaussian moment matching
through order \(2p\) does not force the Gaussian rate of unregularized
critical-point counts in a compact energy window when no spatial
localization restriction is imposed. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.428875+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9e99b0548f84b5e1c3787ed1246be8fad4239df20a6036c097f2525f7c75858","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:limit-u' of arXiv:2604.20094, 'Persistence and local extinction for superprocesses in random environments'] Let $f\in C_c^+({\mathbb R}^d)$, and $\nu\in{\mathcal M}_F({\mathbb R}^d)$. Then for each $t\geqslant 0$ and $\lambda\geqslant 0$,
