{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.906531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_faf9ecde44d3b09fbfb5f192a86eb8e66bdfe12ed0a9cd137372b67c8e075125","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:large-near-top-minor of arXiv:2608.26707, 'An Exponential Lower Bound for the Permanent\\of Random Bernoulli Matrix'] There are absolute constants $C,c>0$ such that, with probability at least $1-\exp\!\left(-\frac{cn}{(\log n)^2}\right),$ there exists $A_*\in\binom{[n]}{k_*}$ satisfying
