{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.593976+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb1921e0cf99fef4f70e4bea7e901175d13b44fa4a0e7863972fc655aa296678","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro3.1 of arXiv:2511.07173, '\bf Mean-field backward stochastic Volterra integral equations: well-posedness and related'] \rm
For any given $(y(\cdot), z(\cdot,\cdot))$ and for almost all $t\in[0,T]$,
if the following mean-field BSDE
\begin{equation}
{\cal Y}(t,s)=\psi(t)+\int_s^Tg(t,r,y(r),{\cal Z}(t,r),\mathbb{P}_{y(r)}, \mathbb{P}_{{\cal Z}(t,r)})dr-\int_s^T{\cal Z}(t,r)dW(r),\quad s\in[t,T]
\end{equation}
admits a unique solution, denoted by $({\cal Y}(t,\cdot),{\cal Z}(t,\cdot))$,
then the following mean-field BSVIE
\begin{equation}
Y(t)=\psi(t)+\int_t^Tg(t,s,y(s),Z(t,s),\mathbb{P}_{y(s)}, \mathbb{P}_{Z(t,s)})ds-\int_t^TZ(t,s)dW(s), \quad  t\in[0,T]
\end{equation}
also admits a unique solution $(Y(\cdot),Z(\cdot,\cdot))$.
Moreover,
\begin{align*}
  Y(t) = \mathcal{Y}(t,t)\quad  \text{and}\quad Z(t,s) = \mathcal{Z}(t,s)\quad  \text{for}\ (t,s) \in \Delta[0,T].
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.321588+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb1de386942aa9474cacf59b096e1c2797dc10b40d9d30ad9fc8f38a642ccf95","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 19 of arXiv:2506.21087, 'Quasi-Stationary Distributions of Interacting Dynamical Systems and their approximation'] Assume that $\MM$ is a closed subset of $\ER^d$. Let $(\zeta_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a centered L\'evy process on $\ER^d$ and $h$ denote the step of the Euler scheme. Assume that $(x,\mu)\mapsto \sigma(x,\mu)$ is bounded (on $\MM\times{\cal P}(\MM)$). Then, \ref{condhvdeux} holds in the two following situations.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)](Standard Euler scheme)  The dynamics is given by \eqref{eq:formegeneralenoyau}, the step $h$ is small enough, $\zeta_1$ has moments of any order and some positive $\alpha$ and $C$ exist such that
$$ \langle x, b(x,\mu)\rangle \le C-\alpha |x|^2\quad\textnormal{and}\quad |b(x,\mu)|^2\le C(1+|x|^2) $$
\item[(ii)](Modified Euler scheme) The dynamics is given by \eqref{eq:formegeneralenoyautronc}, there exists $\alpha>0$ such that $\ES[|\zeta_h|^\alpha]<+\infty$ and Condition \ref{condhtronc} holds.

 \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.986582+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb1f64ea266fd8177acb2bcc0284b7128e2cd65d45171807b59104a458ba6b78","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma estimations complementaires of arXiv:2412.00865, 'On the spectral problem and fractional diffusion limit for Fokker-Planck with(-out) drift '] There exists a constant $C > 0$ such that, for all $\eta >0$ small enough,  and for all $\gamma \in \left(\frac{d}{2}, \frac{d+4}{2}\right)$, the following uniform estimates hold:
