{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.640663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb46344f9f68df73a6c32114b2f2a0f9e913300e59296445fce893b0c3b50061","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 33 of arXiv:2506.17979, '\large\bf A remark on subharmonicity for symmetric Dirichlet forms'] Take $u\in L^1_{\loc}(\R^d)$. Then 
the following are equivalent to each other. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(1)] $\Psi(m^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}|\cdot|)\left(1\land\frac{1}{|\,\cdot\,|} \right)|u|\in L^1(\R^d)$. 
\item[\rm(2)] For any $U\Subset V\Subset \R^d$, 
with $0\in V$, 
\begin{align*}
\int_{U\times V^c}|u(x)-u(y)|\frac{\Psi(m^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}|x-y|)}{|x-y|^{d+\alpha}}{\d} x{\d} y<\infty.
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, take $u\in L^2_{\loc}(\R^d)$. Then the following are equivalent to each other.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(3)] $\Psi(m^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}|\cdot|)\left(1\land\frac{1}{|\,\cdot\,|} \right)|u|^2\in L^1(\R^d)$. 
\item[\rm(4)] For any $U\Subset V\Subset \R^d$, 
\begin{align*}
\int_{U\times V^c}|u(x)-u(y)|^2\frac{\Psi(m^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}|x-y|)}{|x-y|^{d+\alpha}}{\d} x{\d} y<\infty.
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:41.040427+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb4766ad6af6a88d536f448571bb307255aacfec7ab2a7688ecc32aaa9171115","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Cutoff and pre-cutoff of arXiv:2412.04032, 'Cutoff for the mixing time of the Facilitated Exclusion Process'] A sequence of mixing times $(\tau_N)_{N\ge 0}$ exhibits \emph{cutoff} if
\eqs{\forall \varepsilon \in (0,1), \, \lim\limits_{N\to \infty} \frac{\tau_N(\varepsilon)}{\tau_N(\frac{1}{4})} = 1.}
A sequence of mixing times $(\tau_N)_{N\ge 0}$ exhibits \emph{pre-cutoff} if there exist $C, C' >0$ such that
\eqs{\forall \varepsilon \in (0,1), \, C \le \varliminf\limits_{N\to \infty} \frac{\tau_N(\varepsilon)}{\tau_N(\frac{1}{4})}\le \varlimsup\limits_{N\to \infty} \frac{\tau_N(\varepsilon)}{\tau_N(\frac{1}{4})} \le C'.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.191618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb4f0493f707709631eb9079be91bb72e1cc00ab29c535c4d83d65d69459c9c2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 35 of arXiv:2604.19200, 'Stochastic $\Sigma$-convergence in Orlicz setting and Applications'] Let $(u_{\varepsilon})_{\varepsilon\in E}$ be a sequence in $W^{1}L^{\Phi}_{D_{x}}(Q; L^{\Phi}(\Omega))$. Assume that $(Du_{\varepsilon})_{\varepsilon\in E}$ stochastically weakly $\Sigma$-converges componentwise to $\mathbb{D}\mathbf{u} = Du_{0} + \overline{D}_{\omega}u_{1} + \overline{D}_{y}u_{2} \in L^{\Phi}(Q\times\Omega;\mathcal{X}_{A}^{\Phi})^{d}$, where $\mathbf{u} = (u_{0}, u_{1}, u_{2}) \in \mathbb{F}^{1}_{0}L^{\Phi}$. Then 
		\begin{equation*}
			\iint_{Q\times\Omega} \pounds_{A}\int \varrho \circ f(\cdot,\cdot, \mathbb{D}\mathbf{u}(x,\omega,y))  dydxd\mu  \leq \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \iint_{Q\times\Omega}  f^{\varepsilon}\left(\cdot,\cdot, Du_{\varepsilon}\right)  dxd\mu.
		\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.262347+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb5152cccb27b49f68bf5508d2ae71af1a984a9ea9aed3d1336e2609acd185de","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lkzeta of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp For any {\small$(\s,\t,\x,\y)\in[0,\infty)^{2}\times\Z^{2}$} with {\small$\s\leq\t$}, we have

\begin{align}
\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\t,\x,\y}&=\mathbf{L}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\t,\x,\y}+\int_{\s}^{\t}\sum_{\w\in\Z}\mathbf{L}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\r,\t,\x,\w}\cdot\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\r}[\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\r,\cdot,\y}]_{\w}\d\r,
\end{align}

where, for convenience, {\small$\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\r}$} is the operator acting on {\small$\w\mapsto\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta}_{\s,\r,\w,\y}$} arising from the second line in (equation eq:kzetasim-sde of the paper):

\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\r}[\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\r,\cdot,\y}]_{\w}&:=N\boldsymbol{\chi}^{(\zeta)}_{\x}{\ocolor{red}\mathbf{1}_{\mathfrak{t}_{\mathrm{ap}}\geq\r}}\sum_{\substack{\n=1,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\\{m}=0,\ldots,\mathrm{M}\\0<|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\lesssim \mathfrak{n}_{\mathbf{Av}}}}\tfrac{1}{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|\ldots|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|}\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\r}^{\n,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\r,\cdot,\y}]_{\w},\\
\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\r}^{\n,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\r,\cdot,\y}]_{\w}&:=\mathrm{c}_{N,\n,\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{1}}\ldots\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star(\mathbf{Av}^{\mathbf{T},\mathbf{X},\mathfrak{q}_{\n}}_{\r,\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{K}^{N,\zeta,\sim}_{\s,\r,\cdot,\y})]_{\w}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.765544+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb54857d74148d8bee0ffa68ff26486dd7c3d166292992862f65471f7ca94a13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 (\cites{Rei77, ESI92}) of arXiv:2608.26723, 'Extrinsic upper bounds for the harmonic mean of eigenvalues in curved manifolds'] Let $M$ be an $m(\ge 2)$-dimensional closed orientable submanifolds in a space form $\mathbb{R}^{n}(c)$. Then the first nonzero eigenvalue $\lambda_1$  of the Laplacian on $M$ satisfies
	\begin{equation}
		\lambda_1\leq\frac{m}{\vol(M)}\int_M(c+|\mathbf{H}|^2),
	\end{equation}
	where $\mathbf{H}$ is the mean curvature vector of $M$ in $\mathbb{R}^{n}(c)$.

	Equality holds if and only if $M$ is either minimally immersed in $\mathbb{S}^n$ by a subspace of the first eigenspace, or minimally immersed in a geodesic sphere of $\mathbb{R}^{n}(c)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.797301+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb5525a860d0d5d68584219b955fb9e80493afd24187a8b5de27965b62e7a57f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:two_edge_1_gamma_minus_1_le_delta_le_1_gamma of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] Suppose $\frac 1 \gamma-1<\delta<\frac 1 \gamma$. The two-edge crossing probabilities are given~by 
    \begin{align*}
        \pi_{(u),R}^{(2)} 
        &\asymp \pi_{(u),R}+ u^{-\gamma} R^{d(1+\frac 1{\gamma \delta}-\frac 1 \gamma-\frac 1 {\delta})}\wedge 1\\
        &\asymp \begin{cases}
                    u^{-\gamma} R^{d(1+\frac 1{\gamma \delta}-\frac 1 \gamma-\frac 1 {\delta})} & \text{ on } \{u  \geq R^{-d\frac {\delta+1-1/\gamma}{\gamma \delta}} \},\\
                    u^{-\gamma \delta} R^{d(1-\delta)} & \text{ on } \{R^{d(\frac 1 {\gamma \delta}-\frac 1 \gamma)}  \leq u  \leq R^{-d(\frac {\delta+1-1/\gamma}{\gamma \delta})} \},\\
                    1 & \text{ on } \{u \leq R^{d(\frac 1 {\gamma \delta}-\frac 1 \gamma)} \}.
