{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.341893+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fba95c7494c670d7bd92dd4a0a7d2972c7092569522b42ddb663970cf96225d4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Them Markov_stationnaire_bis of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Consider an interval $T$ and a stationary  positive semi-definite kernel $K :(s,t) \in T^2 \mapsto \tilde{K}(t-s) \in \mathbb{R}$ that is continuous and satisfies $\tilde{K}(0) = 1.$ We denote by $P$ a centered Gaussian measure with covariance function $K$ and set $L^K : h \in \mathbb{R}_+^* \mapsto h^{-1}(1-\tilde{K}(h)).$
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item If $\lim_{h \to 0^+} L^K(h) = \alpha \in [0, +\infty],$ then $P_\alpha$ is the strong local Markov transform of $P$.
			\item Assume $\alpha \in [0,+\infty]$ is a cluster point of $L^K$ at $0^+$ and consider a sequence of positive numbers $(s_n)_{n \geq 1}$ converging to zero that satisfies $\lim_{n \to + \infty} L^K(s_n) = \alpha$. For every $s<t \in T^2$, writing $R_n^{s,t} := \left((s_n \mathbb{Z}) \cap ]s,t[\right) \cup \{s,t\}$, we obtain $\lim_{n \to + \infty} P_{\{R_n^{s,t}\}}^{s,t} = P_{\alpha}^{s,t}$. In particular, $P_\alpha$ is a  weak local Markov transform of $P.$
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:43:00.051102+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fbad5a4907c9d67b87f2283107b8bc921b9cfe1c054d789958df5f4f23dd2996","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2 (Information equality for the ignoring estimator) of arXiv:2609.00774, 'Semi-Supervised Classification with Informative Missing Labels in Weibull Mixture Models'] Let
\[
 S_Y(\theta)=\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\log p_Y(Y;\theta),
 \qquad
 S_{Z\mid Y}(\theta)=\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\log p(Z\mid Y;\theta),
\]
and let
\[
 \psi_{\mathrm{ig}}(\theta)
 =S_Y(\theta)+(1-M)S_{Z\mid Y}(\theta)
\]
be the score of $\ell_{\mathrm{ig},1}$. Under the conditional-independence assumption $M\perp Z\mid Y$ and the usual differentiability and moment conditions,
\begin{equation}
 -\E\left\{\frac{\partial\psi_{\mathrm{ig}}(\theta)}
 {\partial\theta^\top}\right\}
 =
 \E\{\psi_{\mathrm{ig}}(\theta)\psi_{\mathrm{ig}}(\theta)^\top\}
 =
 \J_{\mathrm{ig}}(\theta,\xi).

\end{equation}
If, in addition, $\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{ig}}\xrightarrow{p}\theta$, $\J_{\mathrm{ig}}$ is nonsingular, and the standard local regularity conditions for M-estimation hold, then
\begin{equation}
 \sqrt n(\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{ig}}-\theta)
 \xrightarrow{d}
 N\!\left(0,\J_{\mathrm{ig}}^{-1}\right).

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.613296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fbaf14b85b98ace4f8f7ed8aba8eaaea6cb38dc9f4346dcd8a756b6138f8410f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 ({\cite{LR10},\cite[Theorem 3.1) of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] {DV21+}}]

