{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.409175+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc14daa6219f429cbedb09483eb7691464567b53b4ab08a786c4f5c8322365b5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 22 (\cite{LLZ}) of arXiv:2605.15781, 'Mean-Field Backward Stochastic Differential Equations with Nonlinear Resistance and Double'] Let $f_i=f_i(t,\omega,\mu,y,z)$, $i=1,2$ be two drivers satisfying (H1). Moreover, we suppose that one of the drivers satisfies (H2) and for any $\theta_1,\theta_2\in L^2(\mathcal{F}_T)$ and any $(t,y,z)\in[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}$
\begin{align*}
f_i(s,\P_{\theta_1},y,z)-f_i(s,\P_{\theta_2},y,z)\leq L(\E[|(\theta_1-\theta_2)^+|^2])^{1/2}.
\end{align*}
Let $\xi_i\in L^2(\mathcal{F}_T)$, $i=1,2$ and denote by $(Y^i,Z^i)$ the solution of the mean field BSDE \eqref{MFBSDE'} with parameters $(\xi_i,f_i)$, $i=1,2$. Then, if $\xi_1\leq \xi_2$ and $f_1(t,\mu,y,z)\leq f_2(t,\mu,y,z)$ for all $(\mu,y,z)$, we have $Y^1_t\leq Y^2_t$, $t\in[0,T]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

