{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.158814+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc322e151f0a9232cc9a6fa5eb46cbf4320aedb1f1ce199b003efd74d3ef0c19","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] We have the following identity 
    \begin{equation}

        \mathcal R_n^{j\rightarrow k}=\sum_{\ell=1}^{2^p}\mathcal R_{n,p,\ell}^{j\rightarrow k}+\sum_{q=1}^{p}\sum_{m=1}^{2^{q-1}}\mathcal I_{n,q,m}^{j\rightarrow k}+\varepsilon_{n,p}^{j\rightarrow k},
    \end{equation}
    where for each $p\ge1$, $\ell\in[\![2^p]\!]$, $q\in[\![p]\!]$, $m\in[\![2^{q-1}]\!]$, we define 
    \begin{equation}

        \mathcal R_{n,p,\ell}^{j\rightarrow k}\eqdef\sum_{x\in\ZZ^d}\1\left(T_x^j\circ\theta_{\frac{\ell-1}{2^p}n}\le T_x^k\circ\theta_{\frac{\ell-1}{2^p}n}\le n/2^p\right),
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}

        \mathcal I_{n,q,m}^{j\rightarrow k}\eqdef\sum_{x\in\ZZ^d}\1\left(T_x^j\circ\theta_{\frac{m-1}{2^{q-1}}n}<n/2^q<T_x^k\circ\theta_{\frac{m-1}{2^{q-1}}n}\le n/2^{q-1}\right),
    \end{equation}
    and where $\varepsilon_{n,p}^{j\rightarrow k}$ is an error term such that 
    \begin{equation*}
        \underset{n\rightarrow\infty}\lim\frac{h(n)^4b(n)^{2d}}{n^4}\EE\left[(\varepsilon_{n,p}^{j\rightarrow k})^2\right]=0.
    \end{equation*}
    The variables $(\mathcal R_{n,p,\ell}^{j\rightarrow k})_{1\le \ell\le 2^p}$ are mutually independent and equal in distribution to $\mathcal R_{n/2^p}^{j\rightarrow k}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:20:55.704890+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc38fe151620594ca550cbd8dd76b301fd741b5a2f799af77f625ccb397c0849","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LEM:Main0 of arXiv:2411.07840, 'Central limit theorem for the focusing $Φ^4$-measure in the infinite volume limit'] For $F=F(h^\perp,t)$ which is bounded, Lipschitz in the sense of (equation eqn: F-lip of the paper), we have 
