as $L\to \infty$, where $M$ is a large constant from Lemma (lem: mu2-ests of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.334097+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc3c3b1e224f627e0447ef965269ccdea9fbea584672964374172384399d64e2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2512.05466, 'Stochastic Flows and Marked Stable Processes'] Let  $\delta_1\ge 0$, $\delta_2,\delta_3\in \bR$, $b\ge 0$ and $r_0,z\in \bR$. Let $Y$ be the ${\rm BESQ}(\delta_1\,|_z\, \delta_2)$ flow line starting from $(b,r_0)$ driven by $\cW$ and $\cS$ be the ${\rm BESQ}(\delta_1+\delta_3 \,|_z\, \delta_2+\delta_3)$ flow driven by $\cW$. Write $\zeta$ for the absorption time of $Y$, i.e. $\zeta:=\inf\{r\ge \max(r_0,z)\,:\, Y_r =0\}$ if $\delta_2<0$ and $\zeta=\infty$ otherwise.

    (i) The flow $\cS^+$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\cS^+_{r,x}(a):= \max(\cS_{r,x}(a+Y_r)-Y_x,0),\qquad r_0\le r\le x, a\ge 0
\end{equation}

\noindent is on $\bR_+\times [r_0,+\infty)$ a ${\rm BESQ}(\delta_3\,|_{\zeta} \,\delta_2+\delta_3 )$ flow driven by $\cW_Y^+$. 

(ii) Both $Y$ and the collection of processes $\cS^-$ defined by  
\begin{equation}
\cS^-_{r,x}(a):= 
\min(\cS_{r,x}(a),Y_x), \quad  r_0\le r\le x , 0\le a< Y_r 
\end{equation}

\noindent are driven by the martingale measure $\cW_Y^-$. In particular, they are measurable w.r.t. to $\cW_Y^-$. 

(iii)  $\cW_Y^+$ is a white noise independent of $\cW_Y^-$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.116495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc3d5f8e2545ddb3f64115ad067cdf55b46122e4bd404d7a3111c0fb616af4f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:existenceweaksolutions of arXiv:2608.16946, 'A diffusion model for time-dependent compositional data'] The SDEs (equation canonicalSDE of the paper), (equation adjustedSDE of the paper), and (equation truncatedSDE of the paper) have weak
solutions. Specifically:
\begin{enumerate}
\renewcommand{\alph{enumi}}{\alph{enumi}}

\item Given a probability distribution on $\mathbb{R}^d$ with support contained in $\mathcal{S}$, there exist weak
solutions for SDEs  (equation canonicalSDE of the paper) and (equation adjustedSDE of the paper) such that $X(0)$ has the 
prescribed distribution, and $X(t)\in\bar\mathcal{S}$ for all $t\geq0$, $P$-a.s.
\item Given a probability distribution on $\mathbb{R}^{d-1}$ with support contained in $\mathcal{S}^-$, there exists a weak
solution for SDE (equation truncatedSDE of the paper) such that $X^-(0)$ has the 
prescribed distribution, and $X^-(t)\in\bar\mathcal{S}^-$ for all $t\geq0$, $P$-a.s.
\end{enumerate}
Furthermore, these solutions are unique in distribution, and they are equivalent, in the sense that:
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\renewcommand{\alph{enumi}}{\alph{enumi}}

\item Any weak solution for (equation canonicalSDE of the paper) is a solution for (equation adjustedSDE of the paper) and viceversa.
\item If $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft((\Omega,\mathcal{F},P),\{\mathcal{F}_t\},W(t),X(t)\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)$ is a weak solution for (equation canonicalSDE of the paper) and (equation adjustedSDE of the paper),
then $((\Omega,\mathcal{F},P),\allowbreak\{\mathcal{F}_t\},W(t),X^-(t))$ is a weak solution for (equation truncatedSDE of the paper).
\item If $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft((\Omega,\mathcal{F},P),\{\mathcal{F}_t\},W(t),X^-(t)\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)$ is a weak solution for (equation truncatedSDE of the paper),
then $((\Omega,\mathcal{F},P),\{\mathcal{F}_t\},\allowbreak W(t),X^+(t))$ is a weak solution for (equation canonicalSDE of the paper) and (equation adjustedSDE of the paper).
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.655510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc3dbf1ff988d19c8c30654a17b6ae80eaa0d33a072e6fea423ec142d2783022","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2nd lemma for passing limit of arXiv:2411.04631, 'Existence of Martingale Solutions to Stochastic Constrained Heat Equation'] For all $t_1,t_2 \in [0,T]$ with $t_2 \leq t_1$ and $\forall \phi_1, \phi_2 \in \mathcal{V}$ we have 
    \begin{align}
        &\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty } \hat{\mathbb{E}} \left[  \left(\left \langle \hat{M}_{n}(t_1),\phi_1 \right \rangle \left \langle \hat{M}_{n}(t_1),\phi_2 \right \rangle-\left \langle \hat{M}_{n}(t_2),\phi_1 \right \rangle \left \langle \hat{M}_{n}(t_2),\phi_2 \right \rangle \right) h(v_{n|[0,t_2]})\right] \nonumber \\&=\hat{\mathbb{E}} \left[ \left(\left \langle \hat{M}(t_1),\phi_1 \right \rangle \left \langle \hat{M}(t_1),\phi_2 \right \rangle-\left \langle \hat{M}(t_2),\phi_1 \right \rangle \left \langle \hat{M}(t_2),\phi_2 \right \rangle \right) h(v_{n|[0,t_2]}) \right], \; \; h \in  \mathcal{C}([0,T],\mathcal{H}), \nonumber
    \end{align}
    where $\left \langle \cdot , \cdot \right \rangle$ represents duality product in the spaces $\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{V}'$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.541469+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc4059c95e3607298ffd18c773874c244b84cf5525e0a38b405f41160034f8b8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:gradient_L_infinity of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m$ a very-well-connected cube such that $\mathscr{C}_*({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \subseteq \mathscr{C}_{\infty}$, then the following estimates hold for $\nabla v_{m, q}$.
		\begin{itemize}
			\item For any $e \in E_d(\mathscr{C}_*({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m))$, we have 
			\begin{equation}
				|\nabla v_{m,q}(e)| \leqslant \vert q\vert + 4 \osc_{\mathscr{C}_{\infty} \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m} \chi_q.
			\end{equation}
			\item For any $e \in E_d(\mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \setminus \mathscr{C}_*({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m))$, 
			\begin{align}
				|\nabla v_{m,q}(e)| \leqslant \sqrt{d} \vert q\vert \size({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_\mathcal{P}(e)). 
			\end{align}
		\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.906046+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc41c39c027d30f43fb83a99ac1599bbc24ab3935b2ba2812a3eaa28034e4130","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:sign-conjugacy of arXiv:2608.26707, 'An Exponential Lower Bound for the Permanent\\of Random Bernoulli Matrix'] For every $k$ and every sign vector $\varepsilon$,
