{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:26:47.153225+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc803ad0773fd75f4d9ce3f3ec44dbee61198c6dbbdeb8f8ed64d67228830c3e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 34 of arXiv:2608.29361, 'Convergence of the subcritical $\gamma$-LQG metric to the critical $2$-LQG metric as $\gam'] Suppose that the functions $b,M$ are chosen as in Proposition~\ref{prop:tightness}. Let $(\xi_n)_{n \in \NN}$ be a sequence in $(0,\xi_c)$ such that $\xi_n \to \xi_c$ as $n \to \infty$. Then, the sequence of metrics $(\widetilde{D}_h^{\xi_n})_{n \in \NN}$ is tight with respect to the local uniform topology on $\CC \times \CC$. Moreover, if $(\xi_{k_n})_{n \in \NN}$ is a subsequence of $(\xi_n)_{n \in \NN}$ such that $(\widetilde{D}_h^{\xi_{k_n}})_{n \in \NN}$ converges in law (with respect to the local uniform topology on $\CC \times \CC$) to some random metric on $\CC \times \CC$, then we have that the following is true. There exists a $\xi_c$-LQG metric $h \mapsto \widetilde{D}_h$ and a subsequence $(\xi_{k'_n})_{n \in \NN}$ of $(\xi_{k_n})_{n \in \NN}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    \widetilde{D}_h^{\xi_{k'_n}} \to \widetilde{D}_h \quad \text{in probability} \quad \text{as} \quad n \to \infty.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.476416+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc813486fb488a4e60411d2e708161de09e67936d3b1b6145057d87a0afbe7a6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem : Topology/GMT/AreaFormula of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] Let $\gamma : \intervalleff0T \rightarrow X$ be a Lipschitz, injective path. Then
	\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
		\item $\hd$-almost every point of $\gamma$ is regular.
		\item For all measurable function $\Phi : X \times \mathbb{R}^d  \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^+$ such that for all $z \in X$, $\Phi(z, \cdot)$ is absolutely homogeneous,
			\begin{equation}

				\int_0^T \Phi\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft( \gamma(t), \gamma'(t)  \aftergroup\egroup\originalright)\mathrm{d} t
			=\int_\gamma \Phi\p{ z , \frac{\gamma'\p{\gamma^{-1}(z) } }{ \norme[2]{\gamma'\p{\gamma^{-1}(z)} } } } \hd(\mathrm{d} z).
	\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.864206+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc815a6a75a9dcff5546bdf33fc52d17b50aef09818b0c3a49faae307de89a9f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2511.23453, 'Convergence rates of self-repellent random walks, their local time and Event Chain Monte C'] If $Q=\Pi$, then the transition semigroup $(\hat P_t^{(\gamma)})_{t\geq 0}$ satisfies
    \begin{equation}
        \norm{\hat P_t^{(\gamma)}f}_{L^2(\hat\mu)} \leq e^{-\nu (t-T)}\norm{f}_{L^2(\hat\mu)}\qquad\text{for all }f\in L_0^2(\hat\mu)\text{ and }t\geq 0
    \end{equation}
    with $T\in O(n)$ and inverse convergence rate
    $$\nu^{-1}\in O\left(\gamma n^3+\gamma^{-1}n\right).$$ 
    In particular,
    for $\gamma\propto n^{-1}$, the $L^2$-relaxation time is of order $O(n^{2})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.498218+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc84d4bdfed5206fff65d526aa5cfc2aadcaef468b1542db433077b4331250e6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2605.09706, 'A dyadic construction of a three-dimensional attractive point interaction Markov family'] The function $Q_T^\beta(x)$ satisfies the following bounds for $T,\beta>0$ and $x\in\R^3\setminus\{0\}$.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]

\item $\displaystyle 1 + Q_T^\beta(x) \ge  \frac{\sqrt{T}}{|x| \sqrt{\pi}} e^{-\frac{|x|^2}{4T}} $

\item Fix $L>0$. There exists a constant $C_L>0$ such that for all $T, \beta>0$ with $\beta\sqrt T\le L$, and all $x\in\R^3\setminus\{0\}$,
\begin{align}
Q_T^\beta(x)
\;\le\;
C_L\Big(1+\frac{\sqrt T}{|x|}\Big)\,e^{-4\pi\beta|x|}\,.
\end{align}

\item Fix $L>0$. There exists a constant $C_L>0$ such that for all $T, \beta>0$ with $\beta\sqrt T\le L$, and all $x\in\R^3\setminus\{0\}$,
\begin{align} \nonumber
\big|\nabla Q_T^\beta(x)\big|
\,\le\,&
C_L\,
\bigg[
\frac{1}{|x|}
+\frac{1+\sqrt{T}}{|x|^2}
+\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{T}}
\bigg]\,
e^{-4\pi\beta|x|} \,.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.618878+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc853cd4298091410769ff6abb3816cfe4777e0cfcd07fb400363f158275181e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 21 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] For any $B\in\widehat{\Bcal}(S)$ with $\pbb(\nu(B)=0)<1$, there exists a graphon $W_B : [0,1]^2 \to [0,1]$ such that every exchangeable matrix $A^B$ associated with $\xi$ on $B$ admits a representation
