{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.893793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fd0c07ce229bca16ecad8029c7e0eb18f26d519804273bf2ca5db2c785f97a30","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:RRW of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] Given a closed Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$ and a bounded interval $I\subset \mathbb{R}$, we call a \emph{Riemannian Random Wave} any Gaussian field on $M$ of the form
\begin{equation} 
\phi_I^M(x)=\sum_{\{i:\ \lambda_i\in I\}}\gamma_i \varphi_i(x),\qquad \Delta \varphi_i=-\lambda_i^2\varphi_i
\end{equation}
with $\gamma_i\sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$ \emph{i.i.d.}\xspace and where $(\varphi_i)_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ is an $L^2(M)$-orthonormal basis of eigenfunctions of $-\Delta$, ordered so that the eigenvalues are non-decreasing. We will denote
the covariance function of $\phi^M_{I}$, as:
\begin{equation}
E^M_{I}(x,y):=\mathbb{E}\left\{ \phi_{I}^M(x)\phi_{I}^M(y)\right\} =\sum_{\{i :\lambda_i \in I\} }\varphi_i(x)\varphi_i(y).
\end{equation}
In case $M$ has boundary, we will discuss the boundary conditions case-by-case. In particular, for $M\subset \mathbb{R}^n$, we define $\phi_I^{M}$ as the restriction of the stationary Gaussian field $\phi_I^{\mathbb{R}^n}$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ with covariance function
\begin{equation} 
E^{\mathbb{R}^n}_{I}(x,x+u):=E^n_{I}(|u|):=\left\{ \begin{aligned}
&\frac{1}{(2\pi)^n}\int_{I}\int_{\lambda\mathbbm{S}^{n-1}} e^{i\langle u, \xi \rangle} \dd \xi \dd \lambda,
\quad &\text{ if $I$ is an interval, }
\\
& \frac{1}{(2\pi)^n}\int_{\ell \mathbbm{S}^{n-1}} e^{i\langle u , \xi \rangle} \dd \xi, \quad &\text{ if $I=\ell$ is a real number.
}
\end{aligned}\right.\footnote{In fact, it is easy to see that the imaginary part of this integral vanishes, so that 
\begin{equation*} 
\mathbb{E}\left\{ e^{ig_x\langle u-v,\xi\rangle }\right\}=\mathbb{E}\left\{ \cos\left( g_x\langle u-v,\xi\rangle\right)\right\}.
\end{equation*}}
\end{equation}
In particular, $\phi_{1}^{\mathbb{R}^n}$ is known as Berry's random field on $\mathbb{R}^n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:43:00.051245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fd0c6b4bcac0a3e99c4d9055eda45044a71173c21f41d0d8cfade31d9e020d4a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Information under the full likelihood) of arXiv:2609.00774, 'Semi-Supervised Classification with Informative Missing Labels in Weibull Mixture Models'] Assume the model is identifiable and that the true parameter lies in an identifiable regular interior neighbourhood. Assume further that differentiation may be interchanged with integration, $B_{\xi\xi}$ is nonsingular, the relevant full-likelihood and completely classified likelihood maximisers are consistent for the true parameter, and the standard local regularity conditions for maximum-likelihood estimation hold. The per-observation Fisher information for $\theta$, after adjustment for the nuisance parameter $\xi$, is
\begin{equation}
 \J_{\mathrm{full}}(\theta,\xi)
 =\J_{\mathrm{CC}}(\theta)
 -\J_{\mathrm{lost}}(\theta,\xi)
 +\J_{\mathrm{miss}}^{\mathrm{adj}}(\theta,\xi).

\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation*}
 \J_{\mathrm{full}}(\theta,\xi)-\J_{\mathrm{CC}}(\theta)
 =\J_{\mathrm{miss}}^{\mathrm{adj}}(\theta,\xi)
 -\J_{\mathrm{lost}}(\theta,\xi).
\end{equation*}
If
\begin{equation}
 \J_{\mathrm{miss}}^{\mathrm{adj}}(\theta,\xi)
 -\J_{\mathrm{lost}}(\theta,\xi)>0,

\end{equation}
and both $\J_{\mathrm{full}}(\theta,\xi)$ and $\J_{\mathrm{CC}}(\theta)$ are positive definite, then the estimator based on the full likelihood has a smaller asymptotic covariance matrix than the maximum-likelihood estimator based on the completely classified sample. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.439623+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fd11bd80216a7cc0d394c37ab61f8e41d96fadd2a28c3d6bab41efedb205ff5e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 22 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] Let $0<\A{1},\A{2}\leq 1$ be such that $N^{-1}\ll  \pr{\A{1}\A{2}}^{1/2}$ and let $m\geq 1$ be an order function on $\mc{T}_{\A{1},\A{2}}$. Let $p\in S(m,\A{1},\A{2})$ such that $p\geq 0$, $p+i$ is elliptic and admitting an asymptotic expansion $p\sim p_0+\Tilde{h}p_1+\dots$ in $S(m,\A{1},\A{2})$. Then, for all $\psi\in \mathscr{C}^{\infty}_c(\R)$, there exists
    \begin{equation}
        f\in S(1/m,\A{1},\A{2}) \m\m\m\m\m \text{such that}\m\m\m\m\m \psi(p_{N,\alpha_1,\A{2}})=f_{N,\alpha_1,\A{2}}

    \end{equation}
    satisfying
    \begin{equation}
        f\sim \sum_{\nu\in \N}\Tilde{h}^\nu f_\nu \m\m \text{in}\m\m S(1/m,\A{1},\A{2}) \m\m\m\m\m\text{where} \m\m\m\m\m f_\nu\in S(1/m,\A{1},\A{2}).

    \end{equation}
    In particular, we have that $f_0(x,\xi)=\psi\Bigl(p_0(x,\xi)\Bigl)$ and for all $\nu\geq 1$,
    \begin{equation}
        f_\nu(x,\xi)=\sum_{k=1}^{2\nu}g_k(x,\xi,\alpha)\psi^{(k)}\Bigl(p_0(x,\xi)\Bigl) \m\m\m\m\m\text{where} \m\m\m\m\m g_k\in S(1,\A{1},\A{2}).

    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.905519+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fd1220e3e85013d6ab311accde6d336ff987c461acd5cc4e7cce042f8bc22fdc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hoeffding of arXiv:2608.26707, 'An Exponential Lower Bound for the Permanent\\of Random Bernoulli Matrix'] Let $Z_1,\dots,Z_r$ be independent $\{0,1\}$-valued random variables satisfying $\mathbb{E} Z_i\geq 1/2$.
