{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.869857+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fda7745b46161273dc536a2baf11ff8600cdd0aab11eb2161b6b96b4a51c962b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:proper of arXiv:2605.10491, 'Zero-couplings of infinite measures with cyclically monotone support and multivariate regu'] \am{Following Johan's comments, I've put this result in (cor:cdtn-proper-zcoup of the paper).}

    \js{Perhaps merge this with (cor:cdtn_push_forward of the paper).}
    \am{Now that we have added other conditions like in (prop:existpropcoupfinite of the paper) or (prop:existpropcoup1D of the paper) that involve the mass given by $\mu$ to some sets too, I think it may be more clearer to keep (cor:cdtn_push_forward of the paper) as a separate result since it is of another nature (condition on the support of the zero-coupling $\gamma$).} 
    \textcolor{darkteal}{\small\sffamily\upshape [Johan: {Yes, agreed.}]}
    Let $\mu, \nu \in \mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and suppose the following two properties hold:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item $\forall y \in \operatorname{spt} \nu \setminus \left\{0\right\} : \exists x \in \operatorname{spt} \mu : \langle{x,y}\rangle > 0$;
        \item $\exists \alpha > 0 : \forall \lambda > 0 : \mu(\lambda \,\cdot\,) = \lambda^{-\alpha} \mu(\,\cdot\,)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    \js{This is a corollary to (prop:cdtn-proper-zcoup of the paper) I believe: conditions (i) and (ii) imply the condition in that proposition. Indeed, under (i), we can find a small ball centred at $x$ with positive mass, and, making the ball small enough, the ball is contained in $H_+(y,\epsilon)$ for some $\epsilon>0$. Shrinking the ball to zero (multiplying it by $\lambda>0$ with $\lambda\to0$), it remains within the cone $H_+(y,\epsilon)$ while its mass blows up to infinity, and thus $\mu(H_+(y,\epsilon))=\infty$.}
    If $\gamma \in \Gamma_{0}(\mu,\nu)$ has monotone support, then $\gamma$ is proper. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.904160+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fda8370dff445e29a4c029c561f46d7745609da01066b89118b3fdacda2ea505","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2608.29316, 'Powers of the Dirac Operator on $S^1\times S^{n-1}$'] The order $2k+1$ conformally covariant differential operator $A_{2k+1}$ on the spinor bundle over $M$,
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
&A_{2k+1}=\sqrt{-1}\a^0\cdot Z(2k+1)\\
&\quad=\sqrt{-1}\a^0\cdot\prod_{l=0}^{k}(-\sqrt{-1}\a^0\dc-k+2l)\cdot\prod_{m=0}^{k-1}(-\sqrt{-1}\dc\a^0-(k-1)+2m),
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
has the leading term $(-1)^k\cdot\dc^{2k+1}$. And the square of $A_{2k+1}$ acts as
\begin{equation*}
\prod_{l=-k}^k (f+J+l)(-f+J+l)\text{ on }V(f,j,\X,\e).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.245842+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdacf75cadf934607e763eccd1d039deaa79b3f79074e275a31ddfb712308c39","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 (Local well-posedness of the stochastic hypoviscous Navier--Stokes equations in vorticity form) of arXiv:2608.27054, 'GLOBAL WELL-POSEDNESS FOR THE 2D STOCHASTIC HYPOVISCOUS NAVIER-STOKES EQUATIONS'] Let $\gam\in (\frac 12,1]$, $s\in [\gam, 2\gam)$. Furthermore, assume that $p,q\in (2,\infty)$ satisfy
      \begin{equation*}
        \max\{|s-1|, 3\gam-s-1\}<\frac 2q < 4\gam-s-1\quad \text{ and }\quad \frac{2}{p}+\frac{2}{q\gam}\leq 4-\frac{s+1}{\gam}.
    \end{equation*}
    Moreover, set $\kappa:=-1 + \frac{p}{2}\big(4-\gam^{-1}(\frac{2}{q}+s+1)\big)$. Then for all $\xi_0 \in L^0_{\mc{F}_0}(\Om; \dot{B}_{q,p}^{\frac{2}{q}+1-2\gam}(\T^2;\R))$ there exists a unique maximal solution $(\xi,\Theta)$ to \eqref{eq: secondvorticity} satisfying $\Theta>0$ a.s. and
    \begin{align*}
        \xi&\in H^{\theta,p}_{\loc}([0,\Theta),w_{\kappa}; \dot{H}^{-s+2\gam(1-\theta),q}(\T^2; \R)) \,\,\text{a.s. for all }\theta\in [0,1/2),\\
        \xi&\in C([0, \Theta); \dot{B}_{q,p}^{\frac{2}{q}+1-2\gam}(\T^2; \R))\,\, \text{ a.s.}
    \end{align*}
    The solution $(\xi,\Theta)$ instantaneously regularises in time and space:
    \begin{equation*}
    \xi\in L^r_\loc((0,\Theta); \dot{H}^{\gam,\zeta}(\T^2;\R))\cap C^{\theta -\eps}_\loc((0,\Theta); \dot{H}^{-\gam+2\gam(1-\theta),\zeta}(\T^2;\R)) \quad \text{a.s.},
\end{equation*}
for $\theta \in (0,\half)$, $\eps\in (0,\theta)$,  $r,\zeta\in (2,\infty)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.717705+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdb49441b6703f775237cfced8ef6f0661d07b6cb9c94aea71b32792a8cbcc97","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2511.13404, 'Asymptotic stability and mean ergodicity of Feller processes on Polish spaces'] Let $\mathfrak{F}\subset B(\X)$ be a family of measurable function.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item $\Phi$ is \textit{asymptotically stable with respect to $\mathfrak{F}$} if it has a unique invariant measure $\mu\in\mathcal{P}(\X)$ such that $\mathfrak{F} \subset L^1(\mu)$ and for all $x\in\X$ and all $f\in\mathfrak{F}$,
        \begin{equation*}
        \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\left|P_tf(x)-\langle f,\mu \rangle\right|=0.
        \end{equation*}

        \item $\Phi$ is \textit{mean ergodic with respect to $\mathfrak{F}$} if it has a unique invariant measure $\mu\in\mathcal{P}(\X)$ such that $\mathfrak{F} \subset L^1(\mu)$ and for all $x\in\X$ and all $f\in\mathfrak{F}$,
        \begin{equation*}
        \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\left|Q_tf(x)-\langle f,\mu \rangle\right|=0.
        \end{equation*}

         \item $\Phi$ is \textit{uniformly asymptotically stable with respect to $\mathfrak{F}$} if it has a unique invariant measure $\mu\in\mathcal{P}(\X)$ such that $\mathfrak{F} \subset L^1(\mu)$ and for all $x\in\X$,
        \begin{equation*}
            \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\sup_{f\in\mathfrak{F}}\left|P_tf(x)-\langle f,\mu \rangle\right|=0.
        \end{equation*}

         \item $\Phi$ is \textit{uniformly mean ergodic with respect to $\mathfrak{F}$} if it has a unique invariant measure $\mu\in\mathcal{P}(\X)$ such that $\mathfrak{F} \subset L^1(\mu)$ and for all $x\in\X$,
        \begin{equation*}
            \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\sup_{f\in\mathfrak{F}}\left|Q_tf(x)-\langle f,\mu \rangle\right|=0.
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.323534+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdb95781c8cd18b061f6fa1a7245b8f5c79866e4fe71f8138a1e2bfa7678f4e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Cheeger theorem of arXiv:2606.01683, 'Singular-value gap of nonreversible Markov processes'] Consider a Markov jump process on a standard Borel space with generator (equation generator of jump process 2 of the paper), an invariant probability measure $\mu$,
    and jump rates bounded by $q(x,S) \leq q_{\mathrm{max}} < \infty$ for $\mu$-a.e. $x$. Let $\gamma$ be the associated 
    singular-value gap and $\xi$ the Cheeger constant defined in (equation Cheeger constant definition of the paper). Then
