{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.330277+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdd889537ebfbe3e3632936db34ac115db2ced47417775ca69bf9e3fc1b71e04","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] Let $(\xi,\Xi)=(\xi_t,\Xi_t)_{t\geq0}$ be the underlying MAP of $Z$ from (\ref{item_a}) with respect to $\|\cdot\|_1$. The killing rate of $(\xi,\Xi)$ is given by
	\begin{equation}
		q(\boldsymbol{x})=\mathbbm{1}_{(0,\infty)^d}(\boldsymbol{x})\frac{1}{\alpha}\sum_{k=1}^d \frac{\Gamma(1+\alpha)\sin(\pi\alpha(1-\rho_k))}{\pi} \Big(\frac{x_k}{\|\boldsymbol{x}\|_1}\Big)^{-\alpha},\quad \boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_d)\in(0,\infty)^d,

	\end{equation}
where $\rho_k\coloneqq\mathbb{P}(X^{(k)}_1>0)$ is the positivity parameter of $X^{(k)}$ from the description in  (\ref{item_a}). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.831497+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdddb7b00d14434d30b99584753d9a264b8d8563114ed9b86c3d54ed367747f9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 0108390 of arXiv:2606.29143, 'Comonotonic and moment matching approximations for sums of lognormal random variables'] Let $A = (a_1, ..., a_m)^{'}$ and $Q = (q_1, ..., q_m)^{'}$ be two numerical vectors with $\min(a_1, ..., a_m) \geq 0, \max(a_1, ..., a_m) > 0$ and $0 = q_0 < q_1 < q_2 < ... < q_m = 1$. Then, for any continuous random variable $X$ the stop-loss premiums of the step-weighted distribution associated with the numerical vectors $A$ and $Q$ are given by  
\begin{align*}
E[(X_{A, Q} - d)_{+}] &= {a}^{-1}\Big[a_lE[(X - d)_{+}] + d\Big(a_l(1 - q_{l}) - \sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}a_{k + 1}(q_{k + 1} - q_{k})\Big)  \\ 
&\quad + \sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}(a_{k + 1} - a_{k})(1 - q_k)\text{TVaR}_{q_k}[X]\Big], \,\, d \in (F^{-1+}_{X_{A, Q}}(0), F^{-1}_{X_{A, Q}}(1))
\end{align*}
with $F_X(d) \in (q_{l - 1}, q_l]$ for some $l \in \{1, ..., m\}$ and $\sum_{k = m}^{m - 1} = 0$ by convention.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.237907+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fddeec1afd138b1ce8945c9044f60d592df909148b2765da0db08e741ae128f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 113 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] If $\phi:\G\to\rmM_k$ is positive definite, then $\hann(\phi)$ is equal to
	\begin{multline}
	\rmH_\phi(B_0) - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=0}^\infty \sum_{s_{n+1}\cdots s_1 \in S_{n+1}}\big(\rmH_\phi(s_n\cdots s_1\,|\,P(s_n\cdots s_1)) \\ \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad - \rmH_\phi(s_{n+1}\cdots s_1\mid P(s_{n+1}\cdots s_1))\big).
	\end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.662777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fde035618cd3f11128162e5991c7d150df406ac1b6bcea0e770fbf28c07f26f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (\cite{HJPS2}) of arXiv:2604.23656, 'Doubly Reflected Backward SDEs Driven by $G$-Brownian Motion with Quadratic Generator'] If $\eta\in H_G^{\alpha}(0,T)$ with $\alpha\geq 1$ and $p\in(0,\alpha]$, then we have

	\begin{displaymath}
		\underline{\sigma}^p {c_{p,T}}\hat{\mathbb{E}}_t\bigg(\int_t^T |\eta_s|^2ds\bigg)^{p/2}\leq
		\hat{\mathbb{E}}_t\bigg[\sup_{u\in[t,T]}\bigg|\int_t^u\eta_s dB_s\bigg|^p\bigg]\leq
		\bar{\sigma}^p C_{p,T}\hat{\mathbb{E}}_t\bigg(\int_t^T |\eta_s|^2ds\bigg)^{p/2},
	\end{displaymath}
	where $0<c_{p,T}<C_{p,T}<\infty$ are constants depending on $p, T$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.776777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fde05ff50f5235dc241f1ace06aaaa996044888333558984a7e68180c632864b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] For any $\ph\in\cL_0$ and $\psi\in\cL_1$, one has the relations 
