{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.485007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fde34c8a6a89bd309760b118c8ecf2e92385e77f861641ce7db2292178daedfb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] Every nested finite Lie word $\mathcal{C}$ in the $H_{\boldsymbol m}$ is one weighted shift
$T_{\boldsymbol\ell,a}$ with $a\in\mathfrak A$; a finite linear combination
of words is a finite sum of such shifts. For every $r\in\mathbb R$,
\begin{align}
 \mathcal{C}:H^r\to H^{r-1},\qquad
 [\mathcal{C},\Delta]:H^r\to H^{r-2}.
\end{align}
If $s>5$, $h\in H^{s-2}$, and $u\in H^s$ or $H^{s-2}$, then
\begin{align}
 \|[\mathcal{C},H_h]u\|_{H^{s-4}}
 \le C_{\mathcal{C}}\|h\|_{H^{s-2}}
 \begin{cases}
  \|u\|_{H^s},&u\in H^s,\\
  \|u\|_{H^{s-2}},&u\in H^{s-2}.
 \end{cases}
\end{align}
The same mapping statements hold for Fourier adjoints.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.204253+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fde94651adcb350887d79ed98d53f9ad87e4703c5dfae4ba465dc5a6d47ed957","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2608.28964, '2608.28964'] There exists a constant $C>0$, depending only on $\omega$, such that for any $N\in\N^+$ and $f\in H^1(\omega;\R^2)$,
