{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.113916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe362fdb3328570e55b014e44241076806fb7c5a2c6f2bd8a1e4cd1f9ea9c326","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem HKUB of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Given a metric measure space $(M, d, \mu)$ such that there exist some $c>0, \alpha>0$ such that  $c^{-1}r^\alpha \le \mu(B(x,r))\le cr^\alpha$ for all $x\in M$ and $r>0$. Let $X$ be a stochastically complete diffusion on $M$ such that the heat kernel of $X$ is continuous and satisfies the on-diagonal estimate
$$
p(t,x,x)\le C t^{-\alpha/\beta}, \quad \text{for all }x\in M, \, t>0.
$$
Let $B(x, r)$ denote a metric ball of radius $r$ centered at $x \in M$. The following three conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item The off-diagonal sub-Gaussian-type heat kernel estimate
\begin{equation}
p(t,x,y)\le \frac{C}{t^{\alpha/\beta}}\exp\left\{-C\left(\frac{d(x,y)}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta /(\beta -1)}\right\},\quad \text{for all }t>0, \, x\in M;
\end{equation}
\item 
$$
{\mathbb{E}}^x\left[\tau_{B(x,r)}\right]\simeq r^\alpha, \quad \text{for all }x\in M,\, r>0;
$$
\item The tail estimate of the exit time:
\begin{equation}
{\mathbb{P}}^x\left[\tau_{B(x,r)}\le t\right] \le C\exp\left\{-C\left(\frac{r}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta /(\beta -1)}\right\},\quad \text{for all }t>0, \,r>0
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:40:59.372506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe38f1a62e54bccfffbe3ee0f064961a5bd8f46cd61c88b3b082e26c0cef2cfa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2609.03961, 'On the exponential sum over squarefree integers'] Let $N\ge2$, let $\alpha\in\R$, and let $a\in\Z$ and $q\in\N$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
  (a,q)=1,\qquad \bigg|\alpha-\frac aq\bigg|\le\frac1{q^2}.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
  |S(\alpha)|\ll\left(\frac Nq+q\right)(\log 2N)^{5},
\end{equation}
where the implied constant is absolute. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.368697+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe3900236455e5ab2d4a58fe288b047eb8e17ea35b0793796663ce95f2dc64f6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 32 of arXiv:2512.07202, 'Hitting Probabilities for Hypoelliptic Differential Equations Driven by Fractional Brownia'] For any \( \varphi \in C_c^\infty(\{ u \in \mathfrak{g}^{(l)} : \|u\|_{\mathrm{HS}} < r \}) \), one has
\begin{equation*}
\int_{\mathfrak{g}^{(l)}} \varphi(u)\, du = \int_{\mathbb{R}^N} dy \int_{M_{x,y}} K(v,x)\varphi(v)\, m_{x,y}(dv),
\end{equation*}
where the kernel \( K \) is given by
\begin{equation}
K(v,x) = \left( \det(JF_l(v,x) \cdot JF_l(v,x)^*) \right)^{-1/2},
\end{equation}
$JF_l(v,x):\mathfrak{g}^{(l)}\to \mathbb{R}^N$ is the Jacobian of $F_l(\cdot,x)$ at $v\in\mathfrak{g}^{(l)}$ and we define \( m_{x,y} = 0 \) if \( M_{x,y} = \emptyset \). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.973175+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe39d099186a7cbf11ca2081a005339bf5bbead7553118a3c81a3d0a399d9829","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stoZeta of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] Fix $\omega$ supported on $[-1,2]$ and equal to one on $[0,1]$. There exists a smooth function of polynomial growth $P:\R_{\geqslant0}^2\rightarrow\R_{\geqslant0}$ such that for every $\eta>0$ we have
