\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.135436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe4e0e92b6edcf48342e83fe9729d4cba332499581f84be4fa1398d672b60f2c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:random_length_worms_model of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Let us consider a probability mass function $\eta: \mathbb{N}_{>0} \to [0,1] $ and an $\mathbb{N}_{>0}$-valued random variable $\mathcal{L}$ satisfying $\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{L}=\ell)=\eta(\ell), \, \ell \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$. 
	We call the distribution of $\mathcal{L}$ the length distribution of the worms. Let us also consider a nearest neighbour simple random walk $(X(t))_{t \in \mathbb{N}}$ on the vertices of $G$ starting from $X(0)=o$, independent of $\mathcal{L}$.
	Let us define the probability measure $\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}$ on $\mathcal{H}_{o}$ by 
	\begin{equation}

		\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}(H) := P_{o}( \{ X(0), X(1), \ldots, X(\mathcal{L} - 1) \} = H )
	\end{equation}
	for each $H \in \mathcal{H}_{o}$.
	Given any $\lambda >0$, the Poisson zoo $\mathcal{N}_{\eta}^{\lambda} := \mathcal{N}_{\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}}^{\lambda}$ built upon this measure via Definition (def:poisson_zoo of the paper) is called the random length worms model at level $\lambda$, and the corresponding random set $\mathcal{S}_{\eta}^\lambda := \mathcal{S}_{\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}}^\lambda$ is the random length worms set at level $\lambda$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.603040+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe532a6424188966d9d077aecb45e6baef61d5a8506656781ccb94b1eec9b8fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 45 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] Assume the regenerative hypotheses of Section~5 and let $k_t\in\N^d$ satisfy $|k_t-t\lambda|_1=o(t)$ for some $\lambda\in\Delta_d$. Then

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mathcal R_c(k_t)}{t}\xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{} a_c\kappa(\lambda)=\overline c\rho_\lambda
\end{equation*}

in probability, and almost surely under the regenerative construction. If, in addition, $|k_t-t\lambda|_1=o(\sqrt t)$ and the second-moment assumptions of the central limit theorem in Section 5 hold, let $W=(W_Y,W_L,W_R)$ be the Brownian motion associated with the cycle vector $(Y_1,L_1,R_1(c))$ and put $I=\mathcal I(\lambda)$. Then

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mathcal R_c(k_t)-a_c\kappa(\lambda)t}{\sqrt t}
\xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{d}
W_R(\kappa(\lambda))+
a_c\min_{r\in I}\left\{-\frac{W_{Y,r}(\kappa(\lambda))}{m_r}\right\}.
\end{equation*}

Moreover,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mathcal R_c(k_t)-\overline c N(k_t)}{\sqrt t}
\xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{d}
\Normal\left(0,\kappa(\lambda)\Vv_{\mathfrak a}\left[R_1(c)-\overline cL_1\right]\right).
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.801052+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe5420b5687a8a13e31721b0619da51b1b6dae2256b7a48544e3d2db5917851b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:spectral-entropy of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] The \emph{spectral entropy} of the relaxation trajectory at step $k$ is
