{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.533346+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe5e7c90728e83f4c439649fca4f3232e05705c33618b744c07d141ee5b171a6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2603.28093, 'Random Stability of Random Variables'] Take a random variable $N$ with values $0, 1, 2, \ldots$ with distribution $P$. A probability measure $Q$ on $\mathbb R$, or, equivalently, a random variable $X \sim Q$, is called {\it $N$-stable} or, equivalently, {\it $P$-stable}, if there exists a $c > 0$ such that the following equality in law holds: 
\begin{equation}

X_1 + \ldots + X_N \stackrel{d}{=} cX,
\end{equation}
where $X_1, X_2, \ldots \sim Q$ are independent copies of $X$, independent of $N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.977703+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe61466b14639c4826e0c8cd4b13df0a27b50a1548840a9d5bcb0d5d016901c6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 53 (Universal weak-trace area bound) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] If \(V_T\leq K\) almost surely, then for \(q>1\) and \(z\geq0\),
