{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.830339+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fea59bdf9c5d4ddae5e225504080d50e176622f4962d109ff849c778f9ceef0b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop eq:OP_BSig of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] For $\mu > - \frac12$, ${\boldsymbol{\kappa}} \in {\mathbb R}^{d_2}$ with $k_j > -1$, $1 \le j \le d_2$, define 
\begin{equation} 
 \bm Q_{\bm j, \bm k, m}^n(\bm x,\bm y) = \hat {\bm L}_{\bm j, n-m}^{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}}, \alpha+2m)}(\bm y) |\bm y|^m 
      \bm B_{\bm k, m}^\mu \left(\frac{\bm x}{|\bm y|}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\alpha = 2 \mu + d_1-1$. Then
$\{\bm Q_{\bm j, \bm k, m}^n: |\bm k| = m, \, |\bm j| = n-m, \, \bm k \in {\mathbb N}_0^{d_1}, \, \bm j \in {\mathbb N}_0^{d_2}, 0\le m \le n\}$ 
is an orthogonal basis of ${\mathcal V}_n\big({\mathbb B}^{d_1}\!\! \rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, \bm W_{\mu, {\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb B}\rtimes \Sigma}\big)$. 
Moreover, the norm square of $\bm Q_{\bm j, \bm k, m}^n$ is given by 
\begin{equation} 
   \bm h_{\bm j, \bm k, m}^{n; \mu,{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}  

       =  (2\mu+d_1)_{2m} \bm h_{\bm k,m}^{\mu, {\mathbb B}}  
       \bm h_{\bm j,m}^{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}}, {\alpha}+2m), \triangle}. 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.794325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feab3b5054c071e8039b51ab5b476835960161429d020e344fa290675244b575","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:convergegeneral of arXiv:2411.08295, 'Improving the convergence of Markov chains via permutations and projections'] Let $\pi$ be the discrete uniform distribution, $P \in \mathcal{S}(\pi)$ and $Q \in \mathcal{Q}$ be a permutation matrix. Let $\Pi$ be the matrix where each row equals to $\pi$. We have
	\begin{itemize}
		\item(One-step contraction measured by $D^{\pi}_{KL}$) \begin{align*}
			D^{\pi}_{KL}(P \| \Pi) &= D^{\pi}_{KL}(P Q \| \Pi) = D^{\pi}_{KL}(Q P \| \Pi) = D^{\pi}_{KL}(Q P Q\| \Pi).
		\end{align*}

		\item(KL-divergence Dobrushin coefficient)
		\begin{align*}
			c_{KL}(P) = c_{KL}(PQ) = c_{KL}(QP) = c_{KL}(QPQ).
		\end{align*}
	\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.070351+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feafaa67a100c4f1fa37eb4fd939cd9ae11ae787cd77734b3cde25ace56901c7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Assumption \Unif\, of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] We say that $\mathcal{S}$ satisfies the assumption ${\mbox{\textsc{Unif(}$\delta$\textsc{)}}}\,={\mbox{\textsc{Unif(}$\delta,r_0,\theta_0$\textsc{)}}}\ $ for some parameters $\delta,r_0,\theta_0$ if all edge-lengths in $\mathcal{S}$ are comparable to $\delta$, meaning that for any  neighbouring $v^{\bullet}\in G^\bullet$ and $v^{\circ}\in G^\circ$ one has
	\begin{equation}
		r_0^{-1}\cdot \delta \leq  |\mathcal{S}(v^{\bullet})- \mathcal{S}(v^{\circ})| \leq r_0\cdot \delta,
	\end{equation}
and all the geometric angles in the quads $\mathcal{S}$ are bounded from below by $\theta_0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.006903+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feaff33968a0478b8dfd662d3cbcd4f063834a7a0592dcb731c5e341a589a5f7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma seq of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] If $z=z_1-\epsilon$ for some suitable $\epsilon$ (say less or equal to $\frac{\mu_{n,N}}{2\kappa_{N}}$), then there exists a sequence $s_N$ with finite limit such that
    \begin{equation}
    \zeta^{\frac{3}{2}}(z)=\frac{\sqrt{\epsilon^3(z_{2}-z_{1})}}{2z_{1}}\left(1+\frac{3}{5}s_{N}\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2)\right)
\end{equation}
and hence,
\begin{equation}
   \kappa_{N}^{\frac{2}{3}}\zeta(z)=\frac{\epsilon\kappa_{N}}{\sigma_{n,N}}\left(1+\frac{2\epsilon s_{N}}{5}+O(\epsilon^2)\right),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \left(\frac{\kappa_{N}}{\sigma^3_{n,N}}\right)^{\frac{1}{6}}\tilde{f}^{-\frac{1}{4}}(z)=1-\frac{2}{5} s_{N}\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2).
\end{equation}
Here $z_1$, $z_2$, $\kappa$ and $s$ are all double-indexed sequences with respect to $(j,k)\in\mathbb{N}^2$. We will abbreviate them as in this lemma if $(j,k)=(n,N)$. Moreover, the L--G transform $\zeta$ and induced $\tilde{f}$ is always assumed to be around $z_1$ (with parameter $(n,N)$ here).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.590691+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feb0c3b141cde519c5d287ad4bc56ab0441e88067b202150db2f4f776b0c7b4f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma N+1s-NE-PIT of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] For any $t \in \mathop  \cap \limits_{j = 0}^q \left[ {0,{\mathfrak{t}_j}} \right] \cap \left[ {{t_k},{t_{k + 1}}} \right)$, the combination
\begin{equation*}
\left( {{u_{q,n + 1}},{v_{q,n + 1}},{\mathfrak{p}_{q,n + 1}},{\mathring{R}_{q,n + 1}},{S_{q,n + 1}},{\mathfrak{P}_{q + 1,n + 1}}} \right)
\end{equation*}
fulfills the $\left( {n + 1} \right)$-step Newton--Euler--Reynolds system
\begin{equation*}
\left\{ {\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}
{\partial _t}{v_{q,n + 1}} + \mathrm{div} \left( {{u_{q,n + 1}} \otimes {u_{q,n + 1}}} \right) + \nabla {\mathfrak{p}_{q,n + 1}} = \mathrm{div} \left( {{\mathring{R}_{q,n + 1}} + {S_{q,n + 1}} + {\mathfrak{P}_{q + 1,n + 1}}} \right),\\
{u_{q,n + 1}} = {v_{q,n + 1}} + \mathfrak{W}_q,\\
\mathrm{div}\,   {u_{q,n + 1}} = 0 .
\end{array}} \right.  
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.580514+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feb12decfe33041a647367b9ff3246d4ce393b68d102dde417a4018ba3e381fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm-OR01 of arXiv:2411.15401, 'The reference interval in higher-order stochastic dominance'] For $Z\in\mathcal X$ and $n\in\mathbb{N}$, we have
\begin{align*}
\lim_{\eta\to\infty}\left\{\eta-\left(\mathbb{E}[(\eta-Z)_+^n]\right)^{1/n}\right\}=\mathbb{E}[Z].
\end{align*}
As a result, for $X,Y\in\mathcal X$ and $n\in\mathbb{N}$,
$X\ge_n Y$ implies $\mathbb{E}[X]\ge \mathbb{E}[Y]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.066807+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feb575fb862d89c856766fad6248152405a3e96fc4ee5747f63fee9e6aedd976","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem defbsc of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $\Omega$ be a region, and let $\Gamma_1$, $\Gamma_2$, and $\Gamma_3$ be Jordan curves in $\Omega$ that are nested from innermost $(\Gamma_1)$ to outermost $(\Gamma_3)$, and can be continuously deformed into one another within $\Omega$. Let $F({\mathbf u}, {\mathbf v})$ be a meromorphic function with
\begin{itemize}
    \item simple poles at $u_i = v_j$ for all $i$ and $j$,
    \item simple zeros at $u_i = u_j$ and at $v_i = v_j$ for all $i \neq j$,
    \item symmetry in $u_1, \ldots, u_m$, and separately in $v_1, \ldots, v_n$.
\end{itemize}
Then, 
\begin{equation}  \begin{split}
    &\int_{\Gamma_1^m} \mathrm{d} {\mathbf u} \int_{\Gamma_2^n} \mathrm{d} {\mathbf v}  \, \, F({\mathbf u}, {\mathbf v})  
    =  \sum_{i=0}^{m\wedge n}  (-2\pi \mathrm{i})^i i! \binom{m}{i} \binom{n}{i}   \int_{\Gamma_3^{m-i}} \mathrm{d} u_{i+1}\cdots \mathrm{d} u_m \int_{\Gamma_2^n} \mathrm{d} {\mathbf v}  \, \, \res_{u_1=v_1, \cdots, u_i=v_i} F({\mathbf u}, {\mathbf v}).
\end{split} \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.049045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feb7522950c4b317d2a58049aff29272e2857f5d4b576032e1b0d59edcf7637b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem dimhatleastconjecture of arXiv:2506.10448, 'Hitting probabilities and the Ekstr\"om--Persson conjecture'] For every $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ {and} for every $\alpha>0$, one has 
