  \frac{1}{\alpha}, \overline{H}_{\mu}\Bigl(\frac 1\alpha\Bigr)\Bigr\},
\end{equation}
and the above bounds are optimal. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.459375+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feb84c39ce19799a0c3f4e303967aa535af8a5284d0d491d32b2084de2cabdfb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Sampling Theorem) of arXiv:2609.03418, 'Sampling and Density Theorems for the Fractional Opdam--Cherednik Transform'] Let $ f\in PW^\theta_{\alpha,\beta}.$
Then
\begin{align}
f(\lambda) = f(0)S_0^\theta(\lambda) + \sum_{j\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}} f(s_j)S_j^\theta(\lambda),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
S_0^\theta(\lambda) = e^{-\frac i2\lambda^2\cot\theta} \frac{ \Psi_{\alpha,\beta}(\lambda\csc\theta) }{ \Psi_{\alpha,\beta}(0) }, 
\end{align}
and, for $j\ne0$,
\begin{align}
S_j^\theta(\lambda) = e^{-\frac i2(\lambda^2-s_j^2)\cot\theta} \frac{ \lambda\sin\theta }{ s_j(\lambda-s_j) } \frac{ \Psi_{\alpha,\beta}(\lambda\csc\theta) }{ \Psi_{\alpha,\beta}'(\mu_j) }, \quad \mu_j=s_j\csc\theta. 
\end{align}
The value at $\lambda=s_j$ in \eqref{Sec4_Eqn18} is understood by continuity. The interpolation functions satisfy
\begin{align}
S_j^\theta(s_k)=\delta_{jk}, \quad j,k\in\mathbb Z.
\end{align}
The series in \eqref{Sec4_Eqn16} converges in the $PW^\theta_{\alpha,\beta}$-norm and uniformly on compact subsets of $\mathbb R$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.749687+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_febd407e25f3e0eb63661803ac9d52109dc6a0357af1322e88c5a12a12c31879","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:sol_gen_MKV_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] A (general) solution to D-MKV$(b,\sigma)$ is a triple $( W, (\mathcal{F}_t)_t, X)$ which is a solution to MKV$({\bf b}^*,{\boldsymbol\sigma}^*)$, with ${\bf b}^*,{\boldsymbol\sigma}^*$ given by (equation eq:def_b_sig_dens of the paper)-(equation eq:density_standard of the paper), and such that
\begin{equation}
[X_t] \in AC(\mathbb{R}^d), \qquad t> 0. \vspace{-10pt}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:23:37.905216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_febd4c3135f93726be7e4b2bd285e3a8c7e62b2a07745a51c8e957477dc492f9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 10 of arXiv:2609.03653, 'The derived set of multiple star polylogarithms'] Let $m=n+1$.  Then
