{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.713095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fef6a07f72b8268a34904b1fe729c414c1ee8b15bc8aaa4d483e49cbc1066a42","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 38 (Summed deterministic diagonal) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Let $a,b\in\mathfrak C$ with $a\ne b$, $0<s<1-2\beta_b$ and $0<\sigma<1$.  The dyadic series
\begin{equation}
  \cD^{a;b}w
  :=\sum_N\sum_{Q\le c_{\mathrm{ap}}N}
  \cD^{a;b}_{N,Q}P_Qw
\end{equation}
converges as a bounded deterministic operator
\begin{equation}
  \cD^{a;b}:E_T^{2,\sigma}
  \longrightarrow
  C_TH^{s-1}\cap L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
  \norm{\cD^{a;b}}_{E_T^{2,\sigma}\to L_T^1B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}}
  \le T
  \norm{\cD^{a;b}}_{E_T^{2,\sigma}\to L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}},
\end{equation}
so the Besov source norm tends to zero as $T\downarrow0$ on bounded diagonal classes.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:54.983887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fef7bf85205a2d93c171f53c4ef659dcec5fd3012e9e315cd0381a7079cdd41a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition see, e.g., \cite{bozic_models_1984} of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] For an f-model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, we define an \emph{extrinsic satisfaction relation} $\Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M}$ on $W \times \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ by:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \to \psi_2 \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, (x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \mathrel{\Rightarrow} x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \psi_2)$
    \item $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \varphi \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall y \sqsupset x \, (y \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \exists y \sqsupset x\, (y \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item The other cases are identical to the classical $\Vdash_\mathfrak{M}$.
  \end{enumerate}
  We write $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^e \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(e-)valid in} $\mathfrak{M}$'') to mean $\forall x \in W\,(x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$,
  and $\mathfrak{F} \vDash^e \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(e-)valid on} $\mathfrak{F}$'') to mean that $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^e \varphi$ for every $\mathfrak{M}$ based on $\mathfrak{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.666560+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fef7cf4eec100f1cbc78cb379bb7393424e3b058d622e2bb14aad2c017beff1e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Geometric-burst tradeoff) of arXiv:2607.20823, 'Fluctuation impossibility results for stochastic burst networks'] Suppose Assumption~\ref{ass:S-standing} holds and every $K_i$ has the positive
