{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.139847+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff2c13b8a619b51ee1a7b3a5d4d2fc417efc178070af0a66ddc93be04e00d34f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem T:path main of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] Consider the unit interval $\mathfrak{X} = [0,1]$ with its Euclidean metric $d$ and let $\mu$ be an absolutely continuous probability measure on $\mathfrak{X}$ with density $f$ satisfying
\begin{equation}

c \leq f(x) \leq C\mbox{\rm \ for every $x \in \mathfrak{X}$},
\end{equation}
where $0 < c \leq C < \infty$. 
Given $\alpha \in (0, \infty)$ and $n\in\mathbb{N}$, let
\begin{equation}

r(n)\equiv r(n,c, \alpha) := 4 c^{-1} \sqrt{(2 + \alpha) \frac{\ln n}{n}}.
\end{equation}
Then there exists $n_0\equiv n_0(c,C,\alpha)$ such that $n\geq n_0$ implies that $(\mathfrak{X},d,\mu)$ is $(n^{-\alpha},r(n))$-disintegrable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.985390+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff2c181940e5bce3a1a63fe18c5dc9911626f17fa9351f41fc68e1639b38b1c3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:multit of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Choose $m\in \mathbb N$, $\vec \mathbf k \in \mathbb R^m_+ $, and $\vec \boldsymbol{\tau}\in \mathbb R_{\ge 0}^m$ such that $\boldsymbol{\tau}_1\le \cdots \le \boldsymbol{\tau}_m$, any real $C_1>0$, and a large enough $N\in \mathbb N$.
We take the $N$ dimensional DBM $\{Y_i(\tau)\}_{1\leq i\leq N, \tau>0}$ started from zero initial condition, and $\vec k \in \mathbb N^m$, $\tau_1\le \tau_2\le \dots \le \tau_m$ such that
\begin{equation}

|k_\ell-\mathbf k_\ell N^{2/3}|<C_1, \qquad \left|\frac{2}{\beta}N + \frac{2}{\beta}\boldsymbol{\tau}_\ell N^{2/3}-\tau_\ell\right|<C_1,\qquad  \text{for each }\ell\in\llbracket 1,m\rrbracket.
\end{equation}
Then as $N\to\infty$, uniformly over the ($N$--dependent) choices of $\vec k$ and $\vec \tau$ satisfying (equation eq_x2 of the paper):
\[
 \mathbb E \left[ \prod_{\ell=1}^m \left(\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^N \left(\frac{Y_i(\tau_\ell)}{\sqrt{2\beta\tau_\ell N}}\right)^{k_\ell}+\left(\frac{Y_i(\tau_\ell)}{\sqrt{2\beta\tau_\ell N}}\right)^{k_\ell+1}\right) \right]\to \mathbf L_\beta(\vec \mathbf k, \vec\boldsymbol{\tau}).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.327448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff32725018785d8e8dadee49b5ec518f93436ca9bdc33cfe4609da6442ddb2a2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Sigma-bullet-homotopical-left-Quillen of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $G$ be compact Lie. The adjunctions
    \begin{align*}
        \Sigma^\bullet_+\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}&\rightleftarrows\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}
	\nobreak
	\mspace{6mu plus 1mu}
	{:}
	\nonscript\mkern-\thinmuskip
	\mathpunct{}
	\mspace{2mu}
\Omega^\bullet\\
        \Sigma^\bullet\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_*&\rightleftarrows\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}
	\nobreak
	\mspace{6mu plus 1mu}
	{:}
	\nonscript\mkern-\thinmuskip
	\mathpunct{}
	\mspace{2mu}
\Omega^\bullet
    \end{align*}
    are Quillen adjunctions, and $\Sigma^\bullet_+$ is fully homotopical.
    \begin{proof}
        It is clear from the definition that $\Omega^\bullet$ preserves fibrant objects, i.e.\ it sends $G$-global $\Omega$-spectra to static $G$-orthogonal spaces. To complete the proof that these are Quillen adjunctions, it will then suffice by [HTT]*{Corollary A.3.7.2} that $\Sigma^\bullet$ preserves cofibrations or, equivalently, that $\Omega^\bullet$ preserves acyclic (level) fibrations as a functor to $\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_*$. Plugging in the definitions, this amounts to saying that
        \[
            S^V\smashp{-}\colon\cat{$\bm{({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)}$-Top}_*\rightleftarrows\cat{$\bm{({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)}$-Top}_*
	\nobreak
	\mspace{6mu plus 1mu}
	{:}
	\nonscript\mkern-\thinmuskip
	\mathpunct{}
	\mspace{2mu}
\Omega^V
        \] 
        is a Quillen adjunction for the $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V),G}$-model structure. This is an instance of Lemma~(lemma:smash-lQ of the paper), using  that $S^V$ admits the structure of an $({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)$-CW-complex.

        It remains to show that $\Sigma^\bullet_+$ is homotopical, for which it will suffice by factoring a general weak equivalence into an acyclic cofibration followed by an acyclic fibration that $\Sigma^\bullet_+$ preserves $G$-global level weak equivalences. Plugging in the definitions this amounts to saying that
        \[
            S^V\smashp (-)_+\colon\cat{$\bm{({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)}$-Top}\to\cat{$\bm{({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)}$-Top}_*
        \]
        preserves $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V),G}$-weak equivalences, which is an instance of Proposition~(prop:smash-well-based of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.336980+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff334cf80f717a2fa8a27528f7b3c013ec0c58da31f9226d96e4794dd2252f3f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2607.14718, 'Anchored Nash inequalities and heat kernel bounds for a class of random conductance models'] For any $a, b > 0$ and any $\alpha > 1$,

